/architecture/ahmednagar-fort-an-oval-shaped-bhuik_8qan7Hp.png','Ahmednagar Fort, an oval-shaped Bhuikot (ground) fort, features thick stone walls and 24 bastions.[2]','Ahmednagar Fort, an oval-shaped Bhuikot (ground) fort, features thick stone walls and 24 bastions.[2]','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ahmednagar_Fort_Main_Gate.jpg/1280px-Ahmednagar_Fort_Main_Gate.jpg'),(9849,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/bagh-rauza-is-an-indo-islamic-tomb-c_kfftiBQ.png','Bagh Rauza is an Indo-Islamic tomb complex with a black basalt stone mausoleum featuring a white-plastered dome, enclosed within high boundary walls.[3]','Bagh Rauza is an Indo-Islamic tomb complex with a black basalt stone mausoleum featuring a white-plastered dome, enclosed within high boundary walls.[3]','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Ahmed_Nizam_Shah.jpg#:~:text=The%20Bagh%20Rauza%2Ca%20fine,and%20\'faujdar\'%20of%20Ahmednagar'),(9853,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/farah-baksh-mahal-is-a-two-storey-in_dCOkXEy.png','Farah Baksh Mahal is a two-storey Indo-Islamic water palace built from basalt stone, with thick lime plaster blending stone, pottery, and natural fibers.[4]','Farah Baksh Mahal is a two-storey Indo-Islamic water palace built from basalt stone, with thick lime plaster blending stone, pottery, and natural fibers.[4]','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Faria_Bagh_Palace_of_second_Nizam.JPG/1280px-Faria_Bagh_Palace_of_second_Nizam.JPG'),(9855,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/the-interior-of-farah-baksh-mahal-fe_Vj7cRrs.jpg','The interior of Farah Baksh Mahal features recessed niches, carved teakwood lintels, and traces of stucco work. (Source: CKA Archives)','The interior of Farah Baksh Mahal features recessed niches, carved teakwood lintels, and traces of stucco work. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9858,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/salabat-khans-tomb-is-an-octagonal-t_XllujWD.png','Salabat Khan’s Tomb is an octagonal, three-storeyed stone structure in the Indo-Islamic style.[5]','Salabat Khan’s Tomb is an octagonal, three-storeyed stone structure in the Indo-Islamic style.[5]','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salabat_Khan%27s_tomb_2.jpg'),(9862,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/the-vishal-ganpati-mandir-built-in-t_06pUyxp.jpg','The Vishal Ganpati Mandir, built in the traditional Maratha architectural style, features ornate arched windows, finely carved stone panels, and a domed shikhara. Reconstructed using Makrana marble, it has a striking white exterior. (Source: CKA Archives)','The Vishal Ganpati Mandir, built in the traditional Maratha architectural style, features ornate arched windows, finely carved stone panels, and a domed shikhara. Reconstructed using Makrana marble, it has a striking white exterior. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9866,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/the-hume-memorial-church-inspired-by_oytQdnZ.jpg','The Hume Memorial Church, inspired by Gothic Revival architecture, is built from locally sourced stone. It features a pitched roof with a wooden truss system and beautiful stained-glass windows. (Source: CKA Archives)','The Hume Memorial Church, inspired by Gothic Revival architecture, is built from locally sourced stone. It features a pitched roof with a wooden truss system and beautiful stained-glass windows. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9870,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/shri-datta-mandir-built-from-gulabi-_T9GqhA0.jpg','Shri Datta Mandir, built from Gulabi Bansi Paharpur Dagad, follows the modern Navaratna mandir style. (Source: CKA Archives)','Shri Datta Mandir, built from Gulabi Bansi Paharpur Dagad, follows the modern Navaratna mandir style. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9872,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/a-sculptural-panel-depicting-bhagwan_Hkvmgi9.jpg','A sculptural panel depicting Bhagwan Hanuman with the Vanar Sena constructing the stone bridge to Lanka, capturing a key moment from the Ramayan in intricate stone carving. (Source: CKA Archives)','A sculptural panel depicting Bhagwan Hanuman with the Vanar Sena constructing the stone bridge to Lanka, capturing a key moment from the Ramayan in intricate stone carving. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9877,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/a-residential-structure-situated-at-_grnAjY7.jpg','A residential structure situated at the Shimpi Galli which is said to have been built in the 1880s. (Source: CKA Archives)','A residential structure situated at the Shimpi Galli which is said to have been built in the 1880s. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9878,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/the-old-residential-structures-and-t_110XgPc.jpg','The old residential structures and the intimate character of the lanes of Shimpi Galli. (Source: CKA Archives)','The old residential structures and the intimate character of the lanes of Shimpi Galli. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9880,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/the-composite-structure-of-the-house_nhsfKrM.jpg','The composite structure of the house shows distinct building materials on each floor—stone on the ground level and brick on the upper storey. (Source: CKA Archives)','The composite structure of the house shows distinct building materials on each floor—stone on the ground level and brick on the upper storey. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9881,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/similar-architectural-patterns-appea_K6WdZxS.jpg','Similar architectural patterns appear in buildings throughout the area, where brick parapets contrast with stone foundations, each showcasing the unique masonry of a bygone era. (Source: CKA Archives)','Similar architectural patterns appear in buildings throughout the area, where brick parapets contrast with stone foundations, each showcasing the unique masonry of a bygone era. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9883,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/main-entrance-with-yellow-painted-wo_Icjbe5q.jpg','Main entrance with yellow-painted wooden panels and decorative elements. Above the door, a carved niche, distinct in color, is traditionally used to place an image or murti of a devi or devta with the belief that they will protect the home. A half-height railing is attached to the door as a safety barrier. (Source: CKA Archives)','Main entrance with yellow-painted wooden panels and decorative elements. Above the door, a carved niche, distinct in color, is traditionally used to place an image or murti of a devi or devta with the belief that they will protect the home. A half-height railing is attached to the door as a safety barrier. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9884,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/a-blue-colored-door-which-matches-th_ommnLnS.jpg','A blue-colored door which matches the shade of the beautiful decorative elements that adorn the second storey. (Source: CKA Archives)','A blue-colored door which matches the shade of the beautiful decorative elements that adorn the second storey. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9886,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/traditional-gajachya-khidkya-windows_JPY7ev9.jpg','Traditional “Gajachya khidkya” windows set within niches with rounded tops and contrasting colors. (Source: CKA Archives)','Traditional “Gajachya khidkya” windows set within niches with rounded tops and contrasting colors. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9887,'culture/images/maharashtra/ahilyanagar/architecture/the-windows-have-horizontal-and-very_ePI8ZvY.jpg','The windows have horizontal and very symmetrical iron grills that are overshadowed by the metal mesh, which was likely added later. (Source: CKA Archives)','The windows have horizontal and very symmetrical iron grills that are overshadowed by the metal mesh, which was likely added later. (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(9889,'culture/imag