tness drill in schools across Maharashtra.[1]','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lezim'),(11235,'culture/images/maharashtra/amravati/sports-games/the-amravati-district-mallakhamb-organi_8DqTL53.png','The Amravati District Mallakhamb Organisation actively promotes Kushti by organizing competitions at various levels for both boys and girls. These events help in identifying and nurturing talent for state and national levels.[2]','The Amravati District Mallakhamb Organisation actively promotes Kushti by organizing competitions at various levels for both boys and girls. These events help in identifying and nurturing talent for state and national levels.[2]','https://hvpm.org/iks.php'),(11250,'culture/images/maharashtra/amravati/sports-games/the-territorial-army-parade-ground-in-a_poXOu4J.png','The Territorial Army Parade Ground in Amravati, formerly known as the Reforms Club Ground, is a multi-purpose stadium primarily used for cricket and football matches. Established in 1958, it has a rich history, having hosted its first recorded cricket match that year. Owned and managed by the Territorial Army, this ground serves as a key venue for various sporting events and activities.[3]','The Territorial Army Parade Ground in Amravati, formerly known as the Reforms Club Ground, is a multi-purpose stadium primarily used for cricket and football matches. Established in 1958, it has a rich history, having hosted its first recorded cricket match that year. Owned and managed by the Territorial Army, this ground serves as a key venue for various sporting events and activities.[3]','https://www.espncricinfo.com/cricket-grounds/territorial-army-parade-ground-amravati-57869'),(11262,'culture/images/maharashtra/amravati/stories/sri-krishna-rukmini-wedding1-fc670523.png','Sri Krishna-Rukmini Wedding[1]','Sri Krishna-Rukmini Wedding[1]','https://www.starsai.com/krishna-rukmini-divine-romance-wedding/'),(11266,'culture/images/maharashtra/amravati/stories/shri-chhatrapati-shivajiraje-statue-at-malte_M4jmZeY.png','Shri Chhatrapati Shivajiraje Statue at Maltekdi (Source: CKA Archives)','Shri Chhatrapati Shivajiraje Statue at Maltekdi (Source: CKA Archives)',''),(11276,'culture/images/maharashtra/amravati/stories/mahadev-gufa2-62d02d05.jpg','Mahadev Gufa[2]','Mahadev Gufa[2]','https://www.google.co.in/url?sa=i&url=https%3A%2F%2Flightuptemples.com%2Fsalbardi-cave-temple-madhya-pradesh%2F&psig=AOvVaw1WOYoKyT9nfNeI24UdBzna&ust=1724308358861000&source=images&cd=vfe&opi=89978449&ved=0CBQQjRxqFwoTCMjgo6y7hYgDFQAAAAAdAAAAABAE'),(11304,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/architecture/the-nandi-mandap-at-shivleni-caves-features_4NVXzda.png','The Nandi Mandap at Shivleni Caves features an elegantly carved pavilion measuring 9.14 × 9.14 meters, with a sculpture of Nandi at its center. Inside, a hall is supported by thirty-two pillars and decorated with sculptures of Bhagwan Shiv and Ganesh.[1]','The Nandi Mandap at Shivleni Caves features an elegantly carved pavilion measuring 9.14 × 9.14 meters, with a sculpture of Nandi at its center. Inside, a hall is supported by thirty-two pillars and decorated with sculptures of Bhagwan Shiv and Ganesh.[1]',''),(11306,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/architecture/one-of-the-remaining-elephant-sculptures-at_aiICN0h.png','One of the remaining elephant sculptures at the entrance of Shivleni Caves, Ambajogai, Beed district. These life-sized carvings give the site its local name, Hattikhana.[2]','One of the remaining elephant sculptures at the entrance of Shivleni Caves, Ambajogai, Beed district. These life-sized carvings give the site its local name, Hattikhana.[2]',''),(11310,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/architecture/exterior-view-of-kankaleshwar-mandir-beed-s_4ZtyWcB.png','Exterior view of Kankaleshwar Mandir, Beed, showing its Hemadpanthi stone architecture. Originally built at the center of a water body, the Mandir remains one of the region’s oldest surviving religious sites.[3]','Exterior view of Kankaleshwar Mandir, Beed, showing its Hemadpanthi stone architecture. Originally built at the center of a water body, the Mandir remains one of the region’s oldest surviving religious sites.[3]',''),(11314,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/architecture/the-sakleshwar-mandir-also-known-as-bara-kh_LS8feir.png','The Sakleshwar Mandir, also known as Bara Khamba Mandir, is distinguished by its twelve intricately carved stone pillars and is believed to date back to the 13th century Yadava period.[4]','The Sakleshwar Mandir, also known as Bara Khamba Mandir, is distinguished by its twelve intricately carved stone pillars and is believed to date back to the 13th century Yadava period.[4]',''),(11318,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/architecture/interior-of-jama-masjid-beed-showing-stone-_6lQ9ola.png','Interior of Jama Masjid, Beed, showing stone arches, domes, and intricate carvings preserved through restoration.[5]','Interior of Jama Masjid, Beed, showing stone arches, domes, and intricate carvings preserved through restoration.[5]',''),(11321,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/architecture/the-khandoba-mandir-in-beed-features-two-oc_omnUFGB.png','The Khandoba Mandir in Beed features two octagonal Dipmalas and 32 intricately carved stone pillars. Some of the terracotta figures are believed to show influences from classical military motifs.[6]','The Khandoba Mandir in Beed features two octagonal Dipmalas and 32 intricately carved stone pillars. Some of the terracotta figures are believed to show influences from classical military motifs.[6]',''),(11341,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/artforms/gormati-art-which-is-associated-with-the-banjar_0DdIHCm.png','Gormati Art which is associated with the Banjara community[1]','Gormati Art which is associated with the Banjara community[1]','https://penoindia.com/organization'),(11346,'culture/images/maharashtra/beed/artforms/meerabai-umap-with-a-kanjiri2-37f47d57.png','Meerabai Umap with a Kanjiri[2]','Meerabai Umap with a Kanjiri[2]','https://ruralindiaonline.org/en/