ing a Shivling. (Source: CKA Archives)','Interior corridor of a Kharosa cave with carved stone pillars. These pillars likely represent dwarpals (guardian figures) and lead to a shrine housing a Shivling. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16207,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/view-of-udgir-fort-located-in-latur-distri_Dzcgh2b.png','View of Udgir Fort, located in Latur district. Originally believed to have been built during the Chalukya period, the fort was later expanded under Bahmani and Barid Shahi rule. (Source: CKA Archives)','View of Udgir Fort, located in Latur district. Originally believed to have been built during the Chalukya period, the fort was later expanded under Bahmani and Barid Shahi rule. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16208,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/arched-passageway-at-udgir-fort-featuring-_DdynAbg.png','Arched passageway at Udgir Fort, featuring a sequence of connected stone arches typical of medieval Deccan fort architecture. (Source: CKA Archives)','Arched passageway at Udgir Fort, featuring a sequence of connected stone arches typical of medieval Deccan fort architecture. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16210,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/a-small-viewing-window-embedded-in-the-out_KBslvIR.png','A small viewing window embedded in the outer wall of Udgir Fort. Such openings were likely used for surveillance and ventilation. (Source: CKA Archives)','A small viewing window embedded in the outer wall of Udgir Fort. Such openings were likely used for surveillance and ventilation. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16214,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/stone-ramparts-of-ausa-fort-the-forts-arch_rBGRV1W.png','Stone ramparts of Ausa Fort. The fort’s architecture is designed for multi-tiered defense, strength, and strategic concealment. (Source: CKA Archives)','Stone ramparts of Ausa Fort. The fort’s architecture is designed for multi-tiered defense, strength, and strategic concealment. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16215,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/ornamental-stonework-on-the-inner-wall-of-_aNA1BS0.png','Ornamental stonework on the inner wall of Ausa Fort, including carved motifs of lions and elephants typical of the Yadava and Bahmani periods. (Source: CKA Archives)','Ornamental stonework on the inner wall of Ausa Fort, including carved motifs of lions and elephants typical of the Yadava and Bahmani periods. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16217,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/the-entrance-to-ausa-fort-located-in-ausa-_GeEpDvP.png','The entrance to Ausa Fort, located in Ausa taluka, Latur district. Believed to have been in use since at least the 14th century, with later modifications under the Bahmani and Ahmadnagar Sultanates. (Source: CKA Archives)','The entrance to Ausa Fort, located in Ausa taluka, Latur district. Believed to have been in use since at least the 14th century, with later modifications under the Bahmani and Ahmadnagar Sultanates. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16218,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/ruins-of-the-fort-structure-featuring-weat_tCyBoUE.png','Ruins of the fort structure, featuring weathered stone ramparts and twin arched elements now overtaken by vegetation. (Source: CKA Archives)','Ruins of the fort structure, featuring weathered stone ramparts and twin arched elements now overtaken by vegetation. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16222,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/architecture/exterior-of-neelkantheshwar-mandir-in-latu_Pf3ds75.jpg','Exterior of Neelkantheshwar Mandir in Latur district, built in the Hemadpanthi style between the 12th and 14th centuries. The Mandir features a tall shikhara made from locally available black stone. (Source: CKA Archives)','Exterior of Neelkantheshwar Mandir in Latur district, built in the Hemadpanthi style between the 12th and 14th centuries. The Mandir features a tall shikhara made from locally available black stone. (Source: CKA Archives)',NU