 and caves.The site serves both religious and recreational purposes, drawing regular visitors from nearby areas. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16339,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/cultural-sites/dome-of-the-jagdamba-mandirthe-structure_Dhp2izU.jpg','Dome of the Jagdamba Mandir.The structure is part of Latur’s central Ganj Golai market. (Source: CKA Archives)','Dome of the Jagdamba Mandir.The structure is part of Latur’s central Ganj Golai market. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16341,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/cultural-sites/ceiling-of-the-jagdamba-devi-mandir-latu_fu375mh.jpg','Ceiling of the Jagdamba Devi Mandir, Latur decorated with glass and mirror work.This feature was added in a later renovation. (Source: CKA Archives)','Ceiling of the Jagdamba Devi Mandir, Latur decorated with glass and mirror work.This feature was added in a later renovation. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16343,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/cultural-sites/miniature-dolls-called-parson-with-the-f_KMIih46.jpg','Miniature dolls called “Parson” with the face of a child, believed to be Jagadamba devi’s son are worshiped popularly at the Jagdamba Devi Mandir, with women offering prayers and money to these dolls in the hope of bearing children.(Source: CKA Archives)','Miniature dolls called “Parson” with the face of a child, believed to be Jagadamba devi’s son are worshiped popularly at the Jagdamba Devi Mandir, with women offering prayers and money to these dolls in the hope of bearing children.(Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(16346,'culture/images/maharashtra/latur/cultural-sites/exterior-view-of-a-rock-cut-cave-at-khar_W34lFTQ.jpg','Exterior view of a rock-cut cave at Kharosa, Latur district, built during the Chalukya period (6th century CE). Visible on the facade is a carved figure of a yaksha, a guardian spirit commonly found in early Indian religious architecture. (Source: CKA Archives)','Exterior view of a rock-cut cave at Kharosa, Latur district, built during the Chalukya perio