kashrao Abitkar (SHS) and Madhuri Misal (BJP) currently serve as the Guardian Ministers for Kolhapur district. </span></p>'),(25664,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Kolhapur district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c26\"><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c19\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c19\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c19\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Kolhapur Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Satej Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Pune Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Jayant Asgaonkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Pune Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Arun Lad</span></p></td><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">NCP-SP </span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25666,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The following are the key administrative heads of Kolhapur district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c26\"><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c19\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c19\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c19\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Amol Yedge</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">K. Manjulekshmi</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Karthikeyan S.</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Rahul Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Mahendra Pandit</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c8\">Indian Police Services (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25669,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the  Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha constituencies of Kolhapur district.</span></p>'),(25671,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">There are 15 Urban Local Bodies in the Kolhapur District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 10 Nagar Parishad, and 3 Nagar Panchayat. There are 1025 Gram Panchayats and 12 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(25673,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The Kolhapur Zilla Parishad was established in 1962. At present, the President is Rahul Patil from the Indian National Congress, and with the recent Maharashtra administrative reshuffling (July 2023), Santosh C. Patil, previously the Joint Secretary to the Deputy Chief Minister, was made the Chief Executive Officer. In 2021, the Parishad was awarded the State Level Outstanding Zilla Parishad Award.</span></p>'),(25675,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The Kolhapur Municipal Corporation (KMC) was established on 12th October 1982. At present, KMC does not have a Commissioner and is under administrative rule. The last commissioner, Kadambari Balkawade, stepped down in 2020 after her term ended. Having served for almost three years, she was the longest-serving commissioner the district had seen. There are a total of 92 seats in the municipal corporation of Kolhapur. Parakhe Baburao Daulatrao became the first mayor of Kolhapur. Ever since the formation of the corporation, parties like the Indian National Congress, the Nationalist Congress Party, and the Bharatiya Janata Party have been in power most of the time. Shivsena, Shetakari Kamgar Paksha, etc, have also been influential players in the corporation.</span></p>'),(25677,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Kolhapur district has several municipal councils (Nagar Parishads) that serve as urban local governance bodies. These include Gadhinglaj Municipal Committee (considered to be one of the wealthiest in the country), Ichalkaranji Municipal Committee, Jaysingpur Municipal Committee, Kagal Municipal Committee, Kurundwad Municipal Committee, Malakapur Municipal Committee, Murgud Municipal Committee, Panhala Municipal Committee, Shirol Municipal Committee, and Vadgaon Municipal Committee.</span></p>'),(25679,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The district also has three Nagar Panchayats or town councils that function as local administrative units in semi-urban areas. These are the Ajara Nagar Panchayat, Chandgad Nagar Panchayat, and Hatkanangle Nagar Panchayat. </span></p>'),(25681,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Kolhapur district also includes the Hupari Nagar Parishad as one of its urban local governance bodies.</span></p>'),(25684,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In the 2024 Kolhapur Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 23 candidates, out of which five had criminal cases registered against them. Notably, the current MP, Chhatrapati Shahu Shahaji, has one case registered against him. In the 2024 Hatkanangale Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 27 candidates, out of which seven had criminal cases registered against them. Notably, the current MP, Dhairyasheel Sambhajirao Mane, has a clean record without any cases against him.</span></p>'),(25686,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In the 2019 election, there were a total of 58 candidates across all the Vidhan Sabha segments of Kolhapur. Out of which, around 30% of them had criminal cases registered against them, with about 15% of them having serious criminal cases. The SHS and NCP had the highest number of candidates with serious criminal cases. Out of the six elected MLAs, three have criminal cases registered, with two of them having serious criminal cases against them.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In the 2019 election, there were a total of 67 candidates across all the Vidhan Sabha segments of Hatkanangale. Out of which, around 35% of them had criminal cases registered against them, with about 20% of them having serious criminal cases. The SHS and NCP had the highest number of candidates with serious criminal cases.</span></p>'),(25689,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In December 2022, the Maharashtra Ekikaran Samiti (MES), a committee advocating for the merger of Belgaum (Karnataka) with Maharashtra, organized a protest from Belgaum to Kolhapur. The protest was aimed at highlighting the alleged violation of the fundamental rights of Marathi-speaking residents in the border region of Karnataka, wherein they feel that the Kannada language is being forced upon them. The event saw the participation of numerous citizens, activists, political leaders, and workers from both Belgaum and Kolhapur. Additionally, the participants called on political parties within the state to observe a one-day Maharashtra bandh as a show of support for the Marathi-speaking populace in Belgaum. This protest highlights the ongoing regional and linguistic sentiments related to the Maharashtra-Karnataka border dispute.</span></p>'),(25691,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">On 7 June 2023, communal tensions prevailed in the city of Kolhapur after some social media status messages showing images of the 17th-century Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb and 18th-century Mysore ruler Tipu Sultan, accompanied by audio messages allegedly objectionable to Hindu sentiments, went viral. After this, a protest was held by right-wing outfits, which soon turned violent with protestors indulging in stone pelting. A mob also entered Muslim-dominated localities and resorted to stone-pelting, destroying shop facades, and overturning hand carts. The police resorted to a lathi charge and got the situation under control. Internet access was curtailed till June 9, and prohibitory orders were issued till June 19 banning the assembly of five or more persons.</span></p>'),(25694,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Dr. Ratnappa Bharammappa Kumbhar, affectionately known as Deshbhakt Ratnappa Kumbhar, was a remarkable figure in the Indian Independence Movement and played a pivotal role in shaping the future of independent India. His contributions spanned from active participation in the freedom struggle to his involvement in the drafting of the Constitution of India, where he proudly signed alongside Dr. B.R. Ambedkar.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In recognition of his exceptional social work and unwavering dedication to the nation, Dr. Kumbhar was honored with the prestigious Padmashri Award in 1985. His political career was marked by remarkable achievements, including serving as a Member of Parliament in 1952, as well as a Member of the Legislative Council, Minister of Food and Civil Supplies, and even the Home Minister in the Maharashtra Government. His leadership qualities and commitment to public service reached their pinnacle when he assumed the role of the Speaker of the Maharashtra State Legislative Assembly in 1995.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Dr. Ratnappa Kumbhar\'s early years were characterized by a keen interest in political and social work. He established the Prajaparishad on 15 February 1938, as a means to challenge the local princely state rulers. His passion for the freedom movement led him to go underground for approximately six years, earning him the honorable title of Deshbhakt (Patriot) Ratanppa Kumbhar.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span>In addition to his contributions to the independence movement, Dr. Kumbhar was actively involved in the cooperative movement. He initiated the Panchaganga Cooperative Sugar Factory and the People\'s Cooperative Bank, among other endeavors. His legacy as a devoted patriot and advocate for social justice continues to inspire generations in their pursuit of a fair and equitable India.</span></p>'),(25696,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Mahadevrao Ramachandra Mahadik, the 82-year-old wrestler and political veteran from Kolhapur, is a legendary figure whose towering presence has shaped both the wrestling arenas and the political landscape of the district. A senior patriarch of the Mahadik family, one of Kolhapur’s most prominent political lineages, Mahadevrao is often compared to the Kolhapuri creeping falcon, recognizable by his distinctive forehead mark, radiant face, signature head mound, safari-style attire, and commanding physique.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Known as a Baahubali of Kolhapur politics, his stature and oratory earned him a formidable reputation. He served as an MLA in the legislative council for 18 years, and exerted considerable influence over major institutions such as Gokul and the district cooperative bank, reinforcing the family\'s hold over local politics.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span>Though newer generations of the Mahadik family - like his nephew Dhananjay Mahadik and Vishwaraj Mahadik - have entered the political field, Mahadevrao continues to be the central force of the Mahadik faction. His cinematic persona, underlined by dramatic quotes like \"I have the money from Sholay,\" and his unshakeable confidence, make him a towering and unforgettable figure in the political history of Kolhapur.</span></p>'),(25698,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Dhananjay Mahadik, a key member of the influential Mahadik family of Kolhapur, is a dynamic political leader and social activist known for his adaptability and wide-ranging public engagement. He began his political journey with the SHS, later joined the NCP, and eventually became a Rajya Sabha member through nomination by the BJP.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">He first contested the Lok Sabha elections in 2004 under the SHS  banner, but lost to four-time MP Sadashiv Mahadik. A decade later, in 2014, he staged a successful comeback by contesting as an NCP candidate and winning the Kolhapur seat in the Lok Sabha. His tenure was marked by effective parliamentary performance, earning him the Sansad Ratna award.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In 2019, he made a decisive political shift by joining the BJP. Beyond politics, Dhananjay Mahadik plays an active role in managing social welfare trusts and business ventures. His son, Krishnaraj Mahadik, is gaining attention as an emerging F1 driver and social media personality in Maharashtra, indicating the family\'s growing footprint in both political and public spheres.</span></p>'),(25700,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Nivedita Sambha Mane, born on 11 April 1963, is a prominent Indian politician who has been associated with both the NCP and SHS. She served as a Member of Parliament in the 14th Lok Sabha, representing the Hatkanangale constituency in Maharashtra. Mane was elected to the Lok Sabha for two consecutive terms, in 1999 and 2004, under the banner of the NCP. Her political career saw her advocate for the development of her constituency and support various welfare initiatives.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In the 2009 elections, however, she faced a significant defeat to Raju Shetty, a leader from the Swabhimani Shetkari Sanghatana, in the Hatkanangale constituency. Despite the setback, Mane remained active in Maharashtra politics. Over time, her political journey brought her into collaboration with Shiv Sena, where she continued her work for the people of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Throughout her career, Nivedita Mane has focused on issues related to rural development, farmers\' rights, and women\'s empowerment. Her political legacy in Hatkanangale is marked by her efforts to improve local infrastructure and support agricultural communities. Her son, Dhairyasheel Sambhajirao Mane, has been elected from the Hatkanangale seat for two consecutive terms in 2019 and 2024.</span></p>'),(25702,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Shivmati Sunita Chandrakant Patil proudly identifies herself as \"Shivmati,\" reflecting her deep admiration for Rajamata Jijau and Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj. She currently serves as the Kolhapur District President of the Rajmata Jijau Brigade and is the Chairman of the Shyam Housing Society in Devkar Pandan, Kolhapur. Born in Panhala taluka, Sunita was introduced to social work from a young age. Her father, a teacher, ensured she received the same opportunities and education as her brother, instilling in her the importance of equality. Growing up in a joint family, she understood the values of love, humanity, and strong familial bonds. Her grandfather\'s association with Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj influenced her rational and progressive thinking from childhood.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">For her further education, Sunita moved to Kolhapur, taluka Karveer, to live with her maternal uncle. During her time there, she noticed that some students desired extra classes but couldn\'t afford them due to financial constraints. In response, she took the initiative to start free classes for these students near Shivaji Peth in Kolhapur, marking her first significant step into social work.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Even after marriage, Sunita continued to receive strong support from her husband and in-laws. Her husband, a member of the Sambhaji Brigade under the Maratha Sangh, encouraged her involvement in social causes. In 2008, with his support, Sunita joined the Jijau Brigade and devoted herself wholeheartedly to its cause, helping the organization grow significantly. Through this work, she recognized that many issues men and women in society faced were domestic. She began mediating and solving numerous cases outside of court, urging people to avoid court proceedings for minor disputes that could be resolved through dialogue, saving them both time and money.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">However, due to internal conflicts within the Jijau Brigade, Sunita decided to leave the organization. In 2010, alongside her trusted companions Sudha Sarnaik, Suvarna Mithari, Lata Jagtap, and Chhaya Jadhav, she founded the Rajmata Jijau Brigade. As the leader of this new organization, Sunita worked tirelessly to resolve issues related to domestic violence, divorce, neighborhood disputes, and more. She also played a crucial role in exposing corrupt police officers and government officials who refused to assist the public.</span></p>'),(25706,'<p class=\"c11 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c42\">LOCAL POLITICS: LATUR</span></p>'),(25708,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The district of Latur - named after Lattalur, an ancient city of the Rashtrakuta dynasty - was officially established in 1982, carved out from parts of the Dharashiv (then Osmanabad) district. At the time of India’s independence, the region that now forms Latur district was part of the princely Hyderabad State under the rule of Nizam Mir Osman Ali Khan. It was annexed into the Indian Union in 1950 and later, under the States Reorganisation Act of 1956, the Hyderabad State was dissolved. The region was merged into the Bombay State. Following the Bombay Reorganisation Act of 1960, the Bombay State was bifurcated into Gujarat and Maharashtra, and Latur became part of Maharashtra. In 1981, the region was administratively separated from Dharashiv district, and in 1982, Latur was officially declared a separate district.</span></p>'),(25711,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The Latur District includes the Latur Lok Sabha constituency, which is reserved for Scheduled Castes – the seat was established only in 1962, by the third round of elections. The Lok Sabha constituency comprises 6 Vidhan Sabha segments: Ahmedpur, Latur City, Latur Rural, Loha, Nilanga, and Udgir (SC). Within these, the Loha Constituency is located in the Nanded district, making it the only Vidhan Sabha segment of Latur that is located outside the Latur district.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur City</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ahmedpur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur Rural</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Loha</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nanded</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nilanga</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Udgir (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c32\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ausa</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Osmanabad</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Along with this, the Latur district includes the Ausa Constituency, which comes under the Osmanabad  Lok Sabha constituency. The given map provides an overview of the Latur District and the Latur Lok Sabha constituency.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Latur District is represented by Osmanabad-cum-Latur-cum-Beed Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Aurangabad Teachers’ Constituency, and the Aurangabad Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Latur district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it.</span></p>'),(25714,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, it is evident that there have been significant changes to the Vidhan Sabha constituencies within the Latur district over the years. Particularly noteworthy is the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies between the Osmanabad, Latur, and Nanded Lok Sabha constituencies.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Latur Lok Sabha constituency and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(25717,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Latur district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c38\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Dr. Kalge Shivaji Bandappa</span></p></td><td class=\"c38\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25719,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Latur district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c28\"><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Seat</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Name of MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur Rural</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ramesh Karad</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur City</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Amit Vilasrao Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ahmadpur</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Babasaheb Mohanrao Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Udgir</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bansode Sanjay Baburao</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">NCP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nilanga</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nilangekar Sambhaji Diliprao Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ausa</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Abhimanyu Dattatray Pawar</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25721,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">There is no member currently in the Rajya Sabha who is from the Latur district.</span></p>'),(25723,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The Guardian Minister of Kolhapur is Shivendra Raje Bhosale from the BJP. </span></p>'),(25725,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Latur district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c45\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c30\"><td class=\"c45\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Osmanabad-cum-Latur-cum-Beed Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c45\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Aurangabad Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satish Chawan</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">NCP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c45\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Aurangabad Teachers\' Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vikram Kale</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">NCP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25727,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the key administrative heads of Latur district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c16\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c15\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Varsha Thakur-Ghuge</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Anmol Sagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">President of Zilla Parishad, Latur</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bansode Dattatray Gunderao</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Superintendent of Police, Latur District</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Somay Munde</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Police Services (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25730,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the  Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha constituencies of Latur district. </span></p>'),(25732,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">There are 10 Urban Local Bodies in the Latur District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 4 Nagar Parishad, 5 Nagar Panchayat. There are 1156 Gram Panchayats and 15 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(25734,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur district has one Zilla Parishad, headed by Chief Executive Officer Anmol Sagar (IAS).</span></p>'),(25736,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The Latur Municipal Corporation (LMC), established on 25th October 2011, is responsible for the administration of Latur city. As of 2023, the corporation governs a total of 70 wards. Since its formation, political control of the LMC has alternated between the BJP, INC, and SHS. The current Mayor, Vikrant Vikram Gojamunde of the BJP, was elected in 2017; however, subsequent civic body elections were postponed due to the COVID-19 pandemic. In the administrative reshuffle of July 2023, Varsha Thakur-Ghuge (IAS) was appointed as the Collector and District Magistrate of Latur district.</span></p>'),(25738,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Latur district has several municipalities (Nagar Palikas) that function as urban local governance bodies for medium-sized towns. These include the Ahamedpur Nagar Palika, Udgir Nagar Palika, Ausa Nagar Palika, and Nilanga Nagar Palika.</span></p>'),(25740,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The district also has a number of Nagar Panchayats, which serve as town councils for smaller or semi-urban settlements. These are the Jalkot Nagar Panchayat, Deoni Nagar Panchayat, Renapur Nagar Panchayat, Shirur Anantpal Nagar Panchayat, and Chakur Nagar Panchayat.</span></p>'),(25743,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">During the 2024 Lok Sabha elections, there were 28 candidates contesting for the Latur Lok Sabha seat. Out of these nine had criminal cases registered against them, and three had serious criminal cases registered against them. An independent candidate had 10 cases registered against him. The elected MP, Dr. Kalge Shivaji Bandappa, has no cases registered against him.</span></p>'),(25745,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">During the 2019 Vidhan Sabha elections, there were a total of 75 candidates contesting from the Vidhan Sabha segments of Latur. Out of these, 16 candidates had criminal cases registered against them, with nine of them having serious cases registered. Of the six elected MLAs, only Nilangekar Sambaji Patil from Nilanga Vidhan Sabha constituency has serious criminal cases registered against him.</span></p>'),(25748,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2008, Pangaon village in Latur witnessed communal clashes arising due to an issue of access to passages near religious sites. The argument among a young group escalated violently and resulted in the death of 4 individuals. In response, the local administration imposed a curfew in the affected area. It took a week for the situation to return to normalcy.</span></p>'),(25750,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Anandwadi village, more commonly known as Gaur, in Nilanga taluka of Latur district, elected an all-woman panchayat in 2021, with six members chosen unopposed in the local elections. This milestone was made possible by the earlier efforts of two-time sarpanch Bhagyashree Chame, who, along with Shobha Kasle and Kalpana Sagar, had been among the three women elected to the panchayat in 2015.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Under their leadership, the village has witnessed significant transformations. These include improved sanitation, a campaign to eliminate superstitions, and the symbolic change of nameplates on all 112 homes to reflect the names of women occupants. Other initiatives include installing a sanitary napkin dispenser, promoting the inclusion of widows in festivals, encouraging organ donation, and setting up a free flour-grinding mill for residents.</span></p>'),(25752,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In September-November 2023, several districts in eastern Maharashtra experienced the impact of state-wide protests led by Jarange Patil, demanding reservation for the Maratha community. On 24 October 2023, thousands of protestors from the Marathi community gathered at Borgaon Kale village and blocked the Murud-Latur road, holding up traffic for nearly two hours. Protests also took place in the villages of Nagzari and Katgaon.</span></p>'),(25755,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Abhimanyu Pawar, hailing from Ausa in Latur, is a prominent political figure currently serving as the BJP MLA representing the Ausa Vidhan constituency within the Osmanabad Lok Sabha. He has a strong educational background, holding a master\'s degree in management from Savitribai Phule Pune University.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Abhimanyu Pawar\'s political journey began as a dedicated party worker in the Bharatiya Janata Yuva Morcha, the youth wing of the BJP. His commitment to the party and its ideals led him to serve as a Personal Assistant to the former Chief Minister of Maharashtra, Devendra Fadnavis.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span>One of his notable areas of focus in his political career has been advocating for the concerns of aspirants for state public service exams. He has actively engaged with the state government to address these concerns and seek amicable and sustainable solutions to the issues faced by civil service exam candidates.</span></p>'),(25757,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Vilasrao Deshmukh was one of the most influential political figures to emerge from Latur. He began his career at the grassroots level, serving as the Sarpanch of Babhulgaon village in the then-Osmanabad district and later as a member of the Osmanabad Zilla Parishad. In 1980, he contested and won his first election as an INC MLA from the Latur constituency with a commanding 40% margin. Over the course of his career, he was elected to the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly five times.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1999, Vilasrao became the 14th Chief Minister of Maharashtra, a position he held across two terms until 2008. Known for his witty, accessible, and charismatic style, he famously opened Varsha - the chief minister\'s official residence - to the public. He held multiple key portfolios over the years, including home affairs, tourism, agriculture, industry, and rural development.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">His second term as Chief Minister was marked by the 26/11 Mumbai attacks, after which he resigned amid criticism over an incident involving his son, actor Riteish Deshmukh, and filmmaker Ram Gopal Varma visiting the Taj Hotel post-attack. Nevertheless, Vilasrao remained an active figure in national politics, serving as a Union Minister in the UPA II government under Dr. Manmohan Singh. He was instrumental in mediating between the government and Anna Hazare during the Lokpal agitation. Vilasrao Deshmukh passed away on 14 August 2012, and is still remembered as one of Maharashtra’s most beloved and accessible leaders.</span></p>'),(25759,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Amit Deshmukh, the eldest son of Vilasrao Deshmukh, followed closely in his father\'s political footsteps. He began engaging with local politics during his college years in Latur and gradually rose through the ranks of the Congress party. In 2009, he was elected to the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from the Latur City constituency, a seat he has retained in every election since.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">During Prithviraj Chavan\'s tenure as Chief Minister, Amit served as Minister of Food and Drugs Administration as well as Minister of New and Renewable Energy. Under Uddhav Thackeray’s leadership, he held the crucial portfolio of Medical Education and also served as the Guardian Minister of Latur. His handling of health infrastructure during the COVID-19 pandemic was widely appreciated, and he is affectionately referred to by many in Latur as “Chote Vilasrao.” He currently serves as the National Secretary of the Indian National Congress.</span></p>'),(25761,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The Kamble family has played a prominent role in Latur’s early political history. Tulsiram Kamble, representing the INC, was the first Member of Parliament elected from Latur in 1962. He served three consecutive terms, remaining in office until 1971. Continuing the political legacy, his son Arvind Kamble was elected as an INC MLA from the Her Vidhan Sabha constituency in 1980.</span></p>'),(25763,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Keshavrao Sonawane’s political journey began in Mogarga, a village in Ausa taluka, where he was born into a farming family. While practicing law at the Latur district court, his interest in politics steadily grew.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1962, he received a crucial opportunity when Yashwantrao Chavan, then Chief Minister of Bombay State, selected him as the Congress candidate for the Latur Vidhan Sabha seat. Despite a closely contested race, Sonawane won by a slim margin of 5%, marking the beginning of a successful political career. He went on to serve three consecutive terms as an MLA - twice from Latur and once from Ausa - between 1962 and 1978.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Sonawane is remembered for his lasting contributions to Latur’s development, particularly for bringing the first Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation (MIDC) unit to the region in the 1960s. He held important cabinet positions under Chief Ministers Yashwantrao Chavan and Vasantrao Naik. As Cooperative Minister under Chavan, he helped establish Asia’s first cooperative oil mill, the Dalda factory. He also played a key role in drafting the Agricultural Produce Marketing Committee (APMC) Act and supported the founding of institutions such as the Dayanand Education Society and Terna Sugar Factory.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Through his focus on cooperative principles and regional development, Keshavrao Sonawane left a lasting legacy in both Latur and Maharashtra’s political history.</span></p>'),(25765,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Shivajirao Bhaurao Patil-Nilangekar was a towering figure in Maharashtra’s political landscape and served as an MLA from the INC for seven consecutive terms, representing the Nilanga constituency. His long political career was marked by significant contributions, most notably the founding of the Maharashtra Education Trust in 1968. This initiative played a crucial role in expanding access to education across the region and nurturing generations of students.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1985, Shivajirao Nilangekar reached the peak of his career when he was appointed Chief Minister of Maharashtra. However, his term became the shortest in the state’s history. His resignation was prompted by a controversy involving alleged irregularities in medical college admissions, implicating his daughter and her friend. Despite the scandal, his legacy as an educationist and political leader remains significant in the region’s development.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Continuing the family\'s political involvement, Rupali Patil Nilangekar, his daughter-in-law, entered politics through the BJP. In the 2004 Lok Sabha elections, she made history by becoming the first woman Member of Parliament from the Latur constituency, winning by a narrow margin of 4%. Her election marked a new chapter in the Nilangekar family\'s ongoing political presence in the district.</span></p>'),(25767,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Shivraj Patil, a veteran Congress politician and administrator hailing from Chakur in Latur district, is a member of the prominent Patil political family. He began his political journey as an MLA from the Latur constituency in 1972 and was re-elected in 1978. In 1980, he successfully contested the Latur Lok Sabha seat and went on to win seven consecutive terms, making him the longest-serving Member of Parliament from the constituency. As an MP, he served in key ministerial roles - first as Minister of Commerce and Industry under Indira Gandhi, and later as Minister for Personnel, Defense Production, Civil Aviation, and Tourism under Rajiv Gandhi. Between 1983 and 1986, he was also Vice President of the Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR).</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">From 1991 to 1996, Shivraj Patil served as the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, during which he introduced the Best Parliamentarian Award and facilitated the live broadcasting of parliamentary proceedings. In 2004, he was appointed Union Home Minister, but his tenure ended in 2008 following criticism after the Malegaon bombing and the 26/11 Mumbai attacks. He later served as the Governor of Punjab and Administrator of Chandigarh from 2010 to 2015.</span></p>'),(25769,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Archana Patil, a key political figure in Latur and part of the same prominent Patil family, has also played an active role in politics and social development. Initially associated with the INC, she began her career as a grassroots worker and became a significant presence in local politics. As the daughter-in-law of Shivraj Patil, she served as a member of the Latur Municipal Corporation, contributing to governance and civic administration.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Her work has particularly focused on women’s empowerment through skill development and self-employment programs, along with initiatives aimed at improving infrastructure and sanitation in the region. More recently, she joined the BJP, leading to speculation about her potential candidacy from the Latur City Vidhan Sabha seat - a constituency traditionally held by the Congress since 1957, barring two exceptions in 1967 and 1995.</span></p>'),(25771,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Sudhakar Tukaram Shrangare, a prominent Dalit leader in Maharashtra, currently serves as the BJP Member of Parliament from the Latur constituency in the 17th Lok Sabha. Coming from a modest family background, Shrangare faced significant challenges in his early life, including having to discontinue his education after the 12th grade and working in labor and construction to support himself. Despite these hardships, he built a strong grassroots presence and is widely respected for his compassionate and accessible approach. His popularity cuts across community lines, earning him broad admiration for his humility and dedication to public service.</span></p>'),(25776,'<p class=\"c45 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c49\">LOCAL POLITICS: MUMBAI CITY</span></p>'),(25778,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The name ‘Mumbai’ is derived from the patron deity of the city, Mumba Devi, worshiped by the earliest inhabitants, the Kolis. Mumbai (also known as Bombay, the official name until 1995) is the capital city of Maharashtra. It is the most populous city in India with an estimated city proper population of 12.4 million as of 2011. Along with the neighboring regions of the Mumbai Metropolitan Region, it is the second most populous metropolitan area in India. In 1990, the erstwhile Mumbai district was bifurcated into Mumbai City and Mumbai Suburban districts. The current Mumbai city district comprises an area from Colaba in the south to Mahim and Sion in the north.</span></p>'),(25781,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The district consists of the following Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha constituencies:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c11\"><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Dharavi</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mahim</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Sion Koliwada</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Wadala</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Anushakti Nagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai Suburban</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Chembur</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai Suburban</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Byculla</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Colaba</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Malabar Hill</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbadevi</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Shivadi</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Worli</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai City</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Mumbai City District is represented by Mumbai City Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Konkan Teachers’ Constituency, and the Konkan Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Mumbai city district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it.</span></p>'),(25784,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, it is evident that there have been significant changes to the Vidhan Sabha constituencies within the Mumbai city district over the years. The given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of Mumbai’s Lok Sabha constituencies and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(25787,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Mumbai City in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c11\"><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Arvind Sawant</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai South</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Shiv Sena - Uddhav Balasaheb Thackeray (SHS-UBT)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Anil Yeshwant Desai</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai - South Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS (UBT)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25789,'<p class=\"c10\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Mumbai City, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c11\"><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Sana Malik</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Anushakti Nagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Tukaram Kate</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Chembur</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Dr. Jyoti Gaikwad</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Dharavi (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Captain R. Tamil Selvan</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Sion Koliwada</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Kalidas Kolambkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Wadala</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mahesh Sawant</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mahim</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS-UBT</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Aditya Thackeray</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Worli</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS-UBT</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Ajay Choudhari</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Shivadi</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS-UBT</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Manoj Jamsutkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Byculla</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS-UBT</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mangal Prabhat Lodha</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Malabar Hill</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Amin Patel</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbadevi</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c20\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Rahul Narwekar</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Colaba</span></p></td><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25791,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Priyanka Chaturvedi, Anil Desai, and Sanjay Raut currently represent Maharashtra, from this district, in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(25793,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The current Guardian Minister of the district is Eknath Shinde from SHS. </span></p>'),(25795,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Mumbai City district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c11\"><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c41\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Party </span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c41\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Sunil Shinde</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS (UBT)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c41\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Raj Hans Singh</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c41\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Jagannath Abhyankar</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS (UBT)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c41\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mumbai Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Vilas Potnis</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS(UBT)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25797,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The following are the key administrative heads of Mumbai City, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c11\"><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c24\"><span class=\"c31\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Sanjay Yadav</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Bhushan Gagrani</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Kishori Pednekar</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">SHS (Caretaker)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c3\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Vivek Phansalkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c7\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25800,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the  Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha constituencies of Mumbai city district.</span></p>'),(25802,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">There is one single Urban Local Body: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 0 Nagar Parishad, 0 Nagar Panchayat. There are 0 Gram Panchayats.</span></p>'),(25804,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The Brihanmumbai Municipal Corporation, also known as the Municipal Corporation of Greater Mumbai (MCGM), was formally established in 1873, making it one of the oldest local self-governing bodies in India. Its establishment followed the formation of the Bombay Port Trust in 1872. As of 2023, the BMC administers a total of 227 electoral wards. The SHS held dominant influence in the corporation for over 15 years, marking a significant phase in Mumbai’s urban governance. Other major political parties such as the INC, NCP, BJP, and Samajwadi Party (SP) have also maintained an active presence through elected corporators, reflecting the diversity and competitive nature of Mumbai’s local politics.</span></p>'),(25807,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 29 candidates from the Mumbai city district (14 from Mumbai South and 15 from Mumbai South-Central). Out of which, eight candidates had criminal cases against them, with only five of them having serious cases registered. The elected MP of Mumbai South-Central had no criminal cases registered against him, while the elected MP from Mumbai South had one case registered against him.</span></p>'),(25810,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">From the 1940s to the early 2010s, Mumbai witnessed the rise and fall of several gangsters who shaped the city\'s notorious underworld. These individuals and their syndicates operated across the city and its suburbs, influencing not only crime but also politics, law enforcement, and real estate. Prominent names include Haji Mastan, Varadarajan Mudaliar, Karim Lala, Dawood Ibrahim, Arun Gawli, Haseena Parkar, Chhota Rajan, Rama Naik, Babu Reshim, Sai Bansod, Sada Pawle, Kiran Walawalkar, Ganesh Bhosle alias Vakil, Chandrashekhar Mirashi, Ashok Choudhary alias Chhota Babu, Parasnath Pandey, Shashi Rasam, Mohan Sarmalkar, and Kundan Dubey.</span></p>'),(25812,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Haji Mastan, born in 1926 in Pannaikulam near Ramanathapuram, Tamil Nadu, was one of Mumbai’s earliest and most iconic gangsters. After moving to Bombay at the age of 8, Mastan began working as a porter on the docks by 1944. Over time, he entered the world of smuggling and rose rapidly in the city’s underworld. By the 1960s, Mastan had become extremely wealthy through gold, silver, and electronic goods smuggling. Known for his charisma and style, he was closely associated with Karim Lala and became the first gangster in Mumbai to gain celebrity-like status.</span></p>'),(25814,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Varadarajan Mudaliar, widely known as Vardhabhai, was a dominant Tamil Hindu gangster who operated from the 1960s to the 1980s. He held immense influence over Dharavi and Matunga, where he ran a parallel justice system, with his word seen as final by local residents. His rise paralleled that of Haji Mastan and Karim Lala, forming a powerful triad of crime in Mumbai. Vardhabhai was known for maintaining strong ties with the Tamil community and controlling much of the city’s east and north-central regions.</span></p>'),(25816,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Karim Lala led one of Mumbai’s most feared mafia syndicates from the 1940s through the 1980s. His group, often referred to as the “Pathan mafia” or “Afghan mafia” by law enforcement, consisted mainly of ethnic Pashtuns from Afghanistan’s Kunar province. Lala’s syndicate controlled bootlegging, gambling, gold smuggling, hashish trafficking, extortion, and contract killings. He was especially influential around the Mumbai docks and retained power for decades, remaining a major figure until the mid-1980s.</span></p>'),(25818,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Dawood Ibrahim, originally from Khed in Ratnagiri district, founded the infamous D-Company, one of the most powerful and internationally recognised crime syndicates. He initially operated in Mumbai but shifted base after the 1993 Bombay bombings, which he is alleged to have masterminded along with Tiger Memon. D-Company is often described as a criminal-terrorist nexus rather than a traditional cartel, with operations spanning smuggling, extortion, real estate, and international crime. Dawood remains on India’s most-wanted list.</span></p>'),(25820,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Haseena Parkar, Dawood Ibrahim’s younger sister, emerged as a feared and influential figure in Mumbai’s underworld, especially in the Nagpada area. Often referred to as the \"mafia queen\" or the \"godmother of Nagpada,\" she took over many of her brother’s operations in the city after his departure. She was known to have mediated disputes and controlled extortion rackets until her death in 2014 from cardiac arrest.</span></p>'),(25822,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Arun Gawli began his criminal career in Dagdi Chawl, Byculla, which became his stronghold. His gang was involved in extortion, kidnapping, and murder, often using the chawl as a base for criminal activities. Though frequently arrested, Gawli managed to avoid convictions due to witness intimidation. In 2004, he entered politics, founding the Akhil Bharatiya Sena and winning a seat in the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from the Chinchpokli constituency. However, in 2012, he was convicted for the murder of SHS politician Kamalakar Jamsandekar and sentenced to life imprisonment.</span></p>'),(25824,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The demand for a separate state of Maharashtra, with Mumbai as its capital, gained momentum in the early 1960s. Driven by linguistic and cultural identity concerns, Marathi-speaking regions sought autonomy from the bilingual Bombay State. On 21 November 1955, demonstrators were fired upon by the police at Flora Fountain in the capital city of Bombay. Flora Fountain was subsequently renamed Hutatma Chowk or \"Martyr\'s Crossroad\" in their memory. It is estimated that a total of 106 people were shot by security forces during the period of agitation and at different places.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The movement culminated in the formation of Maharashtra on 1 May 1960, with Mumbai as its capital. The creation of Maharashtra was a significant milestone in Mumbai\'s history, solidifying its position as the political and economic heart of the state. The statehood movement underscored the importance of regional identity and self-determination in India\'s diverse political landscape</span></p>'),(25826,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The Great Bombay Textile Strike was a textile strike called on 18 January 1982 by the mill workers of Mumbai under trade union leader Dutta Samant. The purpose of the strike was to obtain a bonus payment and an increase in wages. Nearly 250,000 workers of 65 textile mills went on strike in Mumbai. As a consequence, the majority of the over 80 mills in Central Mumbai closed during and after the strike, leaving more than 150,000 workers unemployed. The textile industry in Mumbai has largely disappeared, reducing labour migration after the strikes.</span></p>'),(25828,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The Bombay riots, a series of violent clashes, occurred in Mumbai (then known as Bombay), between December 1992 and January 1993. These riots were triggered by a series of communal tensions following the demolition of the Babri Masjid in Ayodhya on 6 December 1992. The Masjid’s destruction, carried out by Hindu nationalist groups, incited widespread outrage among Muslims across India, leading to protests and retaliatory violence.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The riots unfolded in two phases. The first phase, in December 1992, was marked by Muslim anger over the demolition, with many attacks on Hindus and Hindu establishments. In response, the second phase, in January 1993, saw violent reprisals by Hindus, inflamed further by right-wing Hindu organizations like the Shiv Sena, which called for retaliatory action.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The riots claimed around 900 lives, though some estimates place the toll much higher. The victims included both Hindus and Muslims, but Muslims suffered a disproportionately high number of casualties. Thousands of people were injured, and property, including homes, businesses, and religious sites, was extensively damaged. In addition to the fatalities, many women were subjected to brutal sexual violence during the unrest.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Several reports and investigations, including the Srikrishna Commission, which was set up to investigate the riots, pointed to the involvement of the Shiv Sena and criticized the role of the police, accusing them of biased and inadequate responses. However, many of the perpetrators were never brought to justice.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The violence culminated in further tragedy with the Bombay bombings of March 1993, a coordinated series of bomb explosions across the city, believed to be orchestrated by the underworld, in retaliation for the riots. These events left a lasting scar on Mumbai, deepening the communal divide and altering the social and political fabric of the city.</span></p>'),(25830,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">On October 10, 2006, clashes erupted between supporters of the SHS and the Maharashtra Navnirman Sena (MNS), led by Raj Thackeray. The conflict began when MNS workers allegedly tore down posters featuring SHS supremo Bal Thackeray near SIES College in Mumbai. In retaliation, SHS workers were accused of removing hoardings bearing Raj Thackeray\'s image near Sena Bhavan in Dadar. As news of the incident spread, groups from both parties gathered near Sena Bhavan, hurling stones at each other.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The violence escalated, leading to injuries among several supporters from both sides and a policeman caught in the crossfire. To disperse the mob, police resorted to firing tear gas shells. Order was eventually restored when Uddhav Thackeray and his cousin Raj Thackeray arrived at the scene. Uddhav appealed to SHS supporters to return home, helping to defuse the situation.</span></p>'),(25832,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">The 2008 attacks on migrants from Uttar Pradesh and Bihar in Maharashtra began on 3 February 2008, following violent clashes between members of two political parties - Maharashtra Navnirman Sena and Samajwadi Party - in Dadar. The violence erupted when MNS workers, part of a splinter faction formed from the SHS, attempted to attack SP workers, a regional party from Uttar Pradesh, as they were heading to a rally organized by the United National Progressive Alliance.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">On 13 February 2008, the state government, which had faced criticism for its delayed response, arrested both Raj Thackeray, leader of the MNS, and Abu Asim Azmi, a local SP leader, on charges of inciting violence and communal disturbance. Although both leaders were released the same day, a gag order was imposed to prevent them from making further inflammatory statements.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Tensions escalated across Maharashtra as news of Raj Thackeray\'s arrest spread, sparking anger among his supporters. Incidents of violence targeting North Indians and their property by MNS workers were reported in various cities, including Mumbai, Pune, Aurangabad, Beed, Nashik, Amravati, Jalna, and Latur.</span></p>'),(25835,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Badruddin Tyabji, born on 10 October 1844, in Bombay, was a distinguished lawyer, judge, and statesman. Coming from a prominent Muslim family, Tyabji received a comprehensive education that laid the foundation for his illustrious career in law and public service. His early exposure to the legal profession sparked his interest in justice and governance, shaping his future endeavors.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">After completing his legal studies in England, Tyabji returned to India and embarked on a successful legal practice in Mumbai. He quickly gained recognition for his expertise in various areas of law, including civil and criminal litigation.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Badruddin Tyabji was deeply engaged in India\'s political landscape, advocating for constitutional reforms and greater Indian representation in government. He was a vocal supporter of the Indian National Congress and played a pivotal role in shaping the party\'s agenda and policies. Tyabji\'s leadership qualities and statesmanship earned him the admiration of his peers, and he was elected as the President of the Indian National Congress in 1887.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">As President of the Congress, Tyabji championed the cause of Indian nationalism and worked tirelessly to advance the interests of the Indian people. He emphasized the importance of unity and solidarity among diverse communities and called for peaceful and constructive dialogue with British authorities. Tyabji\'s diplomatic skills and strategic vision helped consolidate the Congress as a formidable force in India\'s struggle for independence.</span></p>'),(25838,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Balasaheb Thackeray (fondly known as Bal Thackeray) was born on 23 January 1926, in Pune, to Marathi activist Prabodhankar Thackeray and his wife Ramabai. Raised in a middle-class Maharashtrian family, Thackeray developed a deep sense of pride in Marathi culture and identity from a young age. His formative years were marked by a passion for literature, journalism, and social activism, laying the groundwork for his future career in politics.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">In 1966, Bal Thackeray founded the Shiv Sena, a regional political party dedicated to promoting the interests of the Marathi-speaking population in Maharashtra. The party\'s ideology, known as \"Marathi Manoos\" (Marathi Person), emphasized the protection of Marathi culture, language, and identity. Under Thackeray\'s leadership, the SHS emerged as a formidable force in Maharashtra politics, advocating for the rights of the local populace and challenging the dominance of national parties.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Bal Thackeray\'s charismatic leadership and strong personality earned him a devoted following among his supporters, who affectionately referred to him as \"Balasaheb.\" However, his political career was also marked by controversy, particularly due to his often divisive rhetoric and tactics. Thackeray was known for his outspoken views on various social and political issues, including immigration, regionalism, and Hindu nationalism, which garnered both praise and criticism.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Despite his polarizing reputation, Bal Thackeray remains a towering figure in Maharashtra politics, revered by many for his unwavering commitment to the welfare of the Marathi-speaking population. His influence extended beyond politics, shaping cultural and social dynamics in Maharashtra for decades. Thackeray\'s legacy continues to resonate in the ideology and activities of the Shiv Sena party, which remains a dominant force in Maharashtra politics.</span></p>'),(25840,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Kashinath Trimbak Telang, born in 1850 in Bombay, was an eminent lawyer, judge, and social reformer. Raised in an environment that valued education and social service, Telang developed a keen interest in law and governance from an early age. His academic brilliance earned him a place at the Elphinstone College, where he excelled in his studies, later pursuing a career in law.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Telang became one of the youngest judges appointed to the Bombay High Court in 1889, where he served with distinction. Telang was known for his fair judgments and his dedication to upholding the rule of law. His legal acumen and integrity earned him immense respect in the legal fraternity.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">In addition to his judicial duties, Telang was deeply involved in legal education. He played a significant role in the establishment of the Bombay University Law College, where he served as a professor and later as the Vice-Chancellor. His efforts in legal education aimed at producing well-rounded legal professionals who could contribute to the nation\'s development.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Telang was also an active participant in the INC, joining the movement to advocate for Indian rights and self-governance. His speeches at the Congress sessions emphasized the need for constitutional reforms and greater representation for Indians in the colonial administration.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">A staunch advocate for social reform, Telang worked tirelessly to address various social issues in the city. He was particularly vocal about the need for education and empowerment of marginalized communities, advocating for equal opportunities for all. Telang\'s efforts in promoting social justice extended to his involvement in organizations dedicated to uplifting the underprivileged and promoting communal harmony.</span></p>'),(25842,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Pherozeshah Mehta, born on 4 August 1845, in Bombay (now Mumbai), was a prominent lawyer, politician, and social reformer. Coming from a well-to-do Parsi family, Mehta had the advantage of a sound education, which he utilized to its fullest potential. He pursued his higher studies in law at Lincoln\'s Inn in London, where he was called to the Bar in 1868. His time in England exposed him to the ideas of liberalism and democracy, which greatly influenced his future work in India.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Upon his return to India, Pherozeshah Mehta quickly established himself as a leading lawyer in Bombay. His legal acumen and eloquence in the courtroom earned him the title of \"The Lion of Bombay.\" One of his most notable legal battles was his defense of Bal Gangadhar Tilak in the sedition case of 1897, where Mehta’s powerful arguments highlighted the oppressive nature of British colonial rule and the importance of free speech.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Mehta’s legal expertise extended beyond the courtroom. He was instrumental in drafting the Bombay Municipal Act of 1888, which laid the foundation for modern municipal governance in the city. His contributions to this act earned him the nickname \"The Father of Bombay Municipality.\" The act introduced significant reforms, including better sanitation, improved public health services, and more efficient municipal administration.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">He was a founding member of the INC in 1885. Mehta was deeply involved in the Congress’s activities, advocating for constitutional reforms and greater Indian representation in government. In 1890, Mehta became the President of the INC. His speeches and writings emphasized the need for self-governance, civil liberties, and economic development. He believed in the power of dialogue and persuasion, often engaging with British officials to present the demands of the Indian people.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Mehta’s political influence extended to his role as a member of the Bombay Legislative Council, where he served for over three decades. His tenure was marked by relentless advocacy for the rights of Indians, particularly in matters of finance, education, and public health. He was known for his articulate and persuasive speeches, which highlighted the injustices faced by Indians under colonial rule and called for meaningful reforms.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Pherozeshah Mehta’s contributions were not limited to the legal and political arenas. He was a fervent social reformer, deeply committed to improving the lives of the underprivileged. He was a strong advocate for education, believing it to be the cornerstone of social progress. Mehta was instrumental in the establishment of several educational institutions, including the Bombay University, where he served as Vice-Chancellor from 1911 to 1914.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Mehta was also actively involved in various civic organizations and philanthropic activities. He played a key role in the Bombay Presidency Association, which aimed to represent the interests of the Indian community and promote social and economic development within the region.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">His efforts in public health, sanitation, and housing significantly improved the living conditions in Bombay, earning him the respect and admiration of his contemporaries.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span>One of the enduring symbols of Mehta’s legacy is the Pherozeshah Mehta Gardens, popularly known as the Hanging Gardens of Mumbai. These gardens, located on Malabar Hill, were developed to provide a green space for the city’s residents, reflecting Mehta’s commitment to public welfare and urban development.</span></p>'),(25845,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Poonam Mahajan, born on 9 December 1980, hails from Mumbai and comes from a distinguished political family. She is the daughter of late BJP leader Pramod Mahajan, known for his significant contributions to Indian politics.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Poonam Mahajan\'s foray into politics was a natural progression given her family\'s political legacy. She joined the BJP and quickly rose through the ranks, showcasing her leadership skills and dedication to public service. Mahajan contested her first Lok Sabha election from the Mumbai North Central constituency in 2014. She also went on to win the seat in 2019.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">As a Member of Parliament, Mahajan has been actively involved in legislative matters and policy formulation, advocating for the interests of her constituents and the nation at large. She has been vocal on issues such as youth empowerment, women\'s safety, and infrastructure development, working tirelessly to address the needs of Mumbai\'s diverse population.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">In addition to her political endeavors, Poonam Mahajan is actively engaged in community welfare and social initiatives. She has launched numerous programs to address pressing social issues, including healthcare, education, and environmental conservation.</span></p>'),(25847,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Priya Dutt, born on 28 August 1966, into one of Mumbai\'s most prominent political families, is the daughter of the late Sunil Dutt and Nargis Dutt. She joined the Indian National Congress and contested her first Lok Sabha election from the Mumbai North-West constituency in 2005. Dutt\'s campaign focused on issues such as healthcare, education, and women\'s empowerment, reflecting her commitment to addressing the needs of marginalized communities. She contested from the same seat in 2014 and 2019 on a Congress ticket but was unable to register a win.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">During her tenure as a Member of Parliament, Dutt spearheaded several initiatives aimed at improving healthcare infrastructure and access to medical services in Mumbai. Her advocacy for cancer awareness and support for patients undergoing treatment earned her widespread acclaim. Dutt also championed causes related to youth empowerment, environmental conservation, and social justice, actively engaging with constituents to address their concerns.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">In addition to her political career, Priya Dutt has been actively involved in social work and philanthropy. She established the Nargis Dutt Foundation in memory of her mother, focusing on healthcare, education, and women\'s empowerment. The foundation provides support to cancer patients, promotes preventive healthcare measures, and offers scholarships to underprivileged students.</span></p>'),(25850,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Raj Thackeray\'s given name, Swararaj, is often shortened to \"Raj.\" He was born to Shrikant Thackeray, the younger brother of Bal Thackeray, and Kunda Thackeray, who was the younger sister of Bal Thackeray’s wife, Meena. He graduated from Mumbai’s Sir J.J. Institute of Applied Art and later joined Marmik, Bal Thackeray’s weekly magazine, as a cartoonist.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Raj Thackeray’s political journey began with the launch of the Bharatiya Vidyarthi Sena, the student wing of SHS. He gained prominence during the campaign for the 1990 Maharashtra Assembly elections. During the 1990s, Raj saw himself as the potential successor to his uncle Bal Thackeray. However, Bal Thackeray eventually favored his son, Uddhav, for leadership within the SHS.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">After years of feeling sidelined, Raj Thackeray resigned from SHS on 27 November 2005, and announced his plans to form a new political party. On 9 March 2006, in Mumbai, he founded the Maharashtra Navnirman Sena (MNS).</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Since its inception, Raj has sparked numerous controversies through his speeches, which are often provocative and laced with humor, taking a strong pro-Marathi stance and frequently targeting North Indians.</span></p>'),(25853,'<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Varsha Gaikwad was born on 3 February 1975, to Eknath Gaikwad, a prominent politician who served three terms as a Member of Parliament. She holds an M.Sc. in Mathematics and a B.Ed. degree, and before entering politics, she worked as a lecturer at Siddharth College of Arts, Science, and Commerce in Mumbai.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">Her political career began in 2004 when she was elected as the MLA from the Dharavi constituency on a Congress ticket. She successfully retained the seat in subsequent elections in 2009, 2014, and 2019. From 2009 to 2010, she served as the State Minister for Medical Education, Higher and Technical Education, Tourism, and Special Assistance. From 2010 to 2014, she was appointed Cabinet Minister for Women and Child Development.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c7\">In 2019, Varsha Gaikwad was appointed Cabinet Minister for the School Education Department, a role she held until the Uddhav Thackeray government fell in 2022. In the 2024 Lok Sabha elections, she was elected from the Mumbai North Central seat, previously represented by her father in the 14th Lok Sabha, on an INC ticket. She is one of the three Buddhist MPs serving in the 18th Lok Sabha.</span></p>'),(25859,'<p class=\"c39 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c24\">LOCAL POLITICS: MUMBAI SUBURBAN</span></p>'),(25861,'<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">The Mumbai Suburban district was carved out on 1 October 1990, when the larger Mumbai district was bifurcated into Mumbai City and Mumbai Suburban. Its jurisdiction stretches from Bandra to Dahisar in the west, Kurla to Mulund in the east, and southwards up to the Trombay creek. Situated on Salsette Island, it is among the most populous districts in India. Unlike Mumbai City, which developed around the original seven islands, the Suburban district evolved along the broader expanse of Salsette. Over time, it has grown into a dense urban belt marked by residential complexes, industrial hubs, and transport corridors. The district plays a key role in the functioning of the Mumbai Metropolitan Region, housing a significant portion of the city’s working population. Despite being part of Greater Mumbai, it functions as a separate administrative district with its own collectorate and governance structure.</span></p>'),(25864,'<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">The Mumbai Suburban district consists of the following four Lok Sabha constituencies: Mumbai North, Mumbai North West, Mumbai North East, and Mumbai North Central. The Lok Sabha constituencies comprise the following Vidhan Sabha constituencies:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Borivali</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Charkop</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Dahisar</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Kandivali East</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Magathane</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Malad West</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Andheri East</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Andheri West</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Dindoshi</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Goregaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Jogeshwari East</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Versova</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhandup West</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ghatkopar East</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ghatkopar West</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mankhurd Shivaji Nagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mulund</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vikhroli</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Chandivali</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Kalina</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Kurla</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vandre East</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vandre West</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vile Parle</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Anushakti Nagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Chembur</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai South Central</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the district is represented by the Mumbai Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Mumbai Teachers’ Constituency, and the Mumbai Graduates Constituency.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Mumbai Suburban district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it.</span></p>'),(25867,'<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, it is evident that there have been significant changes to the Vidhan Sabha constituencies within the Mumbai suburban district over the years. </span></p>\n<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">The given charts provide an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of Mumbai’s Lok Sabha constituencies and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(25871,'<p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c1\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Mumbai Suburban district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c9\"><td class=\"c43\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c9\"><td class=\"c43\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North West</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Ravindra Waikar</span></p></td><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c9\"><td class=\"c43\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Piyush Goyal</span></p></td><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c9\"><td class=\"c43\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North East</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Sanjay Dina Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena - Uddhav Balasaheb Thackeray (SHS-UBT)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c9\"><td class=\"c43\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mumbai North Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Varsha Gaikwad</span></p></td><td class=\"c35\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian National Congress  (INC)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(25873,'<p class=\"c11\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Mumbai Suburban district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c25\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c14\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Sanjay Upadhyay</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Borivali</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Manisha Chaudhary</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Dahisar</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Prakash Surve</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Magathane</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mihir Kot