1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Shankar Jagtap</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Chinchwad</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Sunil Shelke</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Maval</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">NCP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Anna Daddu Bansode</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Pimpri (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">NCP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Mahesh Landge</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Bhosari</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Dilip Walse Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Ambegaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">NCP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Chetan Tupe</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Hadapsar</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">NCP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Sharad Sonavane</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Junnar</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Independent</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Babaji Kale</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Khed Alandi</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Shiv Sena - Uddhav Balasaheb Thackeray (SHS-UBT)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Dnyaneshwar Katke</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Shirur</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">NCP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26319,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Medha Vishram Kulkarni, Sunetra Pawar, Rajni Patil, and Sharad Pawar are from Pune district and are currently representing Maharashtra in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(26321,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The Guardian Minister for Pune is Ajit Pawar (NCP). </span></p>'),(26323,'<p class=\"c5\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Pune district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c16\"><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c38\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c19\"><span class=\"c8\">Vidhan Parishad  Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c19\"><span class=\"c8\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c19\"><span class=\"c8\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c38\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Pune Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c38\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Pune Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Jayant Asgaonkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c38\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Pune Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Arun Lad</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">NCP (SP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26325,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The following are the key administrative heads of Pune district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c39\"><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c19\"><span class=\"c8\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c19\"><span class=\"c8\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c19\"><span class=\"c8\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Dr. Suhas Diwase</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Vikram Kumar</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Mr. Santosh Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Nirmala Pansare</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Superintendent of Police, Pune Rural</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Sandeep Singh Gill</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c0\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Commissioner of Police, Pune City</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">Amitesh Kumar</span></p></td><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c2\">IPS</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26328,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Pune district.</span></p>'),(26330,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">There are 21 Urban Local Bodies in the Pune District: 2 Mahanagar Palika, 14 Nagar Parishad, and 2 Nagar Panchayat. There are 1407 Gram Panchayats and 13 Panchayat Samitis, and 3 Cantonment Boards. </span></p>'),(26332,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The Pune Zilla Parishad was established on 1 May 1962. Mr. Shankar Ursal served as its first President. The Zilla Parishad comprises 82 elected members and plays a key role in the rural governance and development of the district.</span></p>'),(26334,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The Pune Municipal Corporation (PMC), a key urban local body governing the city of Pune, was established on 15 February 1950. As of 2023, the Corporation governs 162 wards, each representing a distinct geographical and demographic segment of the city.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">A landmark moment in the Corporation’s history came in 1952, when Mr. Baburao Sanas was elected as the first Mayor of Pune. Since then, the PMC has witnessed a dynamic political environment, with leadership alternating among major parties such as the INC, BJP, and NCP. The formation of the NCP under Sharad Pawar has had a lasting influence on the city’s political landscape.</span></p>'),(26336,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The Pimpri-Chinchwad Municipal Corporation (PCMC) was established on 11 October 1982 to manage the growing industrial and urban population in the northern part of Pune district. By 2023, the Corporation had expanded to cover 128 wards.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Mr. Dnyaneshwar Pandurang Landge became the first Mayor of PCMC in 1986, marking a new phase in local governance and development. Since its inception, the NCP has been the dominant political force in Corporation elections. However, parties such as the BJP, INC, and SHS have also played significant roles, reflecting a competitive and diverse political landscape.</span></p>'),(26338,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Pune district has several municipal councils (Nagar Parishads) that serve as urban local governance bodies. These include Alandi Nagar Parishad, Bhor Nagar Parishad, Chakan Nagar Parishad, Baramati Nagar Parishad, Daund Nagar Parishad, Indapur Nagar Parishad, Jejuri Nagar Parishad, Lunnar Nagar Parishad, and Lonavala Nagar Parishad.</span></p>'),(26340,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Pune district is home to three cantonment boards that function as civic administration bodies in military areas. These include the Dehu Road Cantonment Board, Khadki Cantonment Board, and Pune Cantonment Board. </span></p>'),(26343,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 137 contestants from four seats. Out of these, 31 candidates had criminal cases against them, with 13 of the contestants having serious cases registered. The contestant with the highest number of criminal cases registered was the INC candidate from Pune. Out of the elected four MPs, the MPs of Pune and Maval both have three criminal cases against them.</span></p>'),(26345,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 237 contestants from 21 Vidhan Sabha seats. Out of these, 77 contestants had criminal cases against them, with 19% of the contestants having serious cases registered. Contestants with the highest number of criminal cases registered were from the parties MNS and NCP.</span></p>'),(26348,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti was founded in Pune at Jedhe Mansion by Keshavrao Jedhe to advocate for a separate state of Maharashtra for Marathi-speaking people. This movement brought together leaders from various political parties under one banner, with Pune serving as the central hub where strategies and plans were devised. The socio-political ideologies of Sant Dynaneshwar and Mahatma Phule, rooted in the Pune region, laid the foundation for the demand for a Marathi language state.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">On 6 February 1956, the Samiti was formally established at Jedhe Mansion, marking a significant alliance between Pune\'s Brahmin leaders and the Bahujan Samaj. Pune became a hotspot for political activity, with debates, rallies, and discussions held in prominent places like Kesariwada and Shaniwarwada. Leaders actively reached out to the masses, galvanizing public support for the movement. The atmosphere was so charged that Baburao Sanas, then Mayor of Pune, symbolically threw his Gandhi cap from the top of Vishrambaug Wada, the municipal headquarters, in protest against the Congress\'s stance.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Such was the influence of the Samiti\'s leadership that nearly every citizen in Pune became a supporter of the movement, joining the protests for a united Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26350,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In 1818, a significant battle took place in Bhima Koregaon, near Pune, involving the Peshwars (Marathas) and the Dalits. The Dalits, who belonged to a lower caste, sought equal participation in the kingdom\'s affairs, but the Peshwas discriminated against them. Exploiting this social conflict, the East India Company intervened, declaring war on the Peshwas. They assembled a Dalit army and emerged victorious. To honor the Dalits who fought for them, the East India Company erected the \'Vijay Stambh\' or Victory Pillar.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Every year on 1 January, Dalits commemorate this historic victory over the Marathas by paying tribute to these heroes at the Vijay Stambh. However, in 2018, during the 200th-anniversary celebration, a violent altercation erupted between Dalits and members of other castes, resulting in at least one fatality and multiple injuries.</span></p>'),(26352,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Rakesh Fulchand Dhakoliya, infamously known as “Mahakali,” was a feared gangster and one of Pune’s most notorious criminals. He earned the alias “Mahakali” because he invoked the name of the Goddess while placing his foot on a victim’s chest during extortion attempts. Operating primarily in the twin townships of Pimpri-Chinchwad and parts of Pune city, the Mahakali Gang was involved in armed robbery and extortion. His criminal career came to an end when he was killed in a police encounter.</span></p>'),(26354,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Gaja Marne, also known as Gajanan, is the leader of the Marne gang and a known history-sheeter involved in serious crimes such as murder and extortion. He has a long-standing rivalry with the Mohol gang. Marne was convicted of murder and served three years in Pune’s Yerawada Jail, along with an additional year in Nagpur Jail. He briefly joined the unified NCP in a meeting attended by Ajit Pawar, but was expelled following public backlash. His wife, Jayshree Marne, who was a corporator from Kothrud in Pune, later joined the NCP in February 2022.</span></p>'),(26356,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Sharad Mohol, also known as Bhau Mohol, began his criminal career alongside Sandeep Mohol. Both hailed from Pune’s Mulshi taluka in the Western Ghats and were drawn into crime at a young age. The Mohol gang later developed a notorious rivalry with the Marne gang. Western talukas like Mulshi, Bhor, and Velhe became breeding grounds for these gangs, often luring in misguided youth. Over time, gang violence shifted into Pune city, particularly Kothrud, resulting in a series of murders and retaliations during the 2000s.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Sharad Mohol gained notoriety for the jailhouse murder of suspected Indian Mujahideen operative Mohammad Qateel Siddiqui. Despite several prison stints, he rebranded himself as a social worker and grew popular in right-wing Hindutva circles, even receiving the “Hindu Shaurya Award” for his association with their cause. His wife, Swati Mohol, joined the BJP in the presence of Kothrud MLA and Pune Guardian Minister Chandrakant Patil - a move that attracted widespread criticism. Sharad supported her political entry and further cultivated his public image by funding education for underprivileged children and contributing to Ganpati mandals and other social initiatives. He was ultimately killed by a member of his own gang in January 2024.</span></p>'),(26358,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The gang members operating primarily in the Hadapsar suburb of Pune are known for vandalizing properties and instilling fear in their victims by wielding sickles (locally known as koytas in Marathi). These agricultural tools are commonly available, making it difficult for the police to track and apprehend the criminals. Most gang members are young, often juveniles, which allows them to secure bail easily and become repeat offenders. However, the Pune Police have taken strict measures to crack down on these gangs, significantly controlling the situation.</span></p>'),(26361,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Keshavrao Jedhe was a social activist, politician, freedom fighter, and follower of the Satyashodhak Samaj (Truth-Seekers Society) founded by Mahatma Phule. He was deeply involved in the Indian freedom struggle against British colonial rule. Jedhe was associated with the INC and was a close associate of leaders like Mahatma Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">He was a strong Maratha leader who played a pivotal role in the non-Brahmin movement in the region and served as the primary anchor of Jyotirao Phule’s Satyashodak Samaj in the early 20th century. Ideologically and politically, he was opposed to Tilak and similar orthodox traditionalists in Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">As a mark of protest against Tilak’s Ganpati festival, where non-Brahmins and educated women were often criticized, Jedhe organized the Chatrapati Mela. This event brought together young men from marginalized castes and non-Brahmin communities. Songs composed by Jedhe were popularly played throughout the Mela.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">During the British colonial era, Keshavrao Jedhe served as a member of the Bombay Legislative Council and later in the Bombay Legislative Assembly. After India gained independence in 1947, he continued his political career in the newly formed Republic of India. In the 1951 Lok Sabha elections, he was the candidate from the Peasants and Workers Party of India (PWP) but lost to Narhar Gadgil of the INC by a margin of over 60,000 votes.</span></p>'),(26363,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Pre-Independence Pune was home to several prominent socialist thinkers, among them Madhu Limaye (1922–1995), a fiery activist-politician and alumnus of Fergusson College. He actively participated in the Indian freedom movement through both the INC and the Congress Socialist Party. From 1940 to 1944, he endured a four-year prison sentence during the later phase of the national movement. Limaye was also imprisoned by the Portuguese in 1955 during the Goa Liberation Movement, along with fellow socialist N.G. Goray.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">A staunch follower of Ram Manohar Lohia and close associate of trade union leader George Fernandes, Limaye had deep socialist convictions. Though he hailed from Pune, he contested elections from Munger and Banka in Bihar, and represented both constituencies in Parliament. He played a crucial role in the JP Movement against Indira Gandhi’s Emergency regime and was imprisoned from 1975 to 1977. Upon release, he became the Janata Party’s general secretary during the Morarji Desai government (1977–79), a time marked by internal turbulence.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Limaye, along with Raj Narain and Krishan Kant, was instrumental in the fall of the Janata government. He took a principled stand against “dual membership,” opposing the simultaneous affiliation of Janata Party members like L.K. Advani and Atal Bihari Vajpayee with the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS), which he saw as incompatible with the party’s secular ideology. Never one to shy away from holding his own party accountable, Limaye famously took on Prime Minister Desai in Parliament over corruption allegations involving Desai’s son Kanti, earning him the title “Lion of Parliament.”</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">He retired from active politics in 1982 and devoted himself to writing, authoring over 60 books in English, Hindi, and Marathi. His major works include Goa Liberation Movement and Madhu Limaye, Manu, Gandhi, Ambedkar, and Other Essays, Janata Party Experiment (Parts I &amp; II), Madhu Limaye on Famous Personalities, and the four-volume series Mahatma Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru: A Historical Partnership.</span></p>'),(26365,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Popularly known as \"Kakasaheb Gadgil,\" Nahar Gadgil was a distinguished social activist, freedom fighter, accomplished writer, and a veteran Congress leader hailing from Pune. His life journey led him to become a prominent literary figure and a respected politician who drew inspiration from influential leaders like Mahatma Gandhi, Jawaharlal Nehru, Lokamanya Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Vallabhbhai Patel, as well as spiritual luminaries such as Swami Vivekananda and Swami Ramkrishna Paramhans.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the pre-Independence era of India, Gadgil held pivotal roles in the political landscape. He served as the secretary of the Poona District Congress Committee from 1921 to 1925 and assumed the presidency of the Maharashtra Pradesh Congress Committee from 1937 to 1945. Additionally, he played the roles of whip and secretary of the Congress Legislative Party from 1945 to 1947. Notably, he was entrusted with the responsibility of leading the Civil Disobedience Committee of Maharashtra state in 1930, demonstrating his unwavering commitment to the freedom struggle. In 1934, he was elected to the central Legislative Assembly, further solidifying his presence on the national political stage. His residence, known as Gadgil Wada, served as a hub for political discussions and planning. It was within these walls that crucial discussions regarding the reconstitution of the socialist Rashtra Seva Dal took place in the early 1940s, with Kakasaheb playing a prominent role alongside socialists like S.M. Joshi, N.G. Gore, Shirubhau Limaye, among others.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Following India\'s Independence, Kakasaheb Gadgil made significant contributions to the nation\'s governance. He became the first Member of Parliament from the Pune Lok Sabha seat, representing the Indian National Congress. His dedication and expertise led him to become a part of the first cabinet, serving as the Minister of Power under the leadership of Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru in 1952. Subsequently, he assumed the role of the 3rd Governor of Punjab, serving from 1958 to 1962.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In addition to his political career, Kakasaheb Gadgil distinguished himself as a prolific writer. He authored several influential books, including \"Pathik,\" \"Subha Shastra,\" \"Vaktrutva Shastra,\" \"Rajya Shastra Vichar,\" and \"Gyanbacha Arthashastra.\" Through his writings, he contributed to the intellectual and literary landscape of India, leaving a lasting legacy as both a thinker and a statesman.</span></p>'),(26367,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Shreedhar Mahadev Joshi was a key figure in India’s socialist movement, holding prominent positions such as chairman of the Praja Socialist Party (PSP) and the Samyukta Socialist Party (SSP). He was also among the founding members of the Janata Party.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">An alumnus of Pune’s New English School and Fergusson College, Joshi displayed early talent in elocution and literary writing. Influenced by Lokmanya Tilak’s philosophy, he entered politics by forming a students’ organization in Pune and organizing the Youth League Conference in Mumbai. He was actively involved in the Parvati Satyagraha, a movement demanding Dalit entry into the Parvati Mandir in Pune, challenging Brahminical dominance. In this effort, he worked alongside prominent leaders such as B.R. Ambedkar, N.V. Gadgil, Dinkarrao Jawalkar (Jedhe), and Rajbhoj.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Joshi joined the Civil Disobedience Movement in 1930 and participated in the Salt Satyagraha in Alibaug, leading to his first imprisonment, a moment he described as becoming a “full citizen.” In 1934, he was again arrested for making a passionate speech demanding the release of Marxist revolutionary M.N. Roy - an incident later referred to as “Roy’s Day.” He was charged with sedition. That same year, he became one of the founding members of the Congress Socialist Party (CSP) and served as secretary of the Muslim Mass Contact Committee.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">During the Quit India Movement in 1942, Joshi was jailed for three years. After his release, he became an active leader of the Rashtriya Seva Dal, an organization founded by Sane Guruji, Shirubhau Limaye, and N.G. Goray to counteract communal forces and promote secular, democratic ideals.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">After Independence, Joshi played a significant role in the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement. As General Secretary of the Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti, established in 1956, he worked alongside President Shripad Amrit Dange and Vice President Dr. T.R. Naravane to campaign for a separate Marathi-speaking state. He was elected to the Bombay Legislative Assembly from the Shukrawar Peth constituency (which fell under the Poona Lok Sabha seat) and later became the Member of Parliament for Pune in 1967.</span></p>'),(26369,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Ajit Pawar, affectionately known as \"Dada\" in Maharashtra, embarked on his political journey in 1982 as a member of the board of a cooperative sugar factory. His rise in politics has been remarkable, marked by significant roles and accomplishments. He assumed the position of Chairman of the Pune District Cooperative Bank in 1991 and held this post for an impressive 16 years. Ajit Pawar also served as a Member of Parliament (MP) in the Lok Sabha from Baramati in 1991, later vacating the seat for his uncle, Sharad Pawar, who was the Defence Minister in the PV Narasimha Rao government. Notably, he is a nephew of NCP stalwart Sharad Pawar.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Subsequently, he became a Member of the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly (MLA) from the Baramati Assembly Constituency. His electoral success continued as he was elected as an MLA from Baramati in 1995, 1999, 2004, 2009, 2014, and 2019, the last time with a substantial vote margin of 1,65,265 votes. Ajit Pawar\'s influence and responsibilities grew as he was elevated to the Cabinet minister rank in Maharashtra at the age of 40 in 1999. He took charge of key ministries such as Irrigation, Rural Development, Water Resources, and Finance, solidifying his presence and power across the state.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In November 2019, he made headlines by briefly serving as the Deputy Chief Minister in a government led by the BJP\'s Devendra Fadnavis, an administration that lasted just 80 hours. He later resumed the role of Deputy Chief Minister in the MVA government led by Uddhav Thackeray, remaining in the position for two-and-a-half years until the alliance government faced a collapse in June the following year.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Ajit Pawar\'s political career has seen various twists and turns, including his tenure as Deputy CM during the 15-year rule of the Congress-NCP government under Ashok Chavan and Prithviraj Chavan.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Notably, in a surprising turn of events in 2022, Ajit Pawar decided to split from the NCP and joined the government formed by SHS and BJP as the Deputy Chief Minister of Maharashtra. This move occurred even as his uncle, Sharad Pawar, was engaged in uniting opposition parties to challenge the BJP.</span></p>'),(26371,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Girish Bapat was a veteran leader of the BJP known for his inclusive and consensus-driven approach to politics. Widely respected across party lines, he was often described as a politician without adversaries.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">His political journey began with the BJP Yuva Morcha in 1980. In 1983, he won a by-election to the Pune Municipal Corporation (PMC), marking the start of three consecutive terms as a corporator. Bapat was elected as MLA from the Kasba Assembly constituency in 1995 and retained the seat for five consecutive terms. During his legislative tenure, he held key ministerial portfolios including Parliamentary Affairs and Food &amp; Civil Supplies.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In 2019, Bapat was elected as the Member of Parliament from the Pune Lok Sabha constituency. His career reflected his deep roots in both local governance and state-level administration. He remained active in public service until his passing in 2023.</span></p>'),(26373,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Indirabai Anant Maydeo, a senior Congress leader and Gandhian, played a prominent role in both the freedom movement and post-Independence politics. A graduate of Fergusson College, she was associated with the Maharashtra Provincial Harijan Sevak Sangh in the 1930s and the All-India Women’s Conference (AIWC), where she served as General Secretary in 1946.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">She was elected to the Lok Sabha from Pune South in 1951, alongside N.V. Gadgil from Pune Central, and became the most prominent Congresswoman in the district. As one of the few women MPs at the time, she consistently introduced legislation from a gender-sensitive perspective - calling for reserved railway compartments for women, liberal divorce laws, and stricter action against trafficking.</span></p>'),(26375,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Madhuri Misal is a three-term BJP MLA from the Parvati constituency (2009, 2014, 2019) and a former corporator representing the Mahatma Phule ward. She heads the Satish Misal Charitable Trust, is Director of Udyam Vikas Sahakari Bank, and currently serves on the Maharashtra Public Accounts Committee.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Misal\'s work focuses on infrastructure and rehabilitation, including the upliftment of those displaced by the Panshet floods. She has advocated for reliable electricity supply, sanitation improvements, and sustainable solid waste management. Her emphasis on urban greenery has led to the development of several recreational parks. She is the granddaughter of freedom fighter Keshavrao Deshpande.</span></p>'),(26377,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Dr. Medha Kulkarni is a BJP leader who served as MLA from Kothrud (2014–2019) and is currently a Rajya Sabha MP. An academic by training, she earned her doctorate from Savitribai Phule Pune University and taught at Seva Sadan D.Ed College. She has maintained long-standing affiliations with the RSS and serves as national vice-president of the BJP Mahila Morcha.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Kulkarni entered public life as a corporator in 2002, encouraged by senior BJP leader Anil Shirole. She was elected MLA in 2014, but was denied a ticket in 2019. Afterward, she publicly criticized the Pune BJP\'s development agenda— - particularly on hill encroachments - and campaigned for transparency in credit attribution for civic projects.</span></p>'),(26379,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Mukta Tilak, BJP leader and former Mayor of Pune (2017–2019), was known for her resilience, especially during the 2019 Sahakar Nagar flash floods, which she managed while undergoing cancer treatment. She later served as an MLA and was widely admired for her commitment to public service.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Tilak initiated civic-focused campaigns such as \"Save Pune Traffic\" and annual cancer screening for 10,000 women. Her \"Daughters To Be\" project encouraged leadership among schoolgirls by allowing them to shadow corporators. She also launched female literacy classes in slums and welfare programs for sex workers in Pune’s red-light district.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">A descendant of a prominent legacy, she was married to Shailesh Tilak, the great-grandson of Lokmanya Tilak. Mukta Tilak passed away in 2022 after a four-year battle with cancer.</span></p>'),(26381,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Sharad Pawar, born into a middle-class agricultural family in Baramati, began his political journey in Pune, where he earned a degree in commerce and became active in student politics. In 1964, he was appointed President of the Youth Congress, beginning his political career under the mentorship of Yashwantrao Chavan. Three years later, he won his first election to the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from Baramati.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In 1978, after breaking from the Congress, Pawar co-founded the Indian National Congress (Socialist), known as Congress (S), and became Maharashtra’s youngest Chief Minister. His government was dismissed in 1980 when Indira Gandhi returned to power. Pawar later merged Congress (S) with the Indian National Congress in 1986 to counter the rise of SHS. He served three additional terms as Maharashtra’s Chief Minister (1988–91, 1993–95).</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">At the national level, Pawar was elected to the Lok Sabha in 1991 and served as India’s Defence Minister under Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao. In 1999, following internal differences over Sonia Gandhi’s leadership, Pawar co-founded the NCP. While opposing her foreign origin, the NCP still aligned with Congress in Maharashtra and at the Centre.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">A political strategist with an unmatched memory and coalition-building ability, Pawar is regarded as a leader who has shaped both state and national politics for over five decades. At 83, he remains active, commanding respect across the political spectrum.</span></p>'),(26383,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Born in 1925, Shanta Ranade pursued her education at SNDT College in Pune and was deeply influenced by Marxist ideology, becoming actively involved in the Indian freedom movement. She earned an MA in Russian from Pune University and later completed a course at Moscow University. Ranade translated the Russian novel Sagaracha Janam by Konstantin Paustovsky, for which she received the prestigious Soviet Land Nehru Award.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">A regular contributor to Yugantar, the revolutionary newspaper published by the Bhartiya Communist Party, Ranade also worked with the publishing house Pragatik Pustak Prakashan. She was an influential figure in the Communist Party of India (CPI), serving on its national council and as president of the Maharashtra state unit of the National Federation of Indian Women. Her activism led to multiple imprisonments during her lifetime.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Remarkably, even at the age of 93 in 2018, Ranade remained an ardent activist, participating in protests against virginity tests for brides in the Kanjarbhat community.</span></p>'),(26385,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Supriya Sule, daughter of NCP founder Sharad Pawar, is a three-term Member of Parliament from the Baramati constituency. She entered national politics in 2006 as a Rajya Sabha MP from Maharashtra and currently plays a central role in the party’s leadership.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In 2012, Sule launched the Rashtravadi Yuvati Congress to encourage young women’s political participation. A consistently active parliamentarian, she has introduced 16 private member bills, raised over 580 questions, and participated in more than 220 debates. Her performance has earned her multiple accolades for parliamentary excellence.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Her political journey has not been without controversy. She faced scrutiny over alleged links to financial irregularities in the 2010 IPL. However, her legislative focus, particularly on education, women\'s rights, and health, has kept her at the forefront of national politics.</span></p>'),(26387,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Suresh Kalmadi, son of noted Kannada social worker Dr. Shamarao Kalmadi, began his career as an Indian Air Force pilot, serving in both the 1965 Indo-Pak war and the 1971 Bangladesh Liberation War. He entered politics in the late 1970s, mentored by Sanjay Gandhi, and rose to become Maharashtra Youth Congress President.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Kalmadi served multiple terms in both houses of Parliament, including as Minister of State for Railways (1995–96). He was closely associated with the promotion of sports in Pune and is credited with founding the Pune International Marathon. In 2008, he organized the Commonwealth Youth Games in Pune.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Elected as MP from Pune in 1996 and 2004, he was known as the “CEO of Pune” due to his infrastructural influence. However, his legacy was marred by his arrest and subsequent downfall over corruption charges in connection with the 2010 Commonwealth Games.</span></p>'),(26389,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Advocate Vandana Chavan, a trained lawyer from a prominent legal family, began her political career with three terms as a corporator and later became Pune’s Mayor in 1997–98. She is best known for integrating the concept of Biodiversity Parks (BDP) into the city’s development plan, safeguarding Pune’s hills and green spaces.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span>Originally mentored by Suresh Kalmadi, Chavan later joined the NCP under Sharad Pawar. She served as a Rajya Sabha MP from 2012 to 2024 and was awarded </span><span class=\"c36\">Lokmat</span><span class=\"c2\">\'s Best Woman Parliamentarian of the Year in 2023.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Chavan remains an outspoken voice on urban governance, environmental protection, and public transport in Pune. Her family includes Justice Revati Mohite-Dere of the Bombay High Court and the late IPS officer Ashok Kamte, a 26/11 martyr.</span></p>'),(26410,'<p class=\"c41 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c47\">LOCAL POLITICS: RAIGAD</span></p>'),(26412,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad District is situated in the Konkan region of Maharashtra, India. It derives its name and historical significance from Raigad Fort, the former capital of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and a key site during the rise of the Maratha Empire. Historically known as Kolaba, the district is now administratively centered in Alibag, a coastal city governed by a municipal council. Known for its natural beauty and historical landmarks, Alibag and its surroundings attract both tourists and history enthusiasts.</span></p>'),(26415,'<p class=\"c8\"><span>The Raigad district consists of the Raigad Lok Sabha constituency and some Vidhan Sabha segments of the Maval Lok Sabha constituency. Only Dapoli and Guhagar among the Vidhan Sabha segments of Raigad Lok Sabha constituency are situated outside the Raigad district. The other segments of the Maval Lok Sabha are located in Pune.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c35\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Vidhan Sabha Constituencies</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Alibag</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mahad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Pen</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Shrivardhan</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Dapoli</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Guhagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Karjat</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Maval</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Panvel</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Maval</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Uran</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Maval</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Raigad District is represented by Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Konkan Teachers’ Constituency, and the Konkan Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">This chapter focuses on the Raigad Lok Sabha constituency and the Vidhan Sabha segments that comprise it. For the other Vidhan Sabha constituencies that fall in the district, refer to the chapter on Pune. The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Raigad district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26418,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The Raigad Lok Sabha constituency was previously known as the Colaba Lok Sabha constituency. Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, the given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Lok Sabha constituencies of the district and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(26421,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the current Members of Parliament (MP) representing Raigad district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c35\"><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c6\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c6\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c6\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c20\"><span class=\"c1\">Sunil Dattatray Tatkare </span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c20\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad </span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c20\"><span class=\"c1\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c20\"><span class=\"c1\">Shrirang Chandu Barne</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c20\"><span class=\"c1\">Maval</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c20\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26423,'<p class=\"c8\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Raigad district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c35\"><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Party</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Ravisheth Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Pen</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mahendra Dalvi</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Alibagh </span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Aditi Tatkare</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Shrivardhan</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">NCP</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatshet Gogawale</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mahad </span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Yogesh Kadam</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Dapoli</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhaskar Jadhav</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Guhagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena - Uddhav Balasaheb Thackeray (SHS-UBT)</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Prashant Thakur</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Panvel</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mahendra Thorve</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Karjat</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c15\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mahesh Baldi</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Uran</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26425,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">There is no member from the Raigad district currently in the Rajya Sabha representing Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26427,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Aditi Tatkare (NCP) is the current Guardian Minister for Raigad district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26429,'<p class=\"c8\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Raigad district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Konkan Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Dnyaneshwar Mhatre</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Konkan Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Niranjan Davkhare</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26431,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the key administrative heads of Raigad district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c16\"><span class=\"c6\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Kishan Narayanrao Jawale</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Municipal Commissioner, Panvel</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mangesh Sharmila Pandurang Chitale</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Dr. B N Bastewad</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Yogita Pardhi</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena (SHS-SKP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c39\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Somnath Gharge</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Police Services (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26434,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Raigad district.</span></p>'),(26436,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">There are 17 Urban Local Bodies in the Raigad District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 10 Nagar Parishad, 6 Nagar Panchayat. There are 704 Gram Panchayats.</span></p>'),(26438,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The Raigad Zilla Parishad, which was established in 1962, plays a vital role in the administration of the Raigad District. It consists of a total of 59 seats.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Notably, Prabhakar Patil, one of the former Presidents of the Zila Parishad, is recognized as the chief architect of Zila Parishad Raigad. His significant contribution was instrumental in centralizing and transferring all the accounts of the Zila Parishad, which were previously scattered in Pen taluka, to the Raigad Zila Parishad in 1978. This consolidation of accounts likely streamlined the administrative and financial functions of the Zila Parishad, contributing to its effectiveness in serving the district.</span></p>'),(26440,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Raigad district has several municipal councils (Nagar Parishads) that serve as urban local governance bodies. These include Alibag Nagar Parishad, Khopoli Nagar Parishad, Karjat Nagar Parishad, Mahad Nagar Parishad, Matheran Nagar Parishad, Mulud Nagar Parishad, Pen Nagar Parishad, Roha-Ashtami Nagar Parishad, Shrivardhan Nagar Parishad, and Uran Nagar Parishad.</span></p>'),(26442,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The district also has two Nagar Panchayats or town councils which function as local administrative units in semi-urban areas. These are the Motala Nagar Panchayat and the Sangrampur Nagar Panchayat.</span></p>'),(26445,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 13 contestants from Raigad, of which two candidates had criminal cases against them. The elected MP, Sunil Tatkare, has no criminal cases against him and belongs to the Nationalist Congress Party.</span></p>'),(26448,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The Mahad Satyagraha, also known as the Chavdar Tale Satyagraha, was a landmark protest led by Dr. B. R. Ambedkar on 20 March 1927. At the time, Dalits were subjected to widespread social discrimination under the caste system. They were denied access to public resources such as water bodies and roads that were used by other Hindu castes.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">In August 1923, the Bombay Legislative Council passed a resolution stating that members of the depressed classes should be allowed to use public spaces maintained by the government. Following this, in January 1924, the Mahad Municipal Council in the Bombay Province passed a resolution to implement this decision locally. However, its implementation was stalled due to opposition from dominant caste groups.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1927, Dr. Ambedkar launched a nonviolent satyagraha to assert the rights of Dalits to access public water sources. The chosen site was Mahad, a town in the Konkan region, where support had emerged from progressive members of dominant caste communities. Key allies included A. V. Chitre from the Chandraseniya Kayastha Prabhu (CKP) community, G. N. Sahasrabudhe, a Chitpawan Brahmin affiliated with the Social Service League, and Surendranath Tipnis, a CKP and president of the Mahad municipality.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Tipnis formally declared that public spaces in Mahad were open to Dalits and invited Ambedkar to hold a public meeting there in March 1927. After the meeting, Ambedkar and thousands of supporters marched to the Chavdar tank, a public water tank, where he drank water - an act of profound defiance. Thousands of Dalits followed him, symbolically asserting their right to equal access.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">During the satyagraha, Ambedkar also addressed Dalit women, encouraging them to abandon customs that visibly marked them as subordinate and to wear saris in the same manner as women from dominant castes. At the time, Dalit women were socially prohibited from fully draping their saris. After Ambedkar’s appeal, many women began adopting the full drape. They were assisted by progressive upper-caste women such as Lakshmibai Tipnis and Indirabai Chitre, who helped them drape their saris down to the ankles, challenging entrenched norms of caste-based dress codes.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">The Mahad Satyagraha marked a turning point in the anti-caste movement and is commemorated annually on 20 March as Social Empowerment Day in India.</span></p>'),(26450,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">In July 2018, the Marathi Kranti Morcha called for a bandh in Mumbai, Navi Mumbai, and Panvel to demand reservations for the Maratha community in education and government jobs. While the demonstrations began as peaceful, they escalated into violence in several areas, particularly in Navi Mumbai and Thane.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Protesters blocked key routes such as the Sion-Panvel Highway, leading to massive traffic disruptions and a halt in public movement. The police responded with crowd-control measures including lathi charges, rubber bullets, and tear gas. The situation resulted in injuries to several individuals, including police personnel, and one person lost his life due to injuries sustained during the agitation.</span></p>'),(26452,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Keshav Sitaram Thackeray, popularly known as Prabodhankar Thackeray, was born on 17 September 1885 in Panvel. He was a prominent Indian social reformer, writer, and political activist. A vocal critic of social evils, he campaigned against superstitions, untouchability, child marriage, and the dowry system. He was also a prolific writer, using his literary work as a medium for reform and awareness.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Thackeray played a significant role in the Samyukta Maharashtra movement, which aimed to create a unified Marathi-speaking state. He joined the movement in 1951 and was a strong advocate for including the Dang district in Maharashtra rather than Gujarat. As a founding member of the Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti, he actively campaigned for the inclusion of Mumbai and Belgaum in the new state of Maharashtra. He was also the father of Bal Thackeray, founder of the Shiv Sena. Prabodhankar Thackeray passed away on 20 November 1973 in Mumbai.</span></p>'),(26455,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Abdul Rehman Antulay was a prominent figure in Maharashtra politics and holds the distinction of being the state’s first and only Muslim Chief Minister. Born in Raigad district, he was a trained barrister educated at Lincoln’s Inn in London and was closely associated with Sanjay Gandhi. He served as Chief Minister from June 1980 to January 1982, after the Congress (I) won the state elections following Indira Gandhi’s return to power at the Centre. His appointment was widely seen as an attempt to challenge the Maratha-dominated leadership structure within the state Congress.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">As Chief Minister, Antulay undertook administrative reforms, including the bifurcation of large districts for more efficient governance and initiating changes in the public works and irrigation departments. However, his tenure was marred by controversy. In what became a landmark case of investigative journalism in India, journalist Arun Shourie exposed that Antulay had allegedly used his position to collect donations from builders for private trusts in return for cement allocations—then a scarce commodity under the Licence Raj. Though he resigned and was briefly imprisoned, the Supreme Court later overturned his conviction on technical grounds, acquitting him of all charges.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Antulay remained active in national politics. He was elected to the Lok Sabha in the 9th, 11th, and 14th terms. During the United Progressive Alliance (UPA-I) government, he served as Minister of Minority Affairs from 2006 to 2009. He courted fresh controversy in the aftermath of the 2008 Mumbai attacks by suggesting that the death of ATS chief Hemant Karkare be probed, which drew criticism across political lines. Despite these controversies, his career spanned decades, and he left a significant, if debated, legacy in Maharashtra’s political landscape.</span></p>'),(26457,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Anant Gite is a veteran Shiv Sena leader from Ratnagiri district, known for his long-standing association with the party and commitment to the Konkan region. He began his political career as a municipal councilor in Mumbai in 1985 and rose through the ranks during the SHS-BJP alliance in the 1990s. In 1995, he served as the Chairman of the Konkan Irrigation Development Corporation.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Geete was elected to the Lok Sabha for six consecutive terms, initially from Ratnagiri and later from Raigad after delimitation in 2008. He became the first MP to represent the newly formed Raigad constituency, defeating incumbent A. R. Antulay in the 2009 general elections. Within the SHS, he served as the party’s chief whip in Parliament and played a key role in consolidating its presence in the coastal Konkan belt.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">He held ministerial positions in the central government during both National Democratic Alliances (NDA), NDA-I and NDA-II. Between 2014 and 2019, he served as Union Minister for Heavy Industries and Public Enterprises in the Narendra Modi-led cabinet. In 2018, he was formally appointed as the SHS’ parliamentary party leader.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Geete made headlines in 2019 when he resigned from the Union Cabinet amid deteriorating relations between the SHS and the BJP, particularly over seat-sharing arrangements for the Maharashtra Assembly elections. His resignation marked a turning point in the long-standing alliance between the two parties and was a symbolic gesture of SHS asserting its independence in state politics.</span></p>'),(26459,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Meenakshi Patil was a senior leader of the Peasants and Workers Party of India (PWP) and a prominent political figure in the Konkan region. She was elected three times to the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from the Alibaug constituency and also served three terms in the Maharashtra Legislative Council - 1995 to 1999, 1999 to 2004, and 2009 to 2014.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">During her tenure, she held the position of Minister of State for Fisheries in the Vilasrao Deshmukh-led government in 1999. As a representative of a regional party rooted in agrarian and working-class politics, Patil was known for her consistent focus on the welfare of rural communities and the coastal economy.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">She passed away on 29 March, 2024, marking the end of a long and dedicated career in Maharashtra’s public life.</span></p>'),(26461,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Aditi Tatkare, daughter of senior NCP leader Sunil Tatkare, represents the Shrivardhan Assembly constituency in Raigad district. She began her political career in local governance, serving as President of the Raigad Zilla Parishad from 2017 to 2019. In 2019, she was inducted as a minister in the Maharashtra government, handling portfolios including tourism, information and public relations, law and judiciary, food and civil supplies, energy, and finance. A key member of the Tatkare political family, she has emerged as a prominent face of the NCP in the Konkan region.</span></p>'),(26463,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Anil Dattatray Tatkare, a senior NCP (Sharadchandra Pawar) leader and part of the Tatkare political family, served as a member of the Maharashtra Legislative Council from 2010 to 2018, representing the Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Authorities constituency. He has also held local positions, including membership in the Raigad Zilla Parishad. On 20 February, 2024, he was appointed as the state vice-president of the NCP (Sharadchandra Pawar).</span></p>'),(26465,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Avadhut Tatkare, son of former MLC Anil Tatkare and nephew of Sunil Tatkare, is a second-generation politician from the Tatkare family. He served as MLA from Shrivardhan from 2014 to 2019. Prior to that, he was elected to the Roha Municipal Council in 2006, serving as Deputy Mayor and later as Mayor from 2008 to 2014. Elected to the Assembly on an NCP ticket, he resigned in 2019 and joined the BJP.</span></p>'),(26467,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Sunil Tatkare is a senior leader of the NCP and one of its most prominent Other Backward Class (OBC) faces in Maharashtra. Beginning his career as a government contractor, he joined the Congress in 1984 and became an MLA in 1995. He represented Mangaon (1995–2009) and later Shrivardhan (2009–2014) in the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly. During this period, he held key portfolios including Energy, Finance, Water Resources, Urban Development, and Panchayat Raj. In 2019, he won the Raigad Lok Sabha seat, defeating SHS veteran Anant Gite - a victory he repeated in 2024. Tatkare played a key role in the NCP’s 2022 alliance with the BJP and Shinde faction of the SHS, after which he was appointed State President of the NCP. He is the patriarch of the Tatkare political family, which remains highly influential in Raigad district politics.</span></p>'),(26469,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Ramsheth Thakur, born on 2 May 1951, is a senior political leader from Raigad district who began his career with the PWP. He later joined the INC and was elected to the Lok Sabha from the Kolaba constituency, serving two terms from 1998 to 2004. He is now affiliated with the BJP.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Apart from his political career, Ramsheth Thakur is known for his contributions to education and public welfare. In 1992, he founded the Janardan Bhagat Shikshan Prasarak Sanstha, which now runs nearly 19 educational institutions across Panvel and Navi Mumbai - ranging from schools and colleges to professional and technical institutes. He is widely respected for his work in improving educational access and infrastructure development in Raigad. He is the father of Prashant Thakur, a three-time BJP MLA from Panvel.</span></p>'),(26471,'<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">Born on 5 August 1974 in Panvel to Ramsheth Thakur and Shakuntala Thakur, Prashant Thakur is a three-time Member of the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from Panvel. He began his political career with the PWP but later joined the INC. In 2006, he was elected to the Panvel Municipal Council, and in 2009, he won his first assembly seat on a Congress ticket.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2014, Thakur joined the BJP and was re-elected to the state assembly in both 2014 and 2019. He has served in various leadership roles, including as Chairman of CIDCO and President of the Raigad District Youth Congress Committee. He is also involved in educational initiatives as a board member of the Janardan Bhagat Shikshan Prasarak Sanstha and as Managing Director of the Shahu Institute of Information Technology Pvt. Ltd.</span></p>'),(26477,'<p class=\"c39 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span>LOCAL POLITICS: RATNAGIRI</span></p>'),(26479,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Following the defeat of the Peshwas in the Third Anglo-Maratha War in 1818, Ratnagiri became part of the Bombay Presidency. In 1819, the region known as South Konkan, which includes present-day Ratnagiri and Sindhudurg districts, was established as a separate district. Its headquarters were initially located at Bankot but were later shifted to Ratnagiri.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In 1830, three northern subdivisions were transferred to Thane district, and Ratnagiri was reduced to a sub-collectorate under Thane. However, this status was short-lived. By 1832, Ratnagiri was once again recognized as a full-fledged district.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Subsequent administrative changes continued to shape the district. In 1945, a new taluka called Kankavli was created. By 1949, the former princely state of Sawantwadi was merged with Ratnagiri, leading to the reorganization of taluka boundaries and the formation of Sawantwadi, Kudal, and Lanja talukas. After the reorganization of states in 1956, Ratnagiri was incorporated into Bombay State and in 1960, it became part of the state of Maharashtra. Finally, in 1981, Ratnagiri was separated from Sindhudurg to ensure better administrative management and to support the region’s industrial and agricultural development.</span></p>'),(26482,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The Ratnagiri district falls under the joint Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Lok Sabha, which comprises the following six Vidhan Sabha segments:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sawantwadi</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sindhudurg</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Kudal</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sindhudurg</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Kankavli</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sindhudurg</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Chiplun</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Rajapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span>In </span><span class=\"c0\">the Vidhan Parishad, the Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Konkan Teachers’ Constituency, and the Konkan Graduates Constituency represent the region. The Graduates\' and Teachers’ Constituencies are structured according to the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The maps given below provide an overview of the boundaries of the Ratnagiri district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26485,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Lok Sabha constituency was created during the most recent round of delimitation. Before this, Ratnagiri district was represented by two separate constituencies: Ratnagiri and Rajapur.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, the given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Ratnagiri district and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(26488,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Ratnagiri district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c37\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c45\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c37\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Narayan Tatu Rane</span></p></td><td class=\"c45\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26491,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Ratnagiri district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Shekhar Nikam</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Chiplun</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Uday Samant</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Kiran Samant</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Rajapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Nitesh Rane</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Kankawli</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Nilesh Rane</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Kudal</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Deepak Kesarkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sawantwadi</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Bhaskar Jadhav</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Guhagar</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Shiv Sena (Uddhav Balasaheb Thackerey (SS (UBT))</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Yogeshdada Ramdas Kadam</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dapoli</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26494,'<p class=\"c22\"><span class=\"c0\">There is no member from Ratnagiri district currently in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(26496,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The following is the current Guardian Minister for Ratnagiri district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Guardian Minister</span></p></td><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Uday Samant </span></p></td><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26498,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Ratnagiri district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c37\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Vidhan Parishad  Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c48\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c37\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Konkan Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dnyaneshwar Mhatre</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c37\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Konkan  Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c30\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Niranjan Davkhare</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26500,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The following are the key administrative heads of Ratnagiri district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c10\"><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c32\"><span class=\"c14\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">M. Devender Singh</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">–</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">–</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad, Ratnagiri</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Kirthi Kiran Pujar</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">President of Zilla Parishad, Ratnagiri</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Rohan Bane</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c29\"><td class=\"c36\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Superintendent of Police, Ratnagiri District</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dhananjay Kulkarni</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Indian Police Services (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26503,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The following graphs present key variables in Ratnagiri district’s Lok Sabha constituencies, including party-wise distribution, voter turnout, number of candidates, and winning margins.</span></p>'),(26505,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Ratnagiri district has a total of 9 Urban Local Bodies, comprising 1 Mahanagar Palika, 7 Nagar Parishads, and 1 Nagar Panchayat. In addition, there are 704 Gram Panchayats across the district. These local bodies play a key role in managing basic infrastructure, public services, and development programmes at the city, town, and village levels. Their functioning reflects the diverse administrative needs of both urban and rural centres. </span></p>'),(26507,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The Zilla Parishad of Ratnagiri was established in 1962, with official operations commencing on 1 May 1962. Serving as the apex body of rural local governance in the district, it oversees development planning, infrastructure, education, and health services across rural areas. The council comprises a total of 62 elected seats, representing various constituencies within the district.</span></p>'),(26509,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">The Ratnagiri Municipality was established in 1876, making it one of the older municipal institutions in the region. Over the years, it has evolved to manage the civic administration and urban infrastructure of Ratnagiri town. The municipality is responsible for services such as water supply, sanitation, roads, public health, and local governance in the urban area.</span></p>'),(26512,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sitaram Patwardhan, born on 4 November, 1894, in the village of Agargule in Ratnagiri taluka (near Ganpatipule), was a renowned freedom fighter and social reformer, fondly known as \"Koknache Gandhi\" (Gandhi of Konkan). A devoted follower of Mahatma Gandhi, Patwardhan pursued his early education in Ratnagiri before continuing his studies in Pune, where he also briefly worked at Fergusson College.</span></p>'),(26514,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In 1916, he had the opportunity to meet Gandhi during the Bombay session of the Indian National Congress, an encounter that deeply inspired him. Motivated by Gandhi\'s ideals, Patwardhan resigned from his job at Fergusson College and joined the Sabarmati Ashram in 1919. There, he was entrusted by Gandhi with the responsibility of publishing the weekly journal Young India.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Patwardhan\'s commitment to the freedom struggle led to his imprisonment for three and a half years due to his participation in the Satyagraha movement. After his release, he returned to Konkan, where he focused on uplifting the marginalized Kunbi community by establishing the \'Kunbi Seva Sangh.\' His efforts helped the Indian National Congress gain a strong foothold in the region, and he served as the district president of the party.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In addition to his political work, Patwardhan founded the \'Gopuri Ashram\' in Kankavli (Sindhudurg), where he championed social equality. He broke caste barriers by engaging in manual scavenging himself and producing manure (Sonkhat) at the ashram. Defying societal norms, he and his upper-caste colleagues even skinned dead animals to make leather, a task traditionally restricted to the Mahar caste. His ashram became a symbol of unity, where people from 20-25 different castes lived together harmoniously.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In 1952, Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru visited Patwardhan’s Gopuri Ashram and recognized his efforts. Later, Patwardhan worked alongside Vinoba Bhave in the Bhoodan movement, advocating for land redistribution.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sitaram Patwardhan passed away on March 10, 1971, leaving behind a legacy of social justice and reform. His life embodied the Gandhian principles of equality, selflessness, and service to the downtrodden.</span></p>'),(26516,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Burondi Village, located 10 km from Dapoli, experienced its first recorded communal incident in 1986 when tensions flared between Muslim and Hindu residents over a marriage procession that was deemed too loud during Namaz. Since then, the village has witnessed occasional disturbances, particularly during festival seasons. In 2013, individuals from both communities, with assistance from the local police, formed a Samanvay Samiti (Coordination Committee) to address these issues. The committee successfully resolved various petty conflicts, and in 2015, they signed a symbolic 100-year peace treaty that outlined basic guidelines for festival processions. Since then, incidents of animosity have largely faded, and no further conflicts have been recorded.</span></p>'),(26518,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In larger towns like Chiplun, the most recent significant Hindu-Muslim tension occurred in 2014 after a fake message was circulated about Shivaji Maharaj. In December 2022, Hindu nationalist groups organized protests against “Love Jihad”, but these gatherings saw low turnout and did not escalate. Despite the occasional flare-ups, Hindus and Muslims in Chiplun and surrounding areas have lived in relative peace for many years.</span></p>'),(26521,'<p class=\"c1\"><span>Born on 24 August 1888 in Ratnagiri to a middle-class Karhade Brahmin family, Balasaheb Gangadhar (B.G.) Khare served as the Prime Minister of Bombay State during two terms (1937-1939 and 1946-1947) and later became the first Chief Minister of Bombay State (1947-1952). He received his education at New English School in Pune and Wilson College in Mumbai. A key figure in the Indian freedom struggle, Kher spent four years and eight months in jail for his participation in the Civil Disobedience Movement and the Quit India Movement. He played a pivotal role in founding the Savitribai Phule University in Pune. Known for his integrity and statesmanship, Kher was described as a \"Sajjan\" and was honored with the Padma Vibhushan in 1954. He passed away on 8 March 1957 in Pune.</span></p>'),(26523,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Ganesh Vasudev Mavalankar, originally from Mavalange village in Sangameshwar taluka, was a prominent freedom fighter and a senior leader of the Indian National Congress. He served as the Speaker of the Constituent Assembly and later became the first Speaker of the Lok Sabha. Although he spent only a few years of his early life in Rajapur, he later moved to Ahmedabad to pursue a degree in law. There, he came into close contact with leaders such as Sardar Patel and Mahatma Gandhi, which deeply influenced the course of his political journey.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Mavalankar also played a pivotal role in founding the Ahmedabad Education Society, a key institution in the field of higher education. His legacy continued through his son, Purushottam Mavalankar, who was elected to the Lok Sabha from Ahmedabad in 1972.</span></p>'),(26525,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Hamid Dalwai was born on 29 September 1932 in Mirjoli, located in Chiplun taluka of Ratnagiri district. Referred to as the \"last modernist\" by historian Ramachandra Guha in The Makers of Modern India, Dalwai emerged as a significant social reformer and writer committed to progressive change within the Muslim community.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Born into a working-class Muslim family, he joined the Rashtra Seva Dal at the age of 14. Early in his career, he worked for Acharya Atre’s newspaper, Maratha, and published a novel titled Indhan (fuel). However, Dalwai soon turned his attention to social reform. He was a strong critic of practices such as Talaq-e-Biddat (Triple Talaq) and the communalization of the Urdu language. He advocated for the adoption of a uniform civil code based on secular principles.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In 1970, he founded the Muslim Satyashodhak Samaj, inspired by Jyotirao Phule’s Satyashodhak Samaj, to promote internal reform in the Muslim community. He worked closely with his wife, Meherunissa Dalwai, in advancing this mission. His ideas and vision were further articulated in his book Muslim Politics in Secular India, in which he laid out a blueprint for a modern, inclusive society.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Hamid Dalwai passed away at the age of 44 on 3 May 1977, in Mumbai. His reformist legacy lives on through the Muslim Satyashodhak Mandal, the organization that succeeded the original Samaj. His younger brother, Husain Dalwai, has also played an important role in public life serving two terms in the Rajya Sabha (2011–2014 and 2014–2020) and two terms in the Maharashtra Legislative Council as a member of the Indian National Congress.</span></p>'),(26527,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Madhavro Mule was born in Ozarkhol, a village near Chiplun in Ratnagiri. Since there was no school in his village, he completed his education in Nagpur. Mule went on to establish the first Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) Shakha (branch) in the Konkan region, specifically in Chiplun. He later became the head of the Punjab wing of the RSS and was recognized as one of the organization\'s most prominent leaders after independence. In honor of his contributions, the people of Ozarkhol established a school in his memory. He passed away in 2015 in Pune after a prolonged illness.</span></p>'),(26529,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">A prominent political figure from the Konkan region, Narayan Rane has played a central role in shaping the political landscape of Ratnagiri and Sindhudurg districts for over three decades. Beginning his political career as a shakha pramukh (local ward chief) in the Shiv Sena, Rane steadily rose through the party ranks, eventually becoming Chief Minister of Maharashtra in 1999 during the final phase of the Shiv Sena–BJP government. His appointment, replacing Manohar Joshi, was a strategic move by Bal Thackeray, who viewed Rane as a strong Maratha leader capable of consolidating the party’s base ahead of the assembly elections.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Rane’s political journey, however, has also been marked by sharp turns. In July 2005, Bal Thackeray expelled him from the Shiv Sena, accusing him of \"anti-party activities\" after Rane publicly alleged internal corruption involving the distribution of party tickets and posts. Following his departure, Rane joined the Indian National Congress, where he served in key positions, before founding his own political outfit, the Maharashtra Swabhiman Paksha, in October 2017.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In 2018, Rane aligned with the BJP and was nominated to the Rajya Sabha. The following year, he merged his party with the BJP. In 2024, he was elected to the Lok Sabha from the Ratnagiri–Sindhudurg constituency on a BJP ticket, further cementing his political relevance in the region.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Over the years, Rane has held several key portfolios, including Revenue, Finance, and Industry, during his time as a Member of the Legislative Assembly. He also served in the Maharashtra Legislative Council during 2016–17. Rane’s political career has not been without controversy. He has frequently been accused by opponents of involvement in violence, most notably the alleged murder of Shiv Sena worker Shridhar Naik in Sindhudurg—a charge he has consistently denied.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Beyond politics, Rane also ventured into the media. In 2008, he launched the Marathi daily newspaper Prahaar, under the banner of Rane Prakashan Pvt. Ltd., using it as a platform to influence public discourse in the Konkan region. From local leadership in Chiplun and Kankavli to the Chief Minister’s office and national politics, Narayan Rane remains one of the most influential and controversial figures in the political history of Ratnagiri and the broader Konkan belt.</span></p>'),(26531,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Premjibhai Ranchhoddas Assar, fondly remembered as ‘Premswarup Premjibhai’, was a key figure in Ratnagiri’s early post-independence political landscape. In 1957, he was elected to the Lok Sabha from the Ratnagiri North constituency on a Jan Sangh ticket, becoming one of the party’s early representatives in the Konkan region.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Inspired by the leadership and ideals of Madhavrao Mule, Premjibhai played a pivotal role in establishing the Jan Sangh’s presence in Ratnagiri, laying the groundwork for its future political influence in the district. He was also an active participant in the Maharashtra statehood movement and is remembered for his commitment to the cause of a united Ratnagiri district, advocating for its administrative integrity and development.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">In recognition of his contributions, a private/aided primary school in Markandi, Chiplun, was named after him in 1980.</span></p>'),(26533,'<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sumitra Mahajan, born on 12 April 1943 in Chiplun, in Ratnagiri district, into a Chitpavan Brahmin family (Sathe), is a distinguished Indian politician who made significant contributions to Indian parliamentary democracy. She served as the 16th Speaker of the Lok Sabha from 2014 to 2019, becoming the second woman to hold this prestigious position after Meira Kumar. Known for her calm and composed leadership, she was often referred to as Tai, a Marathi term for \"older sister.\"</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Mahajan\'s political career spanned several decades, during which she represented the Indore constituency in the Lok Sabha for eight consecutive terms, from 1989 to 2019, making her one of the longest-serving female MPs in Indian history. In addition to her role as Speaker, she served as Union Minister of State from 1999 to 2004 under Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee’s government, overseeing portfolios such as Human Resource Development, Communications and Information Technology, and Petroleum and Natural Gas.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c1\"><span>Throughout her political career, Mahajan was known for her dedication to public service, earning widespread respect across party lines. In recognition of her contributions, she was awarded the Padma Bhushan, one of India\'s highest civilian honors, in 2021.</span></p>'),(26538,'<p class=\"c30 c50 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c34\">LOCAL POLITICS: SANGLI</span></p>'),(26540,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli district was officially formed on 1 August, 1949, as South Satara district. It comprised six talukas from the former Satara district along with two talukas that were previously part of Karnataka. These included areas from the erstwhile princely states and institutions of Jat, Aundh, Kurundwad, Miraj, and Sangli. After the reorganization of states and the formation of Maharashtra on 21 November, 1960, South Satara was renamed as Sangli district.</span></p>');
INSERT INTO `culture_paragraphblock` VALUES (26543,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The Sangli district comprises the Sangli Lok Sabha constituency, which encompasses the Vidhan Sabha segments of Jat, Khanapur, Miraj, Palus-Kadegaon, Tasgaon-Kavathe, and Mahankal. Additionally, there are two segments within the Hatkanangle Lok Sabha constituency: Islampur and Shirala.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c13\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Miraj (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Palus-Kadegaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Khanapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Tasgaon-Kavathe Mahankal</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Jat</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Islampur</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Hatkanangle</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Shirala</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Hatkanangle</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Sangli District is represented by Sangli-Satara Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Pune Teachers’ Constituency, and the Pune Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Sangli district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26546,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The Sangli Lok Sabha constituency was only established in 1967. Previously, the district was represented by the Miraj Lok Sabha constituency. Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, the given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of Lok Sabha constituencies and the reshuffling of Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation.</span></p>'),(26549,'<p class=\"c7\"><span>The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Sangli district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c13\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Dhairyasheel Sambhajirao Mane</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Hatkanangle</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Vishal (Dada) Prakashbapu Patil,</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Independent </span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26551,'<p class=\"c7\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Sangli district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c13\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Suresh Khade</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Miraj (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sudhir Gadgil</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Vishwajeet Kadam</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Palus-Kadegaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Suhas Babar</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Khanapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Rohit Suman Aba Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Tasgaon-Kavathe Mahankal</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Nationalist Congress Party - Sharadchandra Pawar (NCP-SP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Islampur</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Jayant Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">NCP-SP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Shirala</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Satyajit Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Gopichand Padalkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Jat</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26553,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Ramdas Athawale was nominated from the Sangli district, to represent Maharashtra in the Rajya Sabha. </span></p>'),(26555,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Chandrakant Patil (BJP) is the current Guardian Minister for Sangli district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26557,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Sangli district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>'),(26559,'<table class=\"c13\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli-Satara Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Pune Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Jayant Asgaonkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Pune Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Arun Lad</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">NCP-SP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26561,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Sangli district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c49\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c15\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Dr. Raja Dayanidhi</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Shubham Gupta</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Trupti Ankush Dhodmise</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">–</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c24\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Sandip B. Ghuge</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c1\"><span class=\"c0\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26564,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Sangli district.</span></p>'),(26566,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">There are 11 Urban Local Bodies in the Sangli District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 6 Nagar Parishad, 4 Nagar Panchayat. There are 699 Gram Panchayats and 10 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26568,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli Zilla Parishad was established on 1 May, 1962. There are a total of 60 seats in the Zila Parishad.</span></p>'),(26570,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The Sangli-Miraj-Kupwad City Municipal Corporation (SMKMC) was established on 9 February, 1998 and governs a total of 78 wards across the twin cities. It is the largest municipal corporation in South Maharashtra and serves an estimated population of 6.5 lakh. As the local self-government body, SMKMC is responsible for overseeing urban development, civic infrastructure, and public services in the region. Since its formation, the INC and NCP have largely held a majority in the Corporation.</span></p>'),(26572,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Sangli district has seven municipal councils that manage the administration and civic infrastructure of medium-sized urban areas. These include the Nagar Parishads of Ashta, Jath, Palus, Tasgaon, Uran, Islampur, and Vita. Each council is responsible for essential services such as water supply, waste management, local roads, and public health within its jurisdiction.</span></p>'),(26574,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">There are two Nagar Panchayats in Sangli district, located in Kavthemahakal and Khanapur. These town councils serve smaller urban settlements and act as transitional governance bodies for areas developing from rural to urban status.</span></p>'),(26577,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 20 contestants from the Sangli Lok Sabha constituency. Out of this, eight contestants had criminal cases against them, with two having serious cases registered. The contestant with the highest number of criminal cases registered was an Independent. The elected MP, Vishal (Dada) Prakashbapu Patil, has nine criminal cases against him.</span></p>'),(26579,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 50 contestants from six constituencies. Out of these candidates, 18 contestants had criminal cases against them, with five of them having serious cases registered. Independent candidates had the most cases against them.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Of the six elected MLAs, two have serious criminal cases registered against them. Dr. Suresh (Bhau) Dagadu Khade (BJP) from Miraj (SC) Vidhan Sabha constituency has the highest number of criminal cases. The MLAs without any registered cases were from the Jat, Khanpur, Sangli, Tasgaon-Kavathe-Mahanka, and Palus-Kadegaon Vidhan Sabha Constituencies.</span></p>'),(26582,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Since 2023, districts such as Pune, Solapur, and Kolhapur have experienced protests held by members of the Dhangar community asking to be included in the ST category. The community claims that the Dhangad tribe, which has ST status, is identical to the Dhangars and that a “spelling mistake has cost them the ST status”. The Dhangars are on Maharashtra’s list of Vimukta Jati and Nomadic Tribes (VJNT), but have been demanding Scheduled Tribe (ST) status for decades. According to Dhangar leaders, the community is identified as “Dhangad” elsewhere in the country, and gets reservation as an ST. On 21 September 2023, Members of the Dhangar community held a protest at the Ankali Toll Naka on the Sangli-Kolhapur border. Obstructing traffic for about half an hour.</span></p>'),(26584,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Members of the Vadar community, hailing from both Sangli and Kolhapur districts, gathered to stage a march towards the district collector\'s office in Sangli on 24 July, 2023. The demonstration was organized under the leadership of the Maharashtra State Vadar Sangh.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">The march was organized to demand various rights for the neglected Vadar community in the state, including exemptions from stringent caste certificates and caste verification requirements for students and individuals belonging to the community. The memorandum of demands from the Vadar community was submitted to the district authorities during this event. This demonstration was organized under the leadership of Mukund Pawar, the State President of Maharashtra State Vadar Sangh, and Vinayak Kalgutgi, the Sangli District President.</span></p>'),(26586,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Between September and October 2023, several districts in Maharashtra witnessed widespread protests led by Jarange Patil, a native of Jalna, advocating for the inclusion of the Maratha community in the Other Backward Classes (OBC) category for reservations in government jobs and education. On 17 September, 2023, a massive rally took place in Sangli city, organized by the Maratha Kranti Morcha and Sakal Maratha. At Rammandir Chowk, five youths, including a young woman, spoke on behalf of the protesters. A memorandum addressed to the district collector was read aloud during the event.</span></p>'),(26589,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Jayant Patil began his political career in 1990, when he was elected as a Member of the Legislative Assembly (MLA) from the Walwa constituency in Sangli district. Standing as an INC candidate, his victory in the 1990 Maharashtra Legislative Assembly election marked the start of a long and successful political journey. Over the years, he became a popular and trusted leader in his constituency, securing electoral victories in every subsequent election. He has represented the Islampur-Walwa constituency for seven consecutive terms, spanning over three decades, with an average margin of victory of 62,000 votes.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Throughout his political career, Patil has held several significant positions in Maharashtra politics. He served as the Minister of Finance and Planning for three consecutive terms and later became the Minister for Home. In 2009, he was appointed Minister for Rural Development. He has also served as the state unit chief of the NCP and as the Deputy Leader of the Opposition in the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly. In 2023, when a faction of the NCP led by Ajit Pawar allied with the BJP and the SHS under Eknath Shinde’s leadership, Jayant Patil chose to remain with NCP founder Sharad Pawar. He was subsequently appointed as the State President of the NCP (Sharad Pawar faction) in Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26591,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dr. Patangrao Kadam was a prominent educationalist, social reformer, and senior Congress leader in Maharashtra, whose legacy lies at the intersection of education, politics, and philanthropy. A key figure in the Maharashtra Congress, he rose to political prominence through his enduring commitment to expanding access to education.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">He served as the MLA from the Bhilawadi (now Palus-Kadegaon) constituency for seven consecutive terms from 1991 until his passing in 2018, and held various cabinet portfolios during his tenure. Notably, he served as Forest Minister in the Congress-NCP coalition government from 2009 to 2014.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">At the young age of 19, Dr. Kadam founded Bharati Vidyapeeth, which would go on to become one of Maharashtra’s leading educational institutions, offering programs across disciplines from primary education to postgraduate and professional degrees. As part of his vision for rural development, he also established several cooperative institutions, including Bharati Cooperative Bank, Sonhira Cooperative Sugar Factory, Sagreshwar Cooperative Spinning Mill, Sonhira Cooperative Poultry Federation, and Krishna-Verala Cooperative Spinning Mill, each functioning as sister organizations of Bharati Vidyapeeth.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dr. Kadam was also a noted philanthropist. Through various charitable trusts, he provided housing, financial aid, and educational support to disadvantaged students and communities. He served on the Executive Council of the University of Pune for 12 years, was elected to the Executive Council of Mumbai University with a record margin, and was a member of the Senate of Shivaji University, Kolhapur. His vision and dedication left an indelible mark on the state’s educational and cooperative movements.</span></p>'),(26593,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dr. Patangrao Kadam’s legacy continues through his son, Dr. Vishwajit Kadam, a key figure in Maharashtra’s contemporary political landscape and a representative of the Kadam family, one of the state’s most influential political dynasties.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Elected as the MLA from Palus-Kadegaon in Sangli district, Vishwajit Kadam has served in ministerial roles handling portfolios such as Cooperation, Agriculture, Social Justice, and Consumer Affairs. He brings to his political career a strong academic foundation, holding a B.E., MBA, and a PhD in Management, as well as completing a program in Management and Leadership in Education at Harvard University.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Dr. Kadam has also played a significant role in the Congress party organization, serving twice as president of the Maharashtra Pradesh Youth Congress (2011 and 2014). He continues to be closely involved in the educational and cooperative institutions established by his father, serving as Secretary of Bharati Vidyapeeth and Bharati Cooperative Bank. His work reflects a blend of technocratic expertise and grassroots political engagement, continuing the Kadam family\'s commitment to development through education and social justice.</span></p>'),(26596,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Vasantdada Patil was a freedom fighter, social reformer, and influential politician. Born in the small village of Padmale in Miraj Taluka of Sangli district, his involvement in the freedom movement began early - initially in 1930 and more actively from 1940 onward. The Quit India Movement in 1942 sparked intensified resistance in the Sangli region. Vasantdada and his comrades adopted bold tactics such as cutting telephone lines, setting government posts on fire, sabotaging railway tracks, and even deploying pistol-bombs to undermine the British administration. In Solapur, four young patriots were martyred during this civil disobedience. In their memory, Vasantdada gave up tea - a personal and symbolic act of tribute. To avoid arrest, he went underground for a time but was eventually captured. He spent nearly three years in prison, exemplifying his commitment to the cause. During a daring escape attempt in 1943, he was shot in the shoulder and recaptured. His release was eventually secured through the efforts of leaders like Ramanand Bharti and Barrister Nath Pai, who mobilized public support through protests and meetings. </span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">In the Satara-Sangli region, Vasantdada emerged as a key leader of the freedom struggle alongside prominent figures such as Kranti Singh Nana Patil and Yashwantrao Chavan. After independence, he remained active in public life - collecting weapons from local fighters and encouraging them to redirect their energies toward nation-building. His legacy is a testament to his fearless participation in the freedom movement and his deep commitment to shaping a progressive, independent India.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">In the post-independence period, Vasantdada Patil transitioned into a prominent political career. He served as the 6th Chief Minister of Maharashtra, first from 1977 to 1978 and again from 1983 to 1985. Although his first term was interrupted by Sharad Pawar’s rise to the chief ministership, his leadership and grassroots support helped him return to power. He began his political career as the state’s first Cooperative Minister in 1972, and held the distinction of being elected as the MLA from Sangli in the first election of independent India. </span></p>\n<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Over the course of nearly 25 years, he represented Sangli both in the Maharashtra Vidhan Sabha and the Lok Sabha. He also played key roles outside the legislature, serving as President of the Maharashtra State Cooperative Bank, the National Mill Mazdoor Sangh, and the Maharashtra Pradesh Congress Committee. Vasantdada prioritized party organization and grassroots mobilization, significantly strengthening the Congress party’s presence in rural Maharashtra. His political journey was characterized by resilience, leadership, and unwavering dedication to public service, leaving an indelible mark on Maharashtra’s political landscape.</span></p>'),(26598,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Prakashbapu was the son of Vasantdada Patil (through his first wife Malatitai Patil) and upheld the family’s political legacy. He served as a Member of Parliament (MP) from the Sangli constituency in the 8th, 9th, 10th, 13th, and 14th Lok Sabha, representing the INC. A seasoned parliamentarian, he remained a key political figure in the region until his passing in 2005 at the age of 58.</span></p>'),(26600,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Pratik Prakashbapu Patil, son of Prakashbapu and grandson of Vasantdada Patil, carried forward the Patil family’s INC legacy. He was elected to the 14th Lok Sabha in a 2006 by-election after his father\'s death and re-elected in 2009. During his tenure, he held multiple central government roles, including Minister of State for Coal, Youth Affairs and Sports, and Heavy Industries. However, he lost the 2014 election, marking the first time since 1980 that the Patil family lost hold of the Sangli seat. In 2019, he resigned from INC and declared his exit from politics.</span></p>'),(26602,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Vishal Prakashbapu Patil, son of Prakashbapu and grandson of Vasantdada Patil, is the current Member of Parliament (2024-present) from Sangli. Although affiliated with INC, he contested as an Independent after failing to secure a party ticket due to alliance disagreements between INC and Shiv Sena (UBT). He defeated both BJP and SHS-UBT candidates and declared support for the INDIA bloc and INC in Parliament, identifying himself as the 100th Congress-aligned MP. His mother, Shailja Patil, is a leader in the Mahila Pradesh Congress, and his elder brother is Pratik Patil.</span></p>'),(26604,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Madan Vishwanathrao Patil, a member of the INC, represented Sangli in the 12th Lok Sabha (1998–1999). He is the grandson of Vasantdada Patil and continued the family’s political legacy. In the 2009 Maharashtra Assembly elections, he was elected MLA from the Sangli constituency and served as Cabinet Minister for Women and Child Development in the Ashok Chavan-led government.</span></p>'),(26606,'<p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c0\">Shalini Patil (formerly Shalini Phalke, later Jadhav) was the second wife of Vasantdada Patil and a significant political figure in her own right. She served as a minister in the Maharashtra government during the 1980s under the INC banner. Popularly known as Shalinitai, she also founded the Rajmata Jijau Pratishthan, a charitable organization focused on healthcare and women’s welfare. She later established her own political outfit, Krantisena Maharashtra, and was elected as a Member of Parliament from Sangli in the 1983 general elections. Her philanthropic and political work further solidified the Patil family’s influence in Maharashtra politics.</span></p>'),(26611,'<p class=\"c38 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c36\">LOCAL POLITICS: SATARA</span></p>'),(26613,'<p class=\"c2\"><span>Satara was once the capital of the Maratha Kingdom under Shahu I. The district\'s name is believed to derive from </span><span class=\"c35\">Saat Tara</span><span> or </span><span class=\"c35\">Saat Tare</span><span class=\"c1\">, meaning \"Seven Stars,\" referring to the seven hill forts scattered across the surrounding landscape. Historically, the region was known as the Satara Province. In 1856, twelve new mahals (administrative regions) were added to the existing structure. By 1948, Satara district consisted of eleven subdivisions, including Bijapur (now in Karnataka), Jaoli, Karad, Khanapur, Khatav, Koregaon, Pandharpur, Satara, Tasgaon, Walwa, and Wai.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1949, the district was reorganized into two parts: North Satara, with its headquarters in Satara town, and South Satara, headquartered in Sangli. Both were included in the then Bombay State. Following the formation of Maharashtra in 1960, North Satara was renamed Satara district, and South Satara became the Sangli district.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">According to the 1961 Census, Satara district comprised nine talukas and two mahals, covering a total of 1,160 villages. Satara also holds an important place in Maharashtra\'s political landscape, having produced five chief ministers of the state.</span></p>'),(26616,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">The Satara district consists of the Satara Lok Sabha constituency, which includes the following Vidhan Sabha segments:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c34\"><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Karad North</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Karad South</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Koregaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Patan</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Wai</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Satara District is represented by the Sangli-Satara Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Pune Teachers’ Constituency, and the Pune Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Satara district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26619,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, the given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Satara Lok Sabha constituency and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done. </span></p>'),(26622,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Satara district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c3\"><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Shrimant Chh. Udayanraje Pratapsinha Maharaj Bhonsle</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26624,'<p class=\"c2\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Satara district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c3\"><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Makrand Jadhav Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Wai</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mahesh Shinde</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Koregaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Manoj Ghorpade</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Karad North</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Atulbaba Bhosale</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Karad South</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Shambhuraj Desai</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Patan</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Shivendra Raje Bhosale</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26626,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">There is no member from the Satara district currently in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(26628,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Shambhuraj Desai (SHS) is the current Guardian Minister for Satara district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26630,'<p class=\"c2\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Ratnagiri district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c34\"><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Sangli-Satara Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Pune Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Jayant Asgaonkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c4\"><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Pune Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Arun Lad</span></p></td><td class=\"c5\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Nationalist Congress Party - Sharadchandra Pawar (NCP-SP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26632,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the key administrative heads of Satara district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c34\"><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c27\"><span class=\"c15\">Service / Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Santosh Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Dnyaneshwar Khilari</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Smt. Yashni Nagrajan</span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0 c28\"><span class=\"c1\"></span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0 c28\"><span class=\"c1\"></span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c24\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Tushar Doshi </span></p></td><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26635,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Satara district.</span></p>'),(26637,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">There are 17 Urban Local Bodies in the Satara District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 9 Nagar Parishad, and 8 Nagar Panchayat. There are 1493 Gram Panchayats and 11 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26639,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara Zilla Parishad (ZP Satara) was established in May 1962 under the guidance of Yashwantraoji Chavan. Swasaheb Parlekar was the first president of Satara Zilla Parishad. Today, 11 panchayat samitis and 1501 grampanchayats are working for rural areas.</span></p>'),(26641,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1948, the Satara district, previously known as the Satara Province, consisted of 11 subdivisions: Bijapur (now part of Karnataka State), Jaoli, Karad, Khanapur, Khatav, Koregaon, Pandharpur, Satara, Tasgaon, Walwa, and Wai. In 1856, 12 new mahals (administrative regions) were established.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1947, the Satara district was split into two: South Satara with its headquarters in Sangli and North Satara with its headquarters in Satara. Both of these districts were part of the Bombay State.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">From 1960 onwards, the name of the North Satara district was changed to Satara, and South Satara became Sangli. During the 1961 Census, Satara district comprised 9 talukas (sub-districts) and 2 mahals, encompassing a total of 1160 villages.</span></p>'),(26643,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Satara district has nine Municipal Councils (Nagar Parishads), which serve as the administrative bodies for medium-sized towns. These include the Satara Nagar Parishad, Mahabaleshwar Nagar Parishad, Karad Nagar Parishad, Mhaswad Nagar Parishad, Palus Nagar Parishad, Panchgani Nagar Parishad, Phaltan Nagar Parishad, Rahimatpur Nagar Parishad, and Wai Nagar Parishad.</span></p>'),(26645,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In addition, there are eight Town Councils (Nagar Panchayats) catering to smaller towns and transitional urban areas. These are Dahiwadi Nagar Panchayat, Khandala Nagar Panchayat, Koregaon Nagar Panchayat, Lonand Nagar Panchayat, Medha Nagar Panchayat, Malkapur Nagar Panchayat, Patan Nagar Panchayat, and Waluj Nagar Panchayat.</span></p>'),(26648,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 16 contestants from Satara out of which five contestants had criminal cases against them, with three having serious cases registered. The contestant with the highest number of criminal cases registered was from the BJP, namely Udayanraje Bhosale, the elected MP, and had four cases against them. </span></p>'),(26650,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 49 contestants from six constituencies out of which 12 contestants had criminal cases against them, with three of the contestants having serious cases registered. The political parties, the BJP and the Baliraja Party, had the highest number of contestants with criminal cases.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">From the six elected MLAs, one of them has serious criminal cases registered against him. Bhonsle Shivendrasinh Abhaysinhraje (BJP) from Satara Vidhan Sabha constituency has the highest number of criminal cases. The MLAs without any registered cases were from the Karad North &amp; South and Patan Vidhan Sabha Constituencies.</span></p>'),(26653,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">After the Quit India movement was launched by Mahatma Gandhi in 1942, the people of Satara participated in the movement actively. In the first phase of the agitation in Satara district under the Gandhian ideological program, more than a 100 meetings were held peacefully, the tricolor was hoisted in over a 100 villages, and nearly 60 village heads resigned from their positions. Besides, Forest Satyagraha was also organized to demonstrate Indian resistance to colonialism.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In the second phase, at the taluka level, on 24th August at Karad and Patan, on 3rd September at Tasgaon, and on 9th September 1942 at Vaduj in Khatav, marches were taken to Mamledar offices and the tricolor was raised in which thousands of people including peasants and students from the surrounding villages joined. Protesters faced repression by the local police, including an incident of firing at Vaduj in which 10 were killed and more than twenty were injured. Freedom fighters such as Balkoba Undalkar, Buwa Mhavshikar, and Y.B. Chavan played a crucial role in this phase.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1943, the third phase of the movement began with the establishment of the Prati Sarkar, or the parallel government under the leadership of Krantisinha Nana Patil. The British administrative system in the district virtually collapsed, and the Prati Sarkar ran its administration till 1946, resisting all efforts by the colonial administration to reimpose its rule.</span></p>'),(26655,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">The construction of a dam on the Wang river near Marathwadi in Satara district impacted approximately 1,800 families across nine villages, with four villages fully submerged and five partially submerged. Villagers alleged that the dam work continued without proper rehabilitation for the affected communities. In response, residents of the villages in Patan tehsil launched a jal satyagraha in mid-July 2012, refusing to leave the dam\'s submergence area until their demands were met. Supported by the National Alliance of People’s Movement (NAPM) and other activists, their struggle against the dam began in the summer of 2000 when Anil Shinde, a Marathwadi resident, reached out to Medha Patkar for assistance. The protest concluded in August 2012 after the villagers\' demands were successfully addressed.</span></p>'),(26657,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">During the nationwide farmer protests in 2021, various regions of Maharashtra witnessed farmers uniting to oppose the three farm laws passed by the government in September 2020. On 6 February 2021, under the leadership of Satvasheela Chavan, wife of senior Congress leader Prithviraj Chavan, farmers organized a Chakka Jam at Kolhapur Naka in Karad in Solapur blocking a busy road. As a result, they were detained by the authorities.</span></p>'),(26659,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Since 2023, districts such as Pune, Solapur, and Kolhapur have experienced protests held by members of the Dhangar community asking to be included in the Scheduled Tribe (ST) category. The community claims that the Dhangad community, which has ST status, is identical to the Dhangars and that a “spelling mistake has cost them the ST status”. The Dhangars are on Maharashtra’s list of Vimukta Jati and Nomadic Tribes (VJNT), but have been demanding ST status for decades. According to Dhangar leaders, the community is identified as “Dhangad” elsewhere in the country, and gets reservation as an ST. On 20 September 2023, a protest was held at the Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Chowk by the members of the community who brought in their sheep as well to block the Satara-Pune highway.</span></p>'),(26661,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">On 18 August 2023, insulting comments against Bhagwan Ram and Sita on Instagram went viral on social media. A 21-year-old Muslim resident of Pusesavali village in Satara district was accused of having made the comments. In response, the Hindu community organised a protest. Tensions raged for multiple weeks between the two communities. On 15 August and 10 September, derogatory comments on Instagram against Bhagwan Ram and the Maratha ruler Shivaji Maharaj went viral. Two 23-year-old Muslims and a Muslim teenager from the village were accused of having made the comments. Youth from the Hindu community attacked mosques and Muslim owned shops in Pusesavali on 10 September when the post cited above went viral. Thirty one-year-old Nurul Hasan Shikalgar, an engineer &amp; businessman, was at one of the mosques attacked and died from wounds to the head. Ten other people were injured and 23 were arrested.</span></p>'),(26664,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Nana Patil, popularly known as \"Krantisinh,\" was a prominent freedom fighter and communist leader. He played a crucial role in the founding of the Hindustan Republican Association. His activism against British colonial rule led him to live underground for three years, from 1929 to 1932. During the following decade, from 1932 to 1942, he faced imprisonment on numerous occasions. Notably, the British considered him an eloquent speaker.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1942, during the Quit India Movement, Nana Patil remained underground for nearly 44 months, emerging as one of the foremost leaders of the underground resistance. In 1943, the British government offered a reward for his capture. In the Satara district, he established a parallel government known as the \'Patri Sarkar\' in the local language.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Nana Patil\'s social work began in 1919 with the Prarthana Samaj, where he focused on the upliftment of backward and oppressed communities, raising awareness against blind faith and harmful traditions. After a decade with the Prarthana Samaj, he became associated with the Satyashodhak Samaj, founded by Jyotiba Phule. He advocated for cost-effective \'samaj vivah\' or low-budget marriages, discouraging extravagant traditional wedding ceremonies. Throughout his life, he championed the rights of the poor and farmers, encouraging them to avoid debt and emphasizing the importance of education for social progress.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Initially associated with the INC, Nana Patil later joined the Peasants and Workers Party of India (PWP) in 1948, alongside figures like Shankarrao More, Keshavrao Jedhe, Bhausaheb Raut, and Madhavrao Bagal. He received a nomination from the Communist Party of India in 1957 to contest the Lok Sabha elections in the Satara constituency and again in 1967 from the Beed constituency.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span>Nana Patil was also active in the United Maharashtra Movement, and he was closely associated with Aacharya Atre in advocating for the creation of the state of Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26666,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Yashwantrao Chavan, commonly known as Chavan saheb, was a prominent Indian politician deeply engaged in the fight for Independence from British rule. He rose to become a senior leader in the Indian National Congress. His political journey began with local panchayat elections, and he later held the position of Zilla Parishad chairman in Satara district. Chavan played a crucial role as the third Chief Minister of Bombay state in independent India from 1956 to 1960. When Maharashtra was established as a separate state, he became its inaugural Chief Minister, serving from 1960 to 1962.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">His early years were marked by active participation in the Indian Independence movement, greatly influenced by Mahatma Gandhi. He joined Gandhi\'s civil disobedience campaigns (satyagraha) and faced fines and imprisonment. In 1932, he was incarcerated for 18 months for proudly flying the Indian national flag in Satara. Chavan also played a pivotal role in the Quit India campaign against British colonial rule in 1942, operating underground until his eventual arrest and imprisonment. His unwavering dedication made him an integral part of the Congress Party\'s pre-independence activities.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1962, following India\'s military conflict with China, Chavan was appointed as the Defense Minister in Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru\'s cabinet. He continued in this role under Prime Minister Lal Bahadur Shastri and oversaw the war with Pakistan in the Kashmir region in 1965. Later, in 1966, he assumed the position of Home Minister in the cabinet of Prime Minister Indira Gandhi. He also served as a Member of Parliament in the Lok Sabha. His career continued to ascend, with roles as Finance Minister in 1970 and Foreign Minister in 1974. It\'s worth noting that Chavan was widely considered a top choice for the post of Prime Minister after the passing of Lal Bahadur Shastri, but he proposed the name of Ms. Indira Gandhi for the role.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">During the period of the national emergency declared by Indira Gandhi from 1975 to 1977, Chavan stood by her side, even as numerous opposition leaders were arrested and incarcerated. He is recognized as an architect of modern Maharashtra and played a pivotal role in nurturing future leaders like Sharad Pawar, who went on to become influential figures in Indian politics.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Yashwantrao Chavan\'s autobiography, titled \"Krishnakath,\" is esteemed as an inspiring and motivating book. His legacy continues to hold significance in the political landscape of India.</span></p>'),(26668,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Shalinitai was born in 1931 as Shalini Phalke into a relatively poor family. Her father not only ensured that she received a good education, despite financial constraints, but also ensured that she married a man who would permit her to continue her education after marriage. At a young age, Shalini was married to Shyamrao Jadhav, a gentleman belonging to her community and similar background, in a match arranged by their families in the usual Indian way. Her education continued with the full support of her husband and she earned a law degree and was called to the Bar. She had four children with Shyamrao Jadhav and discharged her family responsibilities with the help of her husband and his near relatives, while pursuing first her education and then her political career.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Shalini Jadhav entered politics when her children were still very young. Her first public position was as a member of Satara Zilla Parishad (District Council). Later, she was appointed president of the women\'s wing of the Maharashtra Pradesh Congress Committee. It was as a worker of the Congress party that she came in contact with Vasantdada Patil, her future second husband.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Shyamrao Jadhav passed away in 1964, and shortly after his death, Shalini married Marathi politician Vasantdada Patil. She held ministerial positions in the Congress Party Governments of Maharashtra during the 1980s. In 1981, she was instrumental in getting the then-chief minister A. R. Antulay to resign. Shalinitai became active in Maharashtra politics after marrying Vasantdada Patil. Later, she represented the Sangli constituency in the Indian parliament (Lok Sabha) during a period in the 1980s. She represented the constituency of Koregaon, Satara in the Maharashtra state legislative assembly, the Vidhan Sabha, from 1999 to 2009. Before that, she unsuccessfully contested the seat in 1990 and 1995 for the Janata Dal and as an independent, respectively.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">During the 1970s, she founded a charity called Rajmata Jijau Pratishthan to raise funds for hospitals for the poor and women-related activities. The charity was in the news for the controversial granting of or purchase of land by the government for the charity. She was well known for speaking her mind, particularly about rights for the Maratha community. During her career, she has campaigned for more seats for women in elected bodies. She has also criticized the Indian Central Government\'s move to extend 27.5 per cent reservation to OBCs in the central academic institutes such as IITs and IIMs. Since the Nationalist Congress Party led by Sharad Pawar supported this policy, Shalinitai was expelled from that party in 2006. In 2009, she launched a new party called Krantisena Maharashtra. The party attracted scant support, and soon Shalinitai joined the Congress party.</span></p>'),(26670,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Shriniwas Dadasaheb Patil, born on 11 February 1941, is a dynamic Indian leader with extensive experience in administration, politics, and the legislature. He shares a school connection with Mr. Sharad Pawar, the NCP supremo. Patil is a former IAS officer and holds an M.A. degree in Economics and an LL.B. degree from Pune and Mumbai Universities, respectively. He belonged to the 1979 batch of the IAS and served as the collector of both Beed district and Pune district. Additionally, he held the position of deputy CEO of the Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation (MIDC).</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">In his political career, Patil was elected as a Member of Parliament in the 13th and 14th Lok Sabha from 1999 to 2009, representing the Karad constituency on behalf of the NCP. In 2013, he assumed the role of the Governor of Sikkim, serving in this position until August 2018.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Since October 2019, Shriniwas Dadasaheb Patil has been serving as the Member of Lok Sabha from the Satara constituency in Maharashtra, India. Notably, he secured his third term as a Member of Parliament by winning the bypolls in October 2019, defeating former Member of Parliament Udayanraje Bhosale with a margin of over 87,000 votes. His diverse background and political achievements mark him as one of India\'s accomplished leaders.</span></p>'),(26672,'<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Udayanraje Bhosale currently holds the prestigious title of Chhatrapati, a position with historical significance that was established by the Maratha emperor Shivaji during his coronation in 1674. Bhosale\'s political journey began in 1998-99 when he became a Member of the Legislative Assembly. During this period, he also served as the Minister for Revenue. Later, he joined the NCP and held the position of Member of Parliament from 2009 to 2019. During the 2019 Lok Sabha elections 2020, he was nominated to the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c2\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhosale enjoys immense popularity among the people of Satara district and is known for his fearlessness. His lifestyle and cheerful demeanor resonate with the youth. Notably, he is recognized for his distinctive habit of holding his shirt collar and incorporating movie dialogues into his speeches, making him a unique and engaging figure in politics.</span></p>'),(26680,'<p class=\"c36 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c30\">LOCAL POLITICS: SINDHUDURG</span></p>'),(26682,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Sindhudurg district is part of the Konkan division in India and was created by separating it from the former Ratnagiri district. It stands as an administrative district with its unique identity and geographical boundaries within the Konkan region. With the reorganization of the states in 1956, the district was included in the Bombay state, and since 1960, it has formed a part of Maharashtra. The name of the district has been adopted from the famous sea fort of Sindhudurg. This was built by Shivaji Maharaj near Malwan, and it means ‘Sea Fort’.</span></p>'),(26685,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The Sindhudurg district consists of the joint Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Lok Sabha, which comprises the following six Vidhan Sabha segments:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c15\"><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Sawantwadi</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Sindhudurg</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Kudal</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Sindhudurg</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Kankavli</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Sindhudurg</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Chiplun</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Rajapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Sindhudurg District is represented by Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Konkan Teachers’ Constituency, and the Konkan Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Sindhudurg district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26688,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The joint Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Lok Sabha constituency was formed only after the latest delimitation committee of 2002. The 2009 elections implemented the delimitation.</span></p>'),(26691,'<p class=\"c9\"><span>The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Sindhudurg district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c15\"><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Narayan Tatu Rane</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26693,'<p class=\"c9\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Sindhudurg district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c15\"><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Shekhar Nikam</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Chiplun</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Uday Samant</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Ratnagiri</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Kiran Samant</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Rajapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Nitesh Rane</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Kankawli</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Nilesh Rane</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Kudal</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Deepak Kesarkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">Sawantwadi</span></p></td><td class=\"c4\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c10\"><span class=\"c8\">SHS</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26695,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">There is no member from the Sindhudurg district currently in the Rajya Sabha representing Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26697,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Nitesh Rane (BJP) is the current Guardian Minister for Sindhudurg district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26699,'<p class=\"c9\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Sindhudurg district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c15\"><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Raigad-Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Konkan Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Dnyaneshwar Mhatre</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Konkan Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Niranjan Davkhare</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26701,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The following are the key administrative heads of Sindhudurg district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c15\"><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c11\"><span class=\"c2\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Anil Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">–</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Makarand Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c21\"><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Mohan Dahikar </span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c13\"><span class=\"c8\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26704,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Sindhudurg district.</span></p>'),(26706,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">There are 8 Urban Local Bodies in the Sindhudurg District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 3 Nagar Parishad, 5 Nagar Panchayat. There are 433 Gram Panchayats and 8 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26708,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">On 1 May 1981, the Ratnagiri district was bifurcated to create the Sindhudurg district, in response to local demands for focused industrial and agricultural development in the southern Konkan region. Sindhudurg now represents the southern part of Maharashtra’s western coastline and functions as a distinct administrative unit. Following the district’s formation, the Zilla Parishad Sindhudurg was also established. It currently comprises 50 seats.</span></p>'),(26710,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Sindhudurg district has three municipal councils: Malwan Nagar Parishad, Sawantwadi Nagar Parishad, and Vengurla Nagar Parishad. These councils are responsible for urban governance and civic administration in their respective towns.</span></p>'),(26712,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">The district also includes five Nagar Panchayats: Devgad-Jamsande, Kasai-Dodamarg, Kankavali, Kudal, and Vabhave Vaibhavwadi. These town councils manage civic services in semi-urban and developing areas.</span></p>'),(26715,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of nine contestants from Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg Lok Sabha. Out of these, two candidates had criminal cases against them, with only one contestant having serious cases registered. The elected MP, Narayan Tatu Rane from the BJP, has 7 criminal cases against him - the highest among the candidates. </span></p>'),(26718,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Sitaram Patwardhan, born on 4 November, 1894, in the village of Agargule in Ratnagiri taluka (near Ganpatipule), was a renowned freedom fighter and social reformer, fondly known as \"Koknache Gandhi\" (Gandhi of Konkan). A devoted follower of Mahatma Gandhi, Patwardhan pursued his early education in Ratnagiri before continuing his studies in Pune, where he also briefly worked at Fergusson College.</span></p>'),(26720,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In 1916, he had the opportunity to meet Gandhi during the Bombay session of the Indian National Congress, an encounter that deeply inspired him. Motivated by Gandhi\'s ideals, Patwardhan resigned from his job at Fergusson College and joined the Sabarmati Ashram in 1919. There, he was entrusted by Gandhi with the responsibility of publishing the weekly journal Young India.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Patwardhan\'s commitment to the freedom struggle led to his imprisonment for three and a half years due to his participation in the Satyagraha movement. After his release, he returned to Konkan, where he focused on uplifting the marginalized Kunbi community by establishing the \'Kunbi Seva Sangh.\' His efforts helped the Indian National Congress gain a strong foothold in the region, and he served as the district president of the party.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In addition to his political work, Patwardhan founded the \'Gopuri Ashram\' in Kankavli (Sindhudurg), where he championed social equality. He broke caste barriers by engaging in manual scavenging himself and producing manure (Sonkhat) at the ashram. Defying societal norms, he and his upper-caste colleagues even skinned dead animals to make leather, a task traditionally restricted to the Mahar caste. His ashram became a symbol of unity, where people from 20-25 different castes lived together harmoniously.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In 1952, Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru visited Patwardhan’s Gopuri Ashram and recognized his efforts. Later, Patwardhan worked alongside Vinoba Bhave in the Bhoodan movement, advocating for land redistribution.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Sitaram Patwardhan passed away on 10 March, 1971, leaving behind a legacy of social justice and reform. His life embodied the Gandhian principles of equality, selflessness, and service to the downtrodden.</span></p>'),(26722,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Mahadev Tukaram Walawalkar, affectionately known as Aba, was born on 20 May 1887, in Sindhudurg. He completed his secondary education at Sawantwadi High School and later graduated from Wilson College, Mumbai. Strongly influenced by Lokmanya Tilak, he became an active participant in the freedom movement, including protests against Bible education in schools. Aba responded to Mahatma Gandhi\'s call to boycott colonial educational institutions and graduated from Tilak Maharashtra University, later joining the National School Industry. He engaged in promoting national pride, self-respect, cow protection, and liquor prohibition, writing patriotic poems to inspire others. Upon returning to his village, Aba established schools focused on national education and dedicated himself to serving the Dalit community by constructing wells, hostels, and providing educational resources.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">He was deeply involved in \'Harijan Seva\' and the promotion of \'Khadi\' (handspun cloth), embodying Gandhian principles of self-reliance. Despite personal losses, including the death of his wife, Aba remained committed to the freedom struggle, rejecting offers of wealth from colonial powers. He worked tirelessly in fields like yarn spinning, khadi promotion, and boycotting foreign goods. Even after India gained independence, he continued his life of selfless service. His dedication to Satyagraha and spinning yarn daily made him a symbol of Gandhian values. In recognition of his contributions, he was honored by Prime Minister Indira Gandhi during the 25th year of Indian independence. Aba Walawalkar\'s legacy is that of a selfless freedom fighter who played a pivotal role in the independence movement, focusing on social justice, national education, and the promotion of self-reliance through Khadi.</span></p>'),(26724,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">During the Civil Disobedience Movement, the region then comprising Ratnagiri and present-day Sindhudurg districts became a significant site of protest against British colonial rule. One of the most prominent events occurred in Shiroda village, Vengurla, between May 12 and 15, 1930. As part of the Salt Satyagraha, volunteers organized Satyagraha camps, held public meetings, and sold illegally procured salt in defiance of British laws. The agitation in Shiroda drew widespread attention and became a symbolic act of civil resistance in the Konkan region.</span></p>'),(26726,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In November 2003, violence erupted in Kankavli following the murder of NCP leader Satyavijay Bhise, allegedly by Shiv Sena workers. In retaliation, a mob of NCP supporters set fire to the house of former Chief Minister Narayan Rane. The unrest also led to the burning and looting of several shops. Bhise, who had recently been elected as head of the Kalsuli gram panchayat, was accused by local Shiv Sena activists of using coercive tactics to influence the election. Approximately 50 individuals were arrested in connection with the incident.</span></p>'),(26729,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Narayan Rane is a senior Indian politician with a decades-long career at both the state and national levels. Born on 10 April 1952, in Mumbai, he began his public life in the 1970s with the Shiv Sena, a regional party with a strong base in Maharashtra. Over time, he rose through the ranks and briefly served as the Chief Minister of Maharashtra in 1999, succeeding Manohar Joshi. Though his tenure lasted only a few months, it was a significant milestone in his political journey.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In 2005, following a fallout with SHS founder Bal Thackeray, Rane left the party and joined the INC. During his time with the Congress, he held key portfolios, including the Revenue Ministry in Maharashtra, and remained an influential figure, particularly in the Konkan region. However, he frequently expressed dissatisfaction over what he perceived as under-recognition by the party leadership.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In 2017, Rane launched his own political outfit, the Maharashtra Swabhiman Paksha, and later merged it with the BJP, formally joining the party in 2019. His political influence extended further in 2021 when he was appointed Union Minister for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) in the Narendra Modi-led cabinet. He is the patriarch of the Rane political family, with both his sons, Nilesh and Nitesh, also active in politics.</span></p>'),(26731,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Nilesh Rane, the elder son of Narayan Rane, is a politician and social activist known for his sharp speeches and outspoken views. He began his political career with the INC and served as a MP from the Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg constituency during the 15th Lok Sabha. While in Parliament, he was a member of the Committee on Home Affairs and the Rules Committee.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">In the 2014 general elections, Nilesh Rane lost his seat to Shiv Sena candidate Vinayak Raut. Following this setback, he gradually distanced himself from the Congress and eventually joined the BJP in 2019. He is now seen as a prominent Maratha leader within the party and remains vocal in his criticism of the Uddhav Thackeray-led Shiv Sena. Nilesh Rane continues to play an active role in shaping the political discourse in the Konkan region.</span></p>'),(26733,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Nitesh Rane, the younger son of Narayan Rane, initially pursued a career in hospitality after completing his MBA in the United Kingdom. However, encouraged by his father, he returned to India and entered active politics. Early in his political career, he founded the Swabhiman Sanghatana, a social organization focused on addressing youth unemployment, access to healthcare, and essential civic amenities in Maharashtra, especially in Mumbai and the Konkan region.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Nitesh also served as General Secretary of the Youth Congress before shifting political allegiances. He contested and won the 2014 Maharashtra Legislative Assembly elections from the Kankavli constituency. Known for his fiery speeches and combative political style, Nitesh Rane has frequently attracted media attention and public controversy. Despite this, he remains a strong grassroots leader and an influential figure in his constituency.</span></p>'),(26735,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Vinayak Raut is a senior leader of the SHS with a long-standing political career that began in 1985 as a corporator in the Brihanmumbai Municipal Corporation (BMC). He gained prominence when he was elected to the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from the Vile Parle constituency, serving from 1999 to 2004. In 2005, he was appointed as the All India Party Secretary of the SHS, marking his rise within the party’s central leadership.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">From 2012 to 2014, Raut served as a Member of the Maharashtra Legislative Council. He was subsequently elected to the 16th Lok Sabha in 2014 from the Ratnagiri-Sindhudurg constituency and re-elected in 2019 to the 17th Lok Sabha. He currently serves as the Shiv Sena\'s leader in the Lok Sabha. Known for his firm opposition to the Rane family’s political influence in Konkan, Vinayak Raut has played a key role in consolidating the party’s base in the region.</span></p>'),(26737,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Uday Samant is a senior SHS leader aligned with the Eknath Shinde faction. He began his political journey in 2004 when he was first elected as an MLA from the Ratnagiri constituency. He has successfully retained this seat in every subsequent assembly election - 2004, 2009, 2014, and 2019.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Over the course of his legislative career, Samant has held multiple ministerial portfolios, including Higher and Technical Education, Housing and Urban Development, and Industries. In 2018, he was appointed Chairman of the Maharashtra Housing and Area Development Authority (MHADA). He currently serves as the Guardian Minister for both Ratnagiri and Sindhudurg districts.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c8\">Samant is known for his administrative skills and political negotiation capabilities. His leadership came to national attention during his tenure as Minister of Industries, particularly in relation to the Vedanta-Foxconn project, a major proposed industrial initiative intended to boost employment and investment in Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26742,'<p class=\"c6 c34 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c39\">LOCAL POLITICS: SOLAPUR</span></p>'),(26744,'<p class=\"c5\"><span>The name \"Solapur\" is believed to be derived from the words </span><span class=\"c37\">sola</span><span> (meaning \"sixteen\" in Marathi) and </span><span class=\"c37\">pura</span><span class=\"c9\"> (meaning \"village\"), referring to the idea that the present-day city developed out of a cluster of sixteen villages. Historically, the region now comprising Solapur district was part of the Ahilyanagar (previously Ahmednagar), Pune, and Satara districts. In 1838, it became a sub-district under Ahilyanagar, including subdivisions such as Barshi, Mohol, Madha, Karmala, Indi, Hippargi, and Muddebihal. However, this administrative unit was abolished in 1864.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In 1871, the district was reorganized by combining the subdivisions of Solapur, Barshi, Mohol, Madha, and Karmala, along with Pandharpur and Sangola from the Satara district. In 1875, the Malshiras subdivision was also added. Following the States Reorganisation Act of 1956, Solapur became part of the Bombay State, and in 1960, it was designated as a full-fledged district in the newly formed state of Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26747,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">The Solapur district consists of the Solapur and Madha Lok Sabha constituencies, which include the following Vidhan Sabha segments:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c33\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Akkalkot</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Mohol</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Pandharpur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur City Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur City North</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur South</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Man</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Phaltan</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Satara</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Karmala</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Malshiras</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Sangola</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c11\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Solapur District is represented by Solapur-Sangli Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Pune Teachers’ Constituency, and the Pune Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Solapur district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26750,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, the given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Solapur Lok Sabha constituency and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(26753,'<p class=\"c5\"><span>The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Solapur district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c36\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c40\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Mohite-Patil Dhairyasheel Rajsinh</span></p></td><td class=\"c40\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Madha</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Nationalist Congress Party - Sharadchandra Pawar (NCP-SP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Praniti Sushilkumar Shinde</span></p></td><td class=\"c40\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26755,'<p class=\"c5\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Solapur district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c33\"><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Raju Khare</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Mohol (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">NCP-SP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Vijay Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur City North</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Devendra Kothe</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur City Central</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Sachin Kalyanshetti</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Akkalkot</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Subhash Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur South</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Samadhan Autade</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Pandharpur</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26757,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">There is no member from the Solapur district currently in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(26759,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Jaykumar Gore (BJP) is the current Guardian Minister for Solapur district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26761,'<p class=\"c5\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Solapur district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c33\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Pune Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Jayant Asgaonkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">INC</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Pune Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Arun Lad</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">NCP (SP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26763,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">The following are the key administrative heads of Solapur district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c33\"><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c15\">Service / Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Kumar Ashirwad</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Sheetal Teli Ugale</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Kuladeep Jangam</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Anirudha Kamble</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c1\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c22\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Atul Kulkarni</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c14\"><span class=\"c9\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26766,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Solapur district.</span></p>'),(26768,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">There are 14 Urban Local Bodies in the Solapur District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 11 Nagar Parishad, and 2 Nagar Panchayat. There are 1031 Gram Panchayats and 11 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26770,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur Zilla Parishad was established in 1962. Currently, it comprises 11 Panchayat Samitis and 1031 Gram Panchayats.</span></p>'),(26772,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur Municipal Corporation is one of the oldest in India, established on 1 Aug 1861 as a Municipal Council and granted Municipal Corporation status in 1963. There are a total of 113 wards under the Corporation. N.M. Wadwan served as the first and longest-serving mayor of the Solapur district. Notably, the INC and BJP are the two major parties in the Municipal Corporation, particularly when it comes to winning margins. These parties have a significant presence and influence in the corporation\'s political landscape.</span></p>'),(26774,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Solapur district has several municipal councils (Nagar Parishads) that serve as urban local governance bodies. These include Akkalkot Nagar Parishad, Barshi Nagar Parishad, Dudhani Nagar Parishad, Karmala Nagar Parishad, Kurduwadi Nagar Parishad, Mahalung-Shripur Nagar Parishad, Mohol Nagar Parishad, Maindargi Nagar Parishad, Mangalvedhe Nagar Parishad, Pandharpur Nagar Parishad, and Sangole Nagar Parishad.</span></p>'),(26776,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">The district also has two Nagar Panchayats or town councils which function as local administrative units in semi-urban areas. These are the Malshiras Nagar Panchayat and the Madha Nagar Panchayat.</span></p>'),(26779,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, a total of 53 contestants contested from the two constituencies. Out of these candidates, 20 candidates had criminal cases against them, with seven having serious cases registered. The contestants with the highest number of criminal cases registered were from the party NCP-SP.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">The elected MP from Madha, Dhairyasheel Rajsinh Mohite-Patil from NCP-SP, has 36 criminal cases against him, and the elected MP from Solapur, Praniti Sushilkumar Shinde from INC, has three cases registered against her.</span></p>'),(26781,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 150 contestants from 11 constituencies. Out of these, 48 contestants had criminal cases against them, with 35% of the 48 contestants having serious cases registered. The political parties, NCP and Vanchit Bahujan Aghadi (VBA), had the highest number of contestants with criminal cases.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">From the 11 elected MLAs, four of them have serious criminal cases registered against them. Rajendra Vitthal Raut from the Barshi Vidhan Sabha constituency has the highest number of criminal cases. The MLAs without any registered cases were from the Solapur City (North), Mohol, Pandharpur, and Sangola Vidhan Sabha Constituencies.</span></p>'),(26784,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">During the Civil Disobedience Movement (1930–34), satyagrahis in Solapur, led by nationalist leader Annasaheb Bhopatkar, hoisted the Indian national flag atop the Municipal building on 6 April, 1930 - the first recorded instance of the flag being raised on a municipal structure.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">When news of Mahatma Gandhi’s arrest reached the city, widespread protests erupted. Strikes were called, government buildings were attacked, and railway lines were damaged. The intensity of the uprising forced the British authorities to temporarily abandon Solapur. From 9 to 11 May 1930, local leaders took over the city’s administration and symbolically declared independence from colonial rule.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">This brief but powerful assertion of self-rule was quickly crushed. The British reoccupied Solapur, imposed martial law, and launched a harsh crackdown. Numerous citizens were arrested and tortured. In a widely condemned act of reprisal, four freedom fighters - Mallappa Dhanshetty, Jagannath Shinde, Kisan Sarda, and Kurban Hussain - were executed after being falsely implicated in the killing of police personnel.</span></p>'),(26786,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Ramakrishna Jaju grew up during a time of immense political upheaval, as India was under British colonial rule. Inspired by the call for Independence, Jaju became involved in the freedom movement at a young age. His activism began with participation in protests and demonstrations against British rule. He aligned himself with the Indian National Congress, the leading political party fighting for India\'s independence. Under the influence of Mahatma Gandhi, Jaju embraced the principles of nonviolence and civil disobedience as effective methods to challenge British authority.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">As his involvement in the freedom struggle deepened, Jaju emerged as a prominent leader within the Congress party, particularly in the Solapur region of Maharashtra. He played a key role in organizing grassroots movements, mobilizing support, and rallying public opinion against colonial rule.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Jaju was also deeply inspired by the 1914 conference held in Solapur, which motivated him to engage in constructive social work. He showed immense dedication during epidemics like plague, cholera, and influenza, personally delivering medicines, clothes, and food to affected households. In 1917, he founded the Marwari Library and Gymnasium and was actively involved in the Harijan Seva Sangh and Charkha Sangh. During the 1922-23 Nagpur Flag Satyagraha, which garnered nationwide attention, Jaju became the first person from the Solapur district to join the movement led by Jamnalal Bajaj. For his involvement, he was sentenced to one year of hard labor and fined Rs. 100, serving his term in Khandwa Jail.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In addition to his political activism, Jaju was dedicated to social reform and upliftment. He understood the importance of education in empowering marginalized communities and worked tirelessly to promote education and social justice throughout his life. His effective management of government affairs and maintenance of order during martial law earned him the title \"Marshal Jaju.\" Jaju\'s active participation in the freedom movement and the punishments he endured for it became a recurring theme throughout his life, as he remained committed to India\'s independence and social progress.</span></p>'),(26788,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Meenakshi R. Karadkar was a courageous labor leader from Solapur, married to Raghunath Govind Karadkar, a prominent Communist leader in the region. In April 1934, during a workers\' strike protesting reduced wages, she was arrested along with other leaders when mill owners, backed by the British government, violently tried to suppress the strike. For her role in organizing labor meetings and leading a procession at a temple, she was sentenced to five months in prison and fined Rs. 200. Although acquitted of the charges related to the meetings, she was found guilty of participating in the procession and faced additional prison time. Despite these challenges, she remained undeterred, continuing her activism by speaking at events and advocating for workers\' rights, even during her time in jail.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">As the secretary of the Beedi Workers Union of Solapur, Meenakshi Karadkar also pushed for improved conditions for female detainees and fought for better pay, working hours, and overall rights for workers. Unlike some leaders of her time, she was not in favor of a purely non-violent path to Independence. She actively encouraged the youth to take part in the national struggle, supporting the Congress in their fight for India\'s freedom.</span></p>'),(26790,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Dwarkabai Deshpande (1909–1989) was a prominent figure in Solapur’s freedom struggle, known for her dedication to social and national service. Married at a young age, she pursued her passion for serving others by completing a nursing course in Indore before settling in Solapur. Inspired by fellow freedom fighter Ramabai Pitre, Dwarkabai joined the INC and became actively involved in its activities. She courageously participated in Mahatma Gandhi’s Satyagraha movement, joining marches and protests despite the personal hardships she faced.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In 1942, during the Quit India Movement, she was sentenced to eight months in jail for her participation in the movement. When unable to pay a fine, her sentence was extended by an additional two months. Dwarkabai made history on 26 January 1943 in Ahmedabad, when she led twelve female prisoners in a protest at the jail gate. She boldly hoisted the Indian flag, marking a significant moment in Solapur’s history.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">After her release, Dwarkabai continued her commitment to public service by working in the Congress Seva Dal. In 1943, she became the president of the Congress Women\'s Association, furthering her efforts to mobilize women in the freedom struggle. She also became the first president of the Mahila Mandal, an organization founded by Smt. Kirloskar and Comrade Meenakshi Sane in 1944. Through this platform, Dwarkabai worked tirelessly to raise awareness and improve the lives of women.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">A staunch follower of Mahatma Gandhi, she promoted the use of Khadi, symbolizing her belief in self-reliance and the spirit of the independence movement. Understanding the importance of education for the poor and underprivileged, even after independence, Dwarkabai founded the Rashtra Vikas Balak Mandir to ensure continued education for children. She remained a lifelong advocate for girls\' education, dedicating herself to empowering women through learning. Dwarkabai Deshpande passed away on 14 September, 1989.</span></p>'),(26793,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Since 2023, districts such as Pune, Solapur, and Kolhapur have experienced protests held by members of the Dhangar community asking to be included in the ST category. The community claims that the Dhangad tribe, which has ST status, is identical to the Dhangars and that a “spelling mistake has cost them the ST status”. The Dhangars are on Maharashtra’s list of Vimukta Jati and Nomadic Tribes (VJNT), but have been demanding Scheduled Tribe (ST) status for decades. According to Dhangar leaders, the community is identified as “Dhangad” elsewhere in the country, and gets reservation as an ST. On 21 September 2023, Members of the Dhangar community held a protest at the Ankali Toll Naka on the Sangli-Kolhapur border. Obstructing traffic for about half an hour.</span></p>'),(26795,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Between September and October 2023, several districts in Maharashtra witnessed widespread protests led by Jarange Patil, a native of Jalna, advocating for the inclusion of the Maratha community in the Other Backward Classes (OBC) category for reservations in government jobs and education. In August 2024, protesters blocked the convoy of NCP-SP President Sharad Pawar and insisted on a clear position regarding Maratha reservation.</span></p>'),(26798,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Ganpatrao Deshmukh was a towering figure in Maharashtra politics and a lifelong leader of the Peasants and Workers Party of India (PWP). Affectionately known as \"Abbasaheb\" and \"Bhai,\" he earned deep respect among farmers and labourers for his unwavering commitment to their rights and welfare.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Deshmukh represented the Sangola constituency in Solapur district for an extraordinary 11 terms as a Member of the Legislative Assembly (MLA), spanning over 54 years. His last electoral victory came in the 2014 Maharashtra Assembly elections, when, at the age of 88, he secured 94,374 votes and won the seat for the 11th time.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">He briefly served as a minister in the First Sharad Pawar-led government in 1978 and later played a key role in aligning the PWP with the Congress–NCP alliance in 1999. A founding member of the PWP, Deshmukh remained a steadfast advocate for agrarian and working-class interests throughout his life.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">His decades-long political journey has left a lasting legacy in the history of Maharashtra’s grassroots politics.</span></p>'),(26800,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Ramabai Pitre (1898–1987), born in Halshi, Khanapur taluka, Sangli district, moved to Solapur after marrying Narasimha Pitre at the age of thirteen. Tragically, her husband passed away just three years later, leaving her to raise a child alone. After completing a nursing course, she settled in Solapur, where her commitment to the freedom struggle began to take shape.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Her brother, Ramchandra, and Dr. Narasimha, who printed bulletins supporting the freedom movement from their home, greatly influenced her. Inspired by their activism and the death of Lokmanya Tilak, Ramabai vowed to dedicate herself to the cause of Indian independence. She became involved with prominent leaders like Dr. K.B. Hedgewar, Antrolikar, Rambhau Rajwade, and Tulsidas Jadhav, and actively participated in protests, marches, and boycotts.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Ramabai delivered her first public speech in 1932 during Mahatma Gandhi\'s civil disobedience movement, becoming Solapur\'s first female satyagrahi. For her activism, she was sentenced to two years in prison and fined Rs 50. When asked to explain her crime, she famously replied, \"Is it a crime to do something for the benefit of our country?\" This defiant response led to her sentence being extended by 50 more days.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">During her imprisonment at Yerwada Jail in Pune, Ramabai displayed her compassion by caring for leprosy patients in the jail barracks. After her release, she continued her service to society by establishing Solapur\'s first maternity hospital in 1935.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Ramabai\'s commitment to the freedom movement deepened in 1942, during the Quit India movement, when she undertook dangerous tasks to support underground freedom fighters. She also lent her support to the freedom struggle in Hyderabad, continuing her activism until 1948. Her contributions were recognized when she won the Solapur municipal elections in 1942, and she was re-elected twice more in 1946.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Ramabai Pitre passed away on 24 February 1987, leaving behind a legacy of courage, compassion, and tireless service to the nation.</span></p>'),(26802,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Surajratan Fatehchand Damani was a distinguished figure who combined political service with business acumen and philanthropic commitment. He represented the Solapur constituency in the 4th, 5th, and 6th Lok Sabha, actively participating in national politics during a formative period for post-independence India.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In the business world, Damani held prominent leadership roles as Chairman of several major enterprises, including Simplex Mills Company in Bombay, Jamsri Ranjit Singhji Spinning and Weaving Mills in Solapur, and Nav Bharat Refrigeration and Industries in New Delhi. His contributions helped shape the industrial landscape in Maharashtra and beyond.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">A committed philanthropist, Damani established a charitable trust that supported a wide range of social initiatives. These included funding educational institutions, setting up free maternity and child welfare centers, offering scholarships for higher studies, and contributing to broader community development efforts.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Politically, he was an influential member of the INC, serving on the party’s Parliamentary Executive Committee and as its treasurer between 1969 and 1971. His legacy spans the spheres of industry, public service, and social welfare.</span></p>'),(26804,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Tulsidas Jadhav was an Indian freedom fighter, political activist, social reformer, farmer, and member of both the Bombay Legislative Council and the Lok Sabha. He was associated with the INC from 1921 to 1947 and was a devoted follower of Mahatma Gandhi. During the Satyagraha movement, Jadhav\'s courage was evident when an officer pointed a pistol at his chest and ordered him to leave. Jadhav refused to back down, he was ultimately let off without harm. His close association with Gandhi led him to serve as Gandhi\'s secretary during his imprisonment at Yerwada Jail in 1932.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">After India\'s independence, Jadhav left the Congress in 1947 and became a founding member of the PWP. He won the 1951 Bombay Assembly election, representing the Barshi-Madha constituency. Although he rejoined the Congress in 1957, he lost the election for the Solapur constituency in the 2nd Lok Sabha.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">As a social reformer, Jadhav dedicated his life to the upliftment of Harijan and Dalit communities, beginning his work in the 1930s and continuing throughout his active years. In recognition of his contributions, a teacher training school in Solapur, the Tulsidas Jadhav Adhyapak Vidyalay, was named in his honor.</span></p>'),(26806,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Nirmalaraje Bhosale, originally from the royal Bhosale family of Akkalkot, was one of the early women legislators and a prominent cabinet member from Solapur district. She first represented the North Solapur constituency from 1952 to 1957 and later the Akkalkot constituency from 1962 to 1967. Throughout her political career, she was an active member and dedicated worker of the Congress Party.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In 1957, Nirmalaraje was appointed as the Deputy Minister for Education in Bombay State. By 1962, she had risen to the position of Cabinet Minister for Social Welfare in Maharashtra, continuing her work in education and social welfare reform during her tenure.</span></p>'),(26808,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Prabhavati Zadbuke was elected as a legislator from the Barshi constituency in Solapur district for two consecutive terms, from 1962 to 1967 and from 1967 to 1972. Before her legislative role, she served as the Chairman of the Municipal School Board, where she focused on improving education by constructing new school buildings, enhancing educational standards, and upgrading facilities for libraries and sports activities.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">During her decade-long tenure as a legislator, she oversaw various development projects in Barshi and the surrounding areas. Notably, she contributed to the development of the Koyna Electricity Center with a capacity of 110 kW. She also played a key role in the completion of 131 km of tarred road between Kurduvadi and Latur, and 66 km of road between Barshi and Solapur. Several bridges were constructed in Pangari, Tadawale, and Namgave under her leadership, and S.T. bus stands were established to improve transportation for residents and boost local businesses.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Prabhavati Zadbuke had a strong interest in rural development, recognizing that farmers are the backbone of the country and democracy. She believed that the progress of farmers was essential to the overall development of India. Her dedication, hard work, and focus on rural progress made her a diligent and successful legislator from the Barshi constituency.</span></p>'),(26810,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Shrimati Parvati Malgonda served as a legislator in the Maharashtra Assembly from 1980 to 1985, representing the Akkalkot constituency in Solapur district. Born on 29 September 1933, in Akkalkot, she came from a strong background in social work before transitioning into politics. She held a B.A. (Hons.) and B.Ed. Degree and was proficient in multiple languages, including Marathi, Hindi, English, and Kannada.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">During her time as a legislator, Malgonda focused on rural development, with a particular emphasis on improving the welfare of women and marginalized communities. She recognized the urgent need for proper medical care in villages, especially for women during pregnancy and childbirth. Malgonda advocated for at least two health centers per taluka, with additional sub-centers in remote areas to ensure better access to healthcare. She also called for the establishment of maternity wards in rural areas, staffed with medical professionals, and funded through government grants to ensure pregnant women across Maharashtra could receive proper care.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Sanitation was another key concern for Malgonda, as many rural areas lacked latrines, which created significant difficulties for women. She pressed the government to allocate funding for the construction of sanitary facilities in these regions. Additionally, Malgonda supported providing economic assistance for disabled individuals and worked to ensure that such support was made universally available across the state.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">In her efforts to boost the local economy, Malgonda also championed the cotton industry. She advocated for integrating cotton producers and workers into the broader cotton industry to expand market opportunities and strengthen the sector. Furthermore, she welcomed proposed housing projects for ordinary citizens but emphasized the need for specific budgetary provisions focused on the social and economic development of women.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Throughout her career, Malgonda remained dedicated to inclusive rural development. Her work centered on addressing the needs of women, the disabled, and disadvantaged groups, while improving healthcare, sanitation, and economic opportunities in Maharashtra’s rural communities.</span></p>'),(26812,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Sushilkumar Shinde had a diverse and dynamic career that spanned various fields. He initially started as a bailiff in a Solapur court before transitioning into the Maharashtra Police, where he served as a sub-inspector for six years. His entry into politics was influenced by Sharad Pawar, who suggested that he join the political arena. Their friendship developed when Mr. Shinde was investigating a case in Mumbai, and Mr. Pawar was a prominent leader in the Congress Party\'s state unit. This pivotal moment occurred in 1971.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Mr. Shinde\'s political journey took off when he won his first election to the Maharashtra Assembly in 1974 from a reserved constituency, Karmala, located in his home district of Solapur. Shortly thereafter, in 1975, then Maharashtra Chief Minister VP Naik appointed him as a junior minister in the government. From this point on, Mr. Shinde\'s political career continued to flourish.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Over the years, he achieved significant electoral success, winning Maharashtra Assembly elections in 1978, 1980, 1985, and 1990. During his tenure as a legislator, he presented nine budgets as the state\'s finance minister, working alongside different chief ministers. In Maharashtra\'s political landscape, where the powerful Marathas traditionally held sway, Mr. Shinde faced significant challenges as a Dalit leader. However, he defied the odds and made history by becoming the state\'s first Dalit Chief Minister in 2003. This milestone occurred when his friend Vilasrao Deshmukh stepped down from the position.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">Mr. Shinde\'s political journey continued as he served as the Governor of Andhra Pradesh and later returned to the national stage by winning a Rajya Sabha seat in 2006. He took on the role of the Power Minister during his time in the central government. His remarkable career is a testament to his determination and resilience, breaking barriers and achieving significant milestones in Maharashtra\'s political landscape. He is considered one of the policy makers in the Congress Party and close to the Gandhi family.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c9\">His daughter Praniti Shinde is a member of the INC and has been elected as a Member of Legislative Assembly (MLA) in Maharashtra three times, representing the Solapur City Central constituency. Additionally, she has held the position of Working President of the Maharashtra Pradesh Congress Committee since 2021. She is also a member of the screening committee of the Kerala Pradesh Congress Committee for assembly elections, showcasing her active involvement in party affairs and elections.</span></p>'),(26820,'<p class=\"c37 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c22\">LOCAL POLITICS: THANE</span></p>'),(26822,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The region now known as Thane district became part of British India in 1817, when the British acquired it from the Peshwa. Initially, it was incorporated into the North Konkan district, with its headquarters at Thana. In 1830, North Konkan was expanded by merging parts of the South Konkan district, and in 1833, it was officially renamed Thane District.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Significant administrative changes followed over the next century. In 1853, the subdivisions of Pen, Roha, and Mahad, along with the Underi and Revadanda agencies of Kolaba, were grouped into the sub-collectorate of Kolaba under Thana. This region was later separated to form the independent Kolaba district (now Raigad) in 1869.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">After Independence, the princely state of Jawhar was merged into Thane district in 1949 and established as a separate taluka. Further territorial adjustments occurred in 1956 when twenty-seven villages and eight towns from Borivali taluka, along with one town and one village from Thana taluka, were incorporated into Bombay Suburban district due to the northward expansion of Greater Bombay into Salsette Island.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Following the formation of Maharashtra in 1960, the Umbargaon taluka underwent reorganization. Forty-seven villages and three towns were transferred to Surat district in Gujarat, while the remaining twenty-seven villages were included in Dahanu taluka, and later, in 1961, carved out as a separate mahal, Talasari.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">On 1 August 2014, the state of Maharashtra created its 36th district - Palghar - by carving it out of the northern part of Thane district, marking the most recent territorial reconfiguration of the region.</span></p>'),(26825,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Thane district is composed of three Lok Sabha constituencies: Bhiwandi, Kalyan, and Thane. Each of these constituencies is further divided into six Vidhan Sabha segments, resulting in a total of 18 Vidhan Sabha constituencies within the Thane district.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c35\"><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Sabha Seat</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">Lok Sabha Seat</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhiwandi Rural (ST)</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhiwandi</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane &amp; Palghar</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhiwandi East</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhiwandi</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\