wspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane-Palghar Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Konkan Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Dnyaneshwar Mhatre</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c18\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Konkan Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c16\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Niranjan Davkhare</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26841,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the key administrative heads of Thane district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c39\"><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c5\">Service / Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Ashok Shingare </span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Saurabh Rao</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Rohan Ghuge</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Pushpa Borhade‑Patil</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c14\"><td class=\"c7\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Commissioner of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c20\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Ashutosh Dumbare</span></p></td><td class=\"c33\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Police Services (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26844,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Thane district.</span></p>'),(26846,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">There are 11 Urban Local Bodies in the Thane District: 6 Mahanagar Palika, 7 Nagar Parishad, 1 Nagar Panchayat. There are 704 Gram Panchayats and 5 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26848,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane Zilla Parishad was established on 1 October 1982 and currently has 54 members. It is worth noting that in 1972, Tarabai Vartak, representing INC, became the first woman in India to hold the position of Zila Parishad Chief when she was elected as the President of Thane Zila Parishad. Over the years, the Zilla Parishad has primarily been dominated by the BJP, INC, or NCP. The 2018 elections were the first instance in which Shiv Sena managed to win the presidency of the Zilla Parishad.</span></p>'),(26850,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane District has six Municipal Corporations: Thane, Mira-Bhayandar, Navi Mumbai, Kalyan-Dombivli, Ulhasnagar, and Bhiwandi-Nizampur.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Thane Municipal Corporation was established in 1982 and currently comprises 131 seats. Prior to gaining Municipal Corporation status, it functioned as a Municipal Council, with Vasant Marathe serving as its first chairman. Since its formation, political control has largely oscillated between the BJP and the SHS. In the most recent election held in 2017, the SHS secured a majority, winning 51% of the seats.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Mira-Bhayandar Municipal Corporation came into existence in February 2002 and has 96 seats. Much like in Thane, political control has primarily alternated between the BJP and the SHS. In the 2017 elections, the BJP emerged as the dominant party, winning 65 of the total seats.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Navi Mumbai Municipal Corporation was formally constituted on 1 January 1992 and currently has 111 seats. Political power in this body has historically shifted between the NCP and the SHS. In the 2017 elections, the NCP secured a clear majority, winning over 50% of the seats.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Kalyan-Dombivli Municipal Corporation, also established on 1 January 1982, currently holds 133 seats. Like other corporations in the region, it has seen political dominance by the BJP and the SHS. In the 2017 elections, the SHS won over half of the total seats. The elections also marked the rise of the All India Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen (AIMIM), which won four seats and emerged as a new political player in the corporation.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Ulhasnagar Municipal Corporation was set up in 2002, although the area’s administrative roots trace back to its establishment as a military transit camp during World War II, meant to house around 6,000 soldiers and 30,000 civilians. The corporation presently includes 78 seats. The INC and the BJP have traditionally been the main contenders here. In 2017, the INC won 47 seats, while smaller parties like the Samajwadi Party also managed to gain a foothold.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Bhiwandi-Nizampur Municipal Corporation was formed in 2002 and currently has 90 seats. Political control has largely shifted between the INC and the SHS. In the 2017 elections, the INC secured 47 seats, while the Samajwadi Party also made significant gains in the area.</span></p>'),(26852,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane district has several municipal councils (Nagar Parishads) that serve as urban local governance bodies. These include Ambernath Nagar Parishad, Dahanu Nagar Parishad, and Badlapur Nagar Parishad.</span></p>'),(26854,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The district also has two Nagar Panchayats or town councils which function as local administrative units in semi-urban areas. These are the Murbad Nagar Panchayat and the Shahapur Nagar Panchayat.</span></p>'),(26856,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Thane district has several Panchayat Samitis that operate as intermediate-level rural local governance bodies within the zilla parishad system. These include the Ambernath Panchayat Samiti, Murbad Panchayat Samiti, Kalyan Panchayat Samiti, Bhiwandi Panchayat Samiti, and Shahapur Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26859,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 79 candidates from Thane district (28 from Kalyan, 24 from Thane, and 27 from Bhiwandi). Out of these, 16 candidates had criminal cases against them, with six of them having serious cases registered. The elected MPs of Kalyan and Bhiwandi had no criminal cases registered against them, while the elected MP from Thane had two cases registered against him.</span></p>'),(26861,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 184 candidates from Thane. Out of these, 77 candidates had criminal cases against them, with around 75% of the 77 candidates having serious cases registered. The political parties, NCP and SHS, had the highest number of candidates with criminal cases. The only constituencies with candidates without criminal cases were Bhiwandi Rural (ST) and Mira Bhayandar.</span></p>'),(26864,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The 1970 Bhiwandi riots were religious riots that occurred between 7 and 8 May in the Indian towns of Bhiwandi, Jalgaon, and Mahad, between Hindus and Muslims. The riots caused the deaths of over 250 people. According to the Justice Madon commission, 142 Muslims and 20 Hindus had been killed in Bhiwandi alone, and 50 Muslims and 17 Hindus in the surrounding areas.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">There had been a prolonged period of tension between Indian Nationalist groups such as the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS), Bharatiya Jana Sangh, and Shiv Sena, and the Muslim radical groups Jamaat-i-Islami, the Muslim League, and Majlis Tameer-e-Millat. The Rashtriya Utsav Mandal had campaigned for permission to have a procession to celebrate the birthday of the Maratha warrior-king Shivaji, which would pass through an area where the residents were predominantly Muslims, and by a mosque. Permission was given over the protests of Muslim leaders, and on 7 May, the procession began. The procession was organised by Shiv Sena and supporters of the Hindu right, who, it was reported, arrived armed with lathis. Between 3,000 and 4,000 people had travelled from villages close to Bhiwandi, and once the procession began, some Muslims threw stones, which triggered the violence. Once the violence began, the police opened fire several times. An indefinite curfew was declared by the police at 10:00 p.m. that night.</span></p>'),(26866,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">On 17 March 2009, an accident between a car and an autorickshaw near Saraswati School led to a communal riot. There were conflicting reports about how the accident escalated into a communal riot, but the police claimed that clashes broke out after the car\'s owner, Mohan Deodhar, sought help from a couple of youths from the area to pin down the autorickshaw driver. Another group, presumably Muslims, sided with the auto driver, and the argument turned into a fist fight and subsequent clashes. Deodhar, however, refuted these claims. Nonetheless, the clashes that ensued left 26 people injured, including 18 policemen, and also resulted in damage to property and vehicles. Thirty-nine people were arrested for rioting, and 14 others were held as a preventive measure.</span></p>'),(26868,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">On 22 January 2024, Naya Nagar was gripped in tension after a mob of around 50 people forcibly stopped and vandalized some vehicles carrying ‘Jai Shri Ram’ flags when they were passing through the locality. The Mira Road police arrested 21 people in connection with the incident. It was also reported that unidentified persons vandalised some shops in Kashimira, Naya Nagar, and Navghar areas.</span></p>'),(26871,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Anand Chintaman Dighe, one of the esteemed founding members of Shiv Sena, embarked on his illustrious political career at the age of 18. Together with Balasaheb Thakare, he laid the foundation for the party in 1966. Anand Dighe held Bal Thakare in the highest regard and considered him his Guru (Mentor). During the formative years of SHS, he stood as a pivotal member of the party\'s intellectual core. He demonstrated remarkable enthusiasm in organizing various public rallies for Bal Thakare, further solidifying his role within the Shiv Sena.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1984, Anand Dighe assumed the presidency of the Thane unit of SHS. Within Thane, he wielded considerable influence and earned the endearing title of \"Dharmaveer\" due to his unwavering commitment to ethical principles across a spectrum of social issues. It was said that he established his own \"justice system\" at Tembhe Naka in Thane, grounded firmly in ethical and moral tenets. He was also affectionately referred to as the \"Bal Thakare of Thane.\"</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">He firmly believed that the strength of any political movement lay in its organization and the dedication of its karyakartas (party workers). Consequently, he placed great emphasis on cultivating robust local Shiv Sena organizations known as Shakhas (local party units). His indefatigable work ethic extended beyond daylight hours to include nights, earning him a reputation as a superhuman figure with extraordinary problem-solving abilities. He was renowned for his unyielding stance against wrongdoing, adhering firmly to the principle of \"Chukila maafi naahi\" (No mercy to wrongdoers). An accusation of masterminding the demise of a Congress leader led to his incarceration for a period. Anand Dighe\'s fearless reputation extended even to encounters with members of the underworld, and he became widely recognized as the \"Lion of Thane.\" He played a pivotal role in mentoring Eknath Shinde, who later ascended to the position of Chief Minister of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">His distinctive working style involved the swift resolution of issues through his extensive network and contacts. It was rumored that he wielded influence even within the government administration, with government officials purportedly heeding his directives. Although he refrained from contesting elections, he played an instrumental role in nurturing leaders like Eknath Shinde, Rajan Vichare, and numerous others. Throughout his political journey, Anand Dighe remained brimming with energy, but his life was tragically cut short at the age of 50. Tragically, Anand Dighe passed away under suspicious circumstances in the hospital. His death is still a mystery. The news of his sudden passing incited public anger to such an extent that the hospital where he breathed his last was set ablaze. To commemorate his life and teachings, Mr. Eknath Shinde produced a biopic titled \"Dharmaveer\" (Protector of Dharma). The acclaimed National Award-winning actor and director, Prasad Oak, portrayed the role of Anand Dighe, a portrayal lauded by all.</span></p>'),(26873,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Eknath Shinde, an influential leader from Thane, has served as the Deputy Chief Minister of Maharashtra since December 2024, alongside Ajit Pawar under Chief Minister Devendra Fadnavis. He also holds the portfolio of Minister of Housing in the state cabinet.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Shinde hails from a modest background, born into a farming family in the Satara district. In his early years, he worked as an auto-rickshaw driver to support his household. His political journey began when he was drawn to the Shiv Sena’s Marathi-centric vision under Bal Thackeray. A crucial turning point came when he met Anand Dighe, the charismatic Shiv Sena leader in Thane, who became his mentor and guide.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Shinde faced personal tragedy when two of his children drowned in an accident, leading to a period of deep despair. It was Dighe’s support that helped him recover and re-engage with public life. Since then, Shinde’s political rise has been marked by resilience and grassroots connect, traits often associated with both Thackeray and Dighe. He has also been credited with playing a role in curbing underworld activities in Thane and Mumbai.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">He has been elected six times to the Maharashtra Vidhan Sabha from the Thane constituency, winning in 2004, 2009, 2014, 2019, and 2024. Over the years, he has handled key ministries such as Urban Development, Public Works (Public Undertakings), Public Health, and Home Affairs, and also served as Leader of the Opposition briefly in 2014. In 2005, he was appointed Thane District Chief of Shiv Sena.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">A close aide of Uddhav Thackeray for many years, Shinde grew increasingly disillusioned with the direction of the party after its 2019 alliance with the Congress and NCP. Although there was speculation that he would be projected as the Chief Ministerial face of the Maha Vikas Aghadi (MVA), Uddhav Thackeray took the post. Shinde grew vocal about the influence of alliance partners in cabinet decisions, especially in the post-COVID period, which deepened the internal rift.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2022, Shinde led a dramatic political realignment by moving 40 MLAs to Guwahati, aligning with the BJP, and precipitating a split in the Shiv Sena. With legislative majority on his side, he was sworn in as Chief Minister of Maharashtra, replacing Uddhav Thackeray. During his tenure, he emphasized administrative responsiveness and earned the moniker “Anathancha Nath” (Protector of Orphans) for his pro-people image.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Following the 2024 assembly elections, Shinde stepped down as Chief Minister and accepted the post of Deputy Chief Minister, continuing his alliance with the BJP while heading the SHS (Shinde faction).</span></p>'),(26875,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Pratap Sarnaik is a Shiv Sena politician, businessman, and social worker based in Thane. He began his political career in 1997 with his election as a Corporator in the Thane Municipal Corporation (TMC), a position he held for two terms. During his tenure, he undertook several civic projects including the development of an eco-garden, a Mahila Bachat Gat Bhavan, an open auditorium, and a welfare centre for senior citizens. He was also involved in infrastructure upgrades such as road concretization.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Sarnaik has been elected three times as a Member of the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from the Ovala-Majiwada constituency, winning in 2009, 2014, and 2019. A key figure in the SHS (Eknath Shinde faction), he played a significant role in the political developments of 2022 that led to the formation of the Shinde-led government in alliance with the BJP.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Outside politics, Sarnaik is the chairman of the Vihang Group of Companies, with interests in hospitality and real estate. He is also known for organizing youth-oriented entrepreneurship programs, such as Ya Tarunano Ya... Chala Life Ghadvu Ya (“Come youngsters, let’s create life”), aimed at inspiring young entrepreneurs.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Sarnaik is widely recognized for organizing large-scale Dahi Handi celebrations in Thane, which draw participants from across India and abroad. His events are among the most prominent in the state, with high prize money and mass public participation. His lobbying efforts contributed to the Maharashtra government\'s 2022 recognition of Dahi Handi as a sport, thereby extending sports category benefits - including health insurance - to participating Govindas. In 2023, he launched the Govinda Premier League (GPL), modeled on the Indian Premier League (IPL), to promote the tradition among younger audiences.</span></p>'),(26877,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Ramchandra Kashinath Mhalgi, a lawyer and United Maharashtra organization activist, was the first Janata Party MP elected from Thane. He began his journey in association with the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) during his youth. Between 1950 and 1955, he held the position of regional secretary within the RSS. Subsequently, he transitioned to working with the Akhil Bharatiya Vidyarthi Parishad and dedicated a substantial portion of his life serving as a Pracharak for the RSS at various locations.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">His entry into the realm of politics commenced under the banner of the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP). In the 1957 Vidhan Sabha elections, he contested from the Maval constituency as part of the United Maharashtra organization (Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti) and emerged as the victor. From 1967 to 1972, he represented the Shukrawar Peth Vidhansabha seat. In 1980, he secured election to the Lok Sabha from the Thane Lok Sabha constituency as a member of the Bhartiya Lok Dal in 1977. Following the establishment of the Bharatiya Janata Party in 1980, he assumed the role of the first president of the BJP’s Maharashtra unit.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Rambhau Mhalagi was renowned for his formidable organizational skills and exceptional oratory prowess. Party leaders frequently enlisted his services to train young party workers and elected representatives. His speeches in the Lok Sabha, particularly during the period of the emergency against Prime Minister Indira Gandhi, continue to serve as instructive references for numerous politicians. He faced arrest under the Maintenance of Internal Security Act (MISA) due to his influential public appeals during the emergency.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Anand Hardikar, the author of \"Lokottar Jeevanvrutti,\" a biography chronicling the life of Rambhau Mhalagi, underscores his tireless dedication to guiding and mentoring young BJP cadres during the party\'s nascent years. This mentorship proved instrumental in nurturing leaders of stature such as Pramod Mahajan and Gopinath Munde. The legacy of Rambhau Mhalgi lives on through the Rambhau Mhalgi Prabodhini in Mumbai. The Prabodhini continues to uphold Rambhau Mhalagi\'s principles by nurturing future leaders who will steer the nation forward. Widely acclaimed as one of the premier institutes for leadership development, the Prabodhini provides training to Members of Parliament (MPs), Members of Legislative Assembly (MLAs), including ministers, local government representatives, students, professionals, and a diverse array of individuals. Distinguished leaders such as Devendra Fadnavis and Vinay Sahastrabudhe have served as presidents of the Prabodhini, underscoring its pivotal role in shaping the nation\'s leadership landscape.</span></p>'),(26879,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Dr. Vinay Sahasrabuddhe is a political thinker, parliamentarian, and institutional leader known for his work in policy training, democratic governance, and cultural diplomacy. With a background in English literature and political science, he earned his PhD from the University of Mumbai with a dissertation titled \"Political Parties as Victims of Populism and Electoral Compulsions: A Quest for Systemic Solutions.\"</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">His political career began in 1983 with the Akhil Bharatiya Vidyarthi Parishad (ABVP), the student wing of the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS), where he rose to the position of General Secretary. In 1988, he became the founding president of the Rambhau Mhalgi Prabodhini (RMP), a unique training and research institution focused on public leadership, policy, and governance. Over the years, he has mentored thousands of public representatives, including many senior BJP leaders. His book Beyond a Billion Ballots was launched by Narendra Modi, who referred to him as a mentor and guide.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Dr. Sahasrabuddhe served as a Rajya Sabha MP from Maharashtra from 2016 to 2022, representing the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP). During his tenure, he was actively involved in parliamentary debates, policy training, and cultural initiatives.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2020, he was appointed as the President of the Indian Council for Cultural Relations (ICCR), an autonomous body under the Ministry of External Affairs, where he worked to strengthen India\'s cultural diplomacy. He has also served on the Management Council of the University of Mumbai and was Vice President of the Asiatic Society of Mumbai from 2012 to 2015.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">A prolific writer, Dr. Sahasrabuddhe has authored and edited several books in Marathi and English. In 2024, he was appointed as the President of the BJP’s Thane unit, further expanding his role in state politics.</span></p>'),(26886,'<p class=\"c20 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c29\">LOCAL POLITICS: WARDHA</span></p>'),(26888,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Wardha region has historically been ruled by a succession of dynasties, including the Mauryas, Shungas, Satavahanas, Rashtrakutas, Vakatakas, and later the Delhi Sultanate. In the 1850s, during British rule, the area - then part of Nagpur district - was incorporated into the Central Provinces. Wardha was carved out as a separate district in 1862.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">At the time of India’s independence in 1947, Wardha remained within the state of Madhya Pradesh. Following the reorganization of states in 1956, it was transferred to the Bombay Province. With the formation of Maharashtra in 1960 under the Bombay Reorganization Act, Wardha became part of the newly created state.</span></p>'),(26891,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Wardha district consists of the Wardha Lok Sabha constituency, which includes six Vidhan Sabha segments: Arvi, Deoli, Dhamangaon Railway, Hinganghat, Morshi, Wardha. Only Dhamangaon Railway and Morshi among the Vidhan Sabha segments are situated in the Amravati district.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c23\"><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Arvi</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Deoli</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Dhamangaon Railway</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Hinganghat</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Morshi</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Amravati</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c10\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Amravati</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Wardha District is represented by Wardha-Chandrapur-Gadhchiroli Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Nagpur Teachers’ Constituency, and the Nagpur Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Wardha district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26894,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Based on records from the delimitation reports by the Election Commission, the given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Wardha Lok Sabha constituency and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(26897,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Wardha district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c23\"><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Amar Sharadrao Kale</span></p></td><td class=\"c37\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Nationalist Congress Party - Sharadchandra Pawar (NCP-SP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26899,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Wardha district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c23\"><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Sumit Wankhede</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Arvi</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Rajesh Bakane</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Deoli-Pulgaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Samir Kunwar</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Hinganghat</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Pankaj Bhoyar</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Pratap Adsad</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Dhamangao Railway</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Amaravati</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c7\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Umesh Yawalkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Morshi</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Amravati</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26901,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">There is no member from Wardha district currently representing Maharashtra in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(26903,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Pankaj Bhoyar (BJP) is the current Guardian Minister for Wardha district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26905,'<p class=\"c9\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Wardha district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c23\"><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha-Chandrapur-Gadhchiroli Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Nagpur Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Sudhakar Adbale</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Independent </span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c15\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Nagpur Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Abhijit Wanjarri</span></p></td><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">INC</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26907,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the key administrative heads of Wardha district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c23\"><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c6\"><span class=\"c5\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Rahul Kardile</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Administrative Services (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Administrative Rule</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Jitin Rahman</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Sarita Gakhare</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c17\"><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Superintendent of Police, Wardha</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Anurag Jain</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Police Services (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26910,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Wardha district.</span></p>'),(26912,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">There are 10 Urban Local Bodies in the Wardha District: 6 Nagar Parishad, 4 Nagar Panchayat. There are 520 Gram Panchayats. Out of the 6 municipalities in the district four i.e. Wardha, Arvi, Hinganghat, Doeli have been established well over a hundred years ago.</span></p>'),(26914,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Wardha Zilla Parishad was established on 1 May 1962. It comprises a total of 50 seats.</span></p>'),(26916,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Wardha district has six municipal councils that administer urban local governance in key towns. These include Wardha Nagar Parishad, which governs the district headquarters, and the Nagar Parishads of Arvi, Deoli, Hinganghat, Pulgaon, and Sindi.</span></p>'),(26918,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In addition to the municipal councils, the district has four town councils, also known as Nagar Panchayats. These are located in Ashti, Samudrapur, Karanja, and Selu. </span></p>'),(26921,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 24 contestants from Wardha. Out of these, three candidates had criminal cases against them, with two of the contestants having serious cases registered. Notably, the elected MP Amar Sharadrao Kale from NCP-SP has no criminal cases against him.</span></p>'),(26923,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were 75 candidates from the six constituencies out of which 20 had criminal cases against them, with seven candidates having serious cases registered. The elected MLA from Morshi Devendra Bhuyar from Swabhimani Paksha and the elected MLA from Dhamangaon Adsad Pratap Arunbhau from BJP had 11 and three cases registered against them respectively.</span></p>'),(26926,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The communal riots of 1925 in British India included sixteen separate incidents across the country. One of these occurred in Arvi, then a small town in Wardha district. The immediate cause of the violence was a private quarrel between two wrestlers, but rising tensions between the Hindu and Muslim communities had created a volatile atmosphere. The riot led to the deaths of three Muslims and left around 40 people - both Hindus and Muslims - injured.</span></p>'),(26928,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The Congress Working Committee has convened in Wardha on several significant occasions, each with important implications for India’s political journey.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">The 1939 meeting was marked by the Congress’s decision not to support the British government in any war-related activities. Congress Ministers were called upon to resign from their positions, and the meeting issued a resolution stating that if Britain genuinely supported a democratic world order, it must first implement democratic freedoms in India.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1942, the Congress Working Committee met again in Wardha, on 14 July. At this meeting, the Committee adopted a resolution demanding complete independence from British rule. The draft warned of a nationwide civil disobedience movement if their demands were not met. This resolution laid the groundwork for what would later become the Quit India Movement, officially launched in Bombay.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2018, the Congress Working Committee returned to Wardha in a symbolic move to commemorate the 150th birth anniversary of Mahatma Gandhi. Breaking from recent tradition, the party chose this historic location to launch its year-long door-to-door campaign titled “Lok Sampark Abhiyan.”</span></p>'),(26931,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Kamalnayan Bajaj, a devoted Gandhian and social reformer, followed in the footsteps of his father, Jamanalal Bajaj, a respected veteran social worker and freedom fighter. Kamalnayan Bajaj\'s commitment to the principles of Mahatma Gandhi and his dedication to social causes were evident in his political career.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">He had the honor of representing the Wardha Lok Sabha constituency on three occasions, serving as a Member of Parliament from 1957 to 1971. His tenure in Parliament provided him with a platform to advocate for the causes he held dear and work towards social reform, in alignment with the Gandhian philosophy of non-violence and constructive change.</span></p>'),(26933,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Ramchandra Ghangare began his political career as a Member of the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly, serving from 1967 to 1972. He later represented the Wardha Lok Sabha constituency during the 10th Lok Sabha from 1991 to 1996. He remains the only Member of Parliament from the Communist Party of India (CPI) to have been elected from Wardha, marking his distinct political presence in the region.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Throughout his career, Ghangare was deeply committed to the upliftment of marginalized and underprivileged communities. His work inspired many social workers, including figures like Sindhutai Sapkal, known for her dedication to orphans and vulnerable groups. His legacy stands as a symbol of principled activism and lifelong commitment to social justice.</span></p>'),(26935,'<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">Datta Meghe is a prominent figure in both politics and education in Maharashtra. His political journey began as a Member of the Maharashtra Legislative Council, representing the Nagpur Vidhan Sabha seat during multiple periods: 1978-1984, 1984-1990, and 2000-2002. His commitment to public service extended nationally, serving as a Member of Parliament for four terms under the Congress party from 1991 to 2014. Additionally, he had the privilege of being a member of the Rajya Sabha from 2000 to 2008. In recent years, he has joined the Bharatiya Janata Party.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c9\"><span class=\"c1\">In the realm of education, Mr. Datta Meghe has made a profound impact. He has taken the lead in establishing numerous CBSE schools, medical colleges, engineering colleges, and IT firms, all with the goal of providing high-quality educational opportunities to rural areas. His contributions to education have played a crucial role in improving access to learning for students in Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26939,'<p class=\"c33 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c26\">LOCAL POLITICS: WASHIM</span></p>'),(26941,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim district was officially formed in 1998 by carving out six talukas from the Akola district - Karanja, Mangrulpir, Risod, Malegaon, Washim, and Manora. Historically, during the time of India’s independence, this region was part of the Berar area within Akola district.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1950, the Berar region was merged into the then-state of Madhya Pradesh. Following the States Reorganisation Act of 1956, its Marathi-speaking areas, including present-day Washim, were incorporated into the Bombay State. Finally, after the Bombay Reorganisation Act of 1960, the Bombay State was divided into Gujarat and Maharashtra, and this region became part of Maharashtra. The formation of Washim as a separate district in 1998 marked the latest step in its administrative evolution.</span></p>'),(26944,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The Washim district consists of the joint Yavatmal-Washim Lok Sabha constituency. Only Karanja and Washim among the Vidhan Sabha segments are situated in the Washim district, with the rest falling in the Yavatmal district. Along with this, the Risod Vidhan Sabha constituency of the Akola Lok Sabha constituency falls in the district.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c24\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Sabha Constituencies</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Karanja</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Digras</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Pusad</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Ralegaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Yavatmal</span></p></td><td class=\"c9\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Washim District is represented by Akola-Washim-Buldhana Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Amravati Teachers’ Constituency, and the Amravati Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Washim district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it. </span></p>'),(26946,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The current chapter focuses on the Yavatmal-Washim Lok Sabha and the Vidhan Sabha segments that comprise it. For other Vidhan Sabha segments that fall in the district, refer to the chapter on Akola Local Politics. </span></p>'),(26948,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Before the formation of the district in 1998, the region was represented by the Washim Lok Sabha constituency. In the latest delimitation, the joint Yavatmal-Washim Lok Sabha constituency was created. The given chart provides an overview of the composition of the Lok Sabha constituencies with the third and fourth delimitation rounds. </span></p>'),(26951,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Washim district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c24\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Sanjay Uttamrao Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c29\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim-Yavatmal</span></p></td><td class=\"c32\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena - Uddhav Balasaheb Thackeray (SHS-UBT)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26953,'<p class=\"c4\"><span>The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Washim district, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c24\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Shyam Khode</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Sai Dahake</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Karanja</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Ashok Wooike</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Ralegaon (ST)</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Anil Mangulkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Yavatmal</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian National Congress (INC)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Sanjay Rathod</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Digras</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Indranil Naik</span></p></td><td class=\"c0\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Pusad</span></p></td><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26955,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">There is no member from the Washim district currently representing Maharashtra in the Rajya Sabha.</span></p>'),(26957,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Hasan Mushrif (NCP) is the current Guardian Minister for Washim district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(26959,'<p class=\"c4\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Washim district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c24\"><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c41\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim/Akola/Buldhana/local authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Vasant Madanlal Khandelwal</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">BJP</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Amaravati Division Teacher Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Kiran Ramrao Sarnaik</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Independent</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c18\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Amravati Division Graduate Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c21\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Dheeraj Lingade</span></p></td><td class=\"c27\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">INC</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26961,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following are the key administrative heads of Washim district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c24\"><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c8\"><span class=\"c5\">Service/Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Buveneswari S.</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Municipal Commissioner</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Mayor</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Vacant (Administrative Rule)</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">–</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad, Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Vaibhav Waghmare</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">President of Zilla Parishad, Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Chandrakant Thakare</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Maha Vikas Aghadi (MVA)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c12\"><td class=\"c23\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Superintendent of Police, Washim District</span></p></td><td class=\"c2\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Anuj Tare</span></p></td><td class=\"c28\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c7\"><span class=\"c1\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(26964,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha constituencies of Wardha district.</span></p>'),(26966,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">There are 9 Urban Local Bodies in the District: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 7 Nagar Parishad, 1 Nagar Panchayat. There are 704 Gram Panchayats and 6 Panchayat Samiti.</span></p>'),(26968,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1869, the Municipal Corporation of Washim was established, which now comes under the Maharashtra Municipalities Act, 1965. The municipal council is composed of 18 members, with two seats each being reserved for the scheduled castes and women.</span></p>'),(26970,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim district has three municipal councils (Nagar Samitis) that administer key urban centres. These include Mangrulpir, Risod, and Washim. These municipalities are responsible for civic amenities, sanitation, local infrastructure, and public welfare services within their respective towns.</span></p>'),(26972,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Washim district has three town councils (Nagar Panchayats) that govern smaller towns. These are Karanja, Malegaon, and Manora. Town councils focus on local development, maintaining basic infrastructure, and implementing government schemes in semi-urban areas.</span></p>'),(26975,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 17 contestants from the Yavatmal-Washim seat. Of these, there were two candidates that had criminal cases against them, with one of the contestants having serious cases registered. The contestant with the highest number of criminal cases registered was an Independent candidate. The elected MP, Sanjay Uttamrao Deshmukh, has no criminal cases against him.</span></p>'),(26977,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 74 contestants from the six  Vidhan Sabha seats that come under the Yavatmal-Washim Lok Sabha seat. Out of these, 14 contestants had criminal cases against them, with 35% of them having serious cases registered.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Of the six elected MLAs, two have serious criminal cases registered against them. Rathod Sanjay Dulichand from Digras Vidhan Sabha constituency has the highest number of criminal cases. The only MLA without any registered cases is Lakhan Sahadeo Malik from Washim (SC) Vidhan Sabha Constituency.</span></p>'),(26980,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Born on 16 April 1907 in Karanja Lad, Washim district, Bankatlalji Kisangopalji Bang pursued his education up to the eighth standard before taking up agriculture as his primary occupation. Deeply inspired by an address delivered by Lokmanya Tilak, he joined the Indian National Congress and actively took part in the Non-Cooperation Movement, Civil Disobedience Movement, and several other satyagrahas. His involvement in the freedom struggle led to multiple arrests, including imprisonment for six months in Akola Central Jail and one year in Nagpur Jail. In recognition of his significant contributions to India’s independence movement, he was awarded a Tamra Patra by the former Prime Minister, the late Smt. Indira Gandhi.</span></p>'),(26982,'<p class=\"c4\"><span>Born in 1922 in the village of Mangarulpir, Damodar Motiramji Rathi was interested in contributing to India’s freedom struggle. Opportunity soon presented itself during the Civil Disobedience movement (1930-34), inspired by Mahatma Gandhi’s vision Damodar actively participated in the movement but he was arrested by the British government and sentenced to one and a half years of rigorous imprisonment along with being imposed with a heavy fine of Rs.1,000/-. He also participated in the Quit India movement (1942-45). Damodar Motiramji Rathi </span><span>passed away</span><span class=\"c1\"> in 1995.</span></p>'),(26984,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In the summer of 2018, due to water shortage, the district administration had diverted water from irrigation projects meant for rural areas to the towns. Protests led by local-rural leaders were carried out against this decision in various places of the district, especially in Selubazaar village and Mangrulpir town, Kokalgaon, Malegaon, and Chakatirth area.</span></p>'),(26986,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2011, it was reported that a couple of youths of the town who were surfing the internet on Sunday morning in a cyber café noticed the picture of a tampered Majsid uploaded by another local youth. The news immediately spread like wildfire. Tension prevailed in the market area of the city when a strong mob set several two-wheelers and a couple of cars afire and damaged at least five shops in protest; no injuries were reported. Later, the boy who uploaded the tampered Masjid picture was arrested by the police.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 2015, following the marriage of a youth from the Muslim community to a girl from the Hindu Community on 4th July (termed as a case of Love Jihad), communal riots erupted at Karanja town on 15th July, resulting in the death of 35-year-old Shujauddin, injuries to many others, and damage to public property.</span></p>'),(26989,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Vasantrao Naik was actively involved in India\'s struggle for independence from a young age. In 1938, he joined the INC and actively participated in numerous movements and protests against British rule. His active involvement in these events often led to his arrest and detention. After India gained independence in 1947, Vasantrao Naik\'s political career began to flourish. In 1952, he was elected to the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly from the Karad constituency. Over the years, he won six consecutive elections and served as a Member of the Legislative Assembly for a total of 32 years.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In 1963, following Yashwantrao Chavan\'s tenure, Vasantrao Naik was appointed as the Chief Minister of Maharashtra. He held this position for 12 years, making him the longest-serving Chief Minister in the state\'s history. During his tenure, he initiated numerous programs and schemes aimed at improving the lives of farmers and rural communities. He was particularly known for his contributions to agricultural development, including the modernization of farming techniques, irrigation systems, and the distribution of high-yield seeds. He also established the Maharashtra State Agricultural Marketing Board to encourage the sale of agricultural products.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">In addition to his focus on agriculture, Vasantrao Naik played a crucial role in rural development. He introduced the \"Gram Sabha\" system, which involved local communities in decision-making processes at the village level. To promote rural development, he established the Maharashtra State Rural Development Board. Vasantrao Naik\'s contributions extended to the field of education as well. He founded several universities and educational institutions, including the Marathwada Vidyapeeth in Aurangabad, the Konkan Agricultural University in Dapoli, and various universities affiliated with Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar Marathwada University in Aurangabad. His commitment to expanding access to education led to the establishment of numerous colleges and schools in rural areas.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Furthermore, Vasantrao Naik made significant efforts to promote the Marathi language. On 1 May 1966, he declared Marathi as the official language of Maharashtra, a historic decision that reinforced the importance of the Marathi language in the state. During his tenure as Chief Minister, he also faced the challenge of the India-Pakistan war in 1965. He delivered inspiring and motivating speeches during this time, contributing to a sense of unity and determination among the people of Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(26991,'<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">Bhavana Gawali is the daughter of veteran political leader Pundalik Gawali and is carrying forward his political legacy. Her political journey began in 1999 when she was elected as a Member of the 13th Lok Sabha for her first term. During her tenure, she served on various committees, including Commerce (1999–2000) and Empowerment of Women (2000–2001). In 2004, she was re-elected to the 14th Lok Sabha for her second term. </span></p>\n<p class=\"c4\"><span class=\"c1\">From 2007 to 2009, she contributed as a Member of the Committee on Health &amp; Family Welfare. Her commitment to public service led to her third term as she was re-elected to the 15th Lok Sabha in 2009 and served on the Committee on Transport, Tourism, and Culture. She continued to represent her constituency and was re-elected to the 16th Lok Sabha in 2014. Additionally, in September 2014, she was appointed as a Member of the Standing Committee on Empowerment of Women and Human Resource Development. Her dedication to public service remained steadfast as she secured her fifth term by being re-elected to the 17th Lok Sabha in 2019.</span></p>'),(26999,'<p class=\"c28 title\" id=\"h.dew99zeca6du\"><span class=\"c41\">LOCAL POLITICS: YAVATMAL</span></p>'),(27001,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Formerly known as \"Yeoti\" or \"Yeotmal,\" the region of present-day Yavatmal district was historically part of the Berar region. It served as a key town of the Berar Sultanate and was once described in old records as \"the safest place in the world.\" Earlier referred to as the Wani region of Berar, it was renamed Yavatmal in 1905. After India’s independence, the Berar region, including Yavatmal, became part of the then-state of Madhya Pradesh in 1950. In 1956, the Marathi-speaking areas of Madhya Pradesh were merged into Bombay State. Following the Bombay Reorganisation Act of 1960, Bombay State was divided into Gujarat and Maharashtra, and Yavatmal became a part of Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(27004,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The Yavatmal district consists of the Yavatmal-Washim Lok Sabha constituency, which includes six Vidhan Sabha segments: Washim, Karanja, Ralegaon, Yavatmal, Digras, and Pusad. Washim and Karanja among the Vidhan Sabha segments are situated outside the Yavatmal district. Along with these, segments of Chandrapur and Hingoli Lok Sabha constituencies fall in this district, too.</span></p>\n<table class=\"c22\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Vidhan Sabha Constituencies</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Karanja</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Washim</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Washim</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Digras</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Pusad</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Ralegaon</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Arni</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Chandrapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Wani</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Chandrapur</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c1\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Umarkhed</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Hingoli</span></p></td><td class=\"c14\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Within the Vidhan Parishad, the Yavatmal District is represented by Yavatmal Local Bodies Authorities Constituency, the Amravati Teachers’ Constituency, and the Amravati Graduates Constituency. The Graduates and Teachers’ Constituencies are based on the administrative divisions of Maharashtra. This chapter focuses on only the Yavatmal Lok Sabha and the Vidhan Sabha segments that comprise it.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The given maps provide an overview of the boundaries of the Yavatmal district and the Vidhan Sabha and Lok Sabha Constituencies that fall within and around it.</span></p>'),(27008,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Till 2009, the district was represented by Yavatmal Lok Sabha constituency. In the latest delimitation, a new Lok Sabha constituency, Yavatmal-Washim, was constituted. The given chart provides an overview of the changes that have occurred in the composition of the Lok Sabha constituencies in the district and the reshuffling of the Vidhan Sabha constituencies with every delimitation that has been done.</span></p>'),(27011,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The following is the current Member of Parliament (MP) representing Yavatmal district in the Lok Sabha, as of 2024: </span></p>\n<table class=\"c22\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">MP</span></p></td><td class=\"c25\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Lok Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c25\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c34\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Sanjay Uttamrao Deshmukh</span></p></td><td class=\"c25\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal-Washim</span></p></td><td class=\"c25\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Shiv Sena - Uddhav Balasaheb Thackeray (SHS-UBT)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(27013,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The following are the current Members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) representing constituencies in Yavatmal district, as of 2024: </span></p>'),(27015,'<table class=\"c30\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">MLA</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Vidhan Sabha Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Party</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">District</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Shyam Khode</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Washim (SC)</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Washim</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Sai Prakash Dahake</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Karanja</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Washim</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Ashok Wooike</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Ralegaon ( ST )</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Anil Mangulkar</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">BJP</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Sanjay Rathod</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Digras</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Shiv Sena (SHS)</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c12\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Indranil Naik</span></p></td><td class=\"c6\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Pusad</span></p></td><td class=\"c31\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Nationalist Congress Party (NCP)</span></p></td><td class=\"c26\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(27017,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">There is no member from the Yavatmal district currently in the Rajya Sabha representing Maharashtra.</span></p>'),(27019,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Sanjay Rathod (SHS) is the current Guardian Minister for Yavatmal district, as of 2024.</span></p>'),(27021,'<p class=\"c5\"><span>The following table lists the current Members of the Maharashtra Vidhan Parishad representing Yavatmal district through various constituencies, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c22\"><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Vidhan Parishad Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Representative</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal Local Bodies Authorities Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Vacant</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">-</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Amravati Teachers’ Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Kiran Sarnaik</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">IND</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c10\"><td class=\"c19\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Amravati Graduates Constituency</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Dhiraj Lingade</span></p></td><td class=\"c13\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">INC</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(27023,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The following are the key administrative heads of Yavatmal district, as of 2024:</span></p>\n<table class=\"c22\"><tr class=\"c11\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Position</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Name</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c15\"><span class=\"c21\">Service / Party</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c11\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Collector / District Magistrate</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Dr. Pankaj Ashiya</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Indian Administrative Service (IAS)</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c11\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Chief Executive Officer, Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Mandar Patki</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">IAS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c11\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">President of Zilla Parishad</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Kalinda Pawar</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">SHS</span></p></td></tr><tr class=\"c11\"><td class=\"c3\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Superintendent of Police</span></p></td><td class=\"c8\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Pawan Bansod</span></p></td><td class=\"c17\" colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\"><p class=\"c0\"><span class=\"c2\">Indian Police Service (IPS)</span></p></td></tr></table>'),(27026,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">The following graphs provide an overview of the party distribution, turnout, number of candidates, and the winning margin of the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha constituencies of Yavatmal district.</span></p>'),(27028,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">There are 17 Urban Local Bodies in Yavatmal district: 1 Mahanagar Palika, 10 Nagar Parishad, 7 Nagar Panchayat. There are 1201 Gram Panchayats.</span></p>'),(27030,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Yavatmal district has ten municipal councils that function as urban local bodies across various towns. These include the Arni Nagar Parishad, Darwah Nagar Parishad, Digras Nagar Parishad, Ghatanji Nagar Parishad, Ner Nababpur Nagar Parishad, Pandharkawda Nagar Parishad, Pusad Nagar Parishad, Umerkhed Nagar Parishad, Wani Nagar Parishad, and Yavatmal Nagar Parishad.</span></p>'),(27032,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In addition to municipal councils, the district also has smaller urban bodies designated as town councils or Nagar Panchayats. These include Dhanki Nagar Panchayat, Kalamb Nagar Panchayat, Mahagaon Nagar Panchayat, and Babhulgao Nagar Panchayat.</span></p>'),(27035,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the 2024 Lok Sabha election, there were a total of 17 contestants from the Yavatmal-Washim seat. Out of these 17 candidates, there were two candidates that had criminal cases against them, with one of the contestants having serious cases registered. The contestant with the highest number of criminal cases registered was an Independent candidate. The elected MP, Sanjay Uttamrao Deshmukh, has no criminal cases against him.</span></p>'),(27037,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the 2019 Vidhan Sabha election, there were a total of 74 contestants from the six Vidhan Sabha seats that come under the Yavatmal-Washim Lok Sabha seat. Out of these, 14 contestants had criminal cases against them, with 35% of the contestants having serious cases registered.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Of the six elected MLAs, two of them have serious criminal cases registered against them. Rathod Sanjay Dulichand from Digras Vidhan Sabha constituency has the highest number of criminal cases. The only MLA without any registered cases is Lakhan Sahadeo Malik from Washim (SC) Vidhan Sabha Constituency.</span></p>'),(27040,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In September-November 2023, several districts in Marathwada experienced the impact of state-wide protests led by Jarange Patil, demanding reservation for the Maratha community. In the Dhanki area of Yavatmal\'s Umarkhed, activists organised \'rasta roko\' protests by blocking traffic. They tossed burning tyres on Dhanki-Umarkhed, Dhanki-Bittergaon, and Dhanki-Phulsawangi roads, triggering gridlocks. In the village of Arni town, activists observed a one-day fast, while in Sakur, they raised anti-government slogans.</span></p>'),(27043,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Madhav Shrihari Aney was a versatile figure in India\'s history, known for his roles as an educationalist, social reformer, and freedom fighter. He played a pivotal role in founding the Congress Nationalist Party, demonstrating his commitment to India\'s struggle for independence. He stands as a prominent disciple of Lokmanya Tilak, alongside other eminent figures like N C Kelkar, Kakasaheb Khadilkar, Gangadhar Deshpande, Dr. B S Munje, Abhyankar, T B Paranjpe, and Vaman Malhar Joshi. These individuals followed in the footsteps of Tilak, embodying his vision and principles.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Furthermore, he actively participated in the home rule movement, a significant chapter in India\'s fight for self-governance. His dedication to social reform, education, and the freedom struggle left an enduring mark on the nation\'s history.</span></p>'),(27045,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Jambuwantrao Dhote, affectionately known as the \"Lion of Vidarbha\" for his staunch support for statehood for the region, had a dynamic political journey. He began his political career as a devoted follower of Subhash Chandra Bose, starting with the Forward Bloc. Later, in the late 70s, he transitioned to Congress and eventually joined the SHS.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In 1962, he was first elected to the state assembly from Yavatmal, but his membership was temporarily suspended when he threw a paperweight at the assembly speaker due to his strong stance on a separate Vidarbha. However, he was re-elected in 1967. In 1972, he contested and won the Lok Sabha elections from Nagpur, defeating Congress veteran Rikhab Chandra Sharma. In 1978, he aligned himself with Indira Gandhi and made an electoral pact with the Congress in the state elections. His Forward Bloc secured 22 seats from Vidarbha, and four of his supporters were appointed as ministers. For the 1980 Lok Sabha elections, Dhote ran as a Congress candidate from Nagpur and won with a substantial margin. Subsequently, he parted ways with the Congress and founded the Vidarbha Janata Congress.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Dhote was not only a prominent political figure but also had strong family ties to politics. He was the son-in-law of the veteran Congress leader and former deputy chief minister, the late Ramrao Adik. His wife, Vijaya, also served as a Congress legislator from Yavatmal.</span></p>'),(27047,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Jawaharlal Darda was a social activist from the Yavatmal district. He was a remarkable figure in India\'s history, embodying the values of Gandhian leadership and the spirit of the freedom struggle. He actively participated in Mahatma Gandhi\'s Satyagrahas in 1942 and joined the Quit India Movement, demonstrating his unwavering commitment to the cause of India\'s independence. Besides his role in the freedom movement, he made significant contributions as a journalist and was one of the founding members of the Lokmat Media group, leaving a lasting imprint on the field of journalism.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the realm of politics, he served as a Member of the Legislative Assembly for an impressive four terms, representing the Yavatmal constituency from 1972 to 1996. Throughout his political career, he held various portfolios, including those of Minister of Health, Education, Energy, and Tourism, underscoring his multifaceted contributions to the state\'s governance.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In 1973, he had the honor of representing the Government of India at an International Conference on Housing held in Copenhagen, Denmark, highlighting his engagement in international affairs. His legacy is further commemorated by the issuance of a 100-rupee coin by the government to mark the birth centenary of Jawaharlal Darda, a testament to his significant contributions to the nation. Additionally, the Yavatmal Airport bears his name, serving as a lasting tribute to his impact on the region.</span></p>'),(27049,'<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">Mr. Sudhakarrao Naik was a Congress politician from Yavatmal. In 1962, he embarked on his political journey by assuming the role of Chairman of the Panchayat Samiti in Pusad. In 1976, he secured a seat in the Maharashtra Legislative Council as a member of the Congress party. Just two years later, he became a Member of the Legislative Assembly (MLA) and dutifully represented the Pusad constituency for a remarkable 17 years.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">During this extensive period of public service, he held various ministerial positions in the Maharashtra cabinet. His ministerial responsibilities included overseeing the housing, animal husbandry, and fisheries departments in 1978. He also served as the Minister for Industries, Revenue, and Social Welfare in 1985-86, followed by the role of Minister for Social Welfare, Energy, and Legislative Affairs from 1988 to 1990. In 1990-91, he assumed the important position of Minister for Revenue and Parliamentary Affairs.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">In the early 1990s, during a brief two-year period, when Sharad Pawar transitioned to the Union government as the Defense Minister in P.V. Narasimha Rao\'s cabinet, Sudhakarrao Naik took on the role of Maharashtra Chief Minister. During this short but eventful tenure, Naik faced the challenging task of managing riots that erupted in Bombay (now Mumbai) following the demolition of the Babri Masjid in Ayodhya. Notably, as Chief Minister, he took a strong stance against mafia figures associated with Dawood Ibrahim, Mumbai\'s most notorious gangster.</span></p>\n<p class=\"c5\"><span class=\"c2\">One of his significant actions was expelling Pappu Kalani from the Congress party and initiating an investigation against him for alleged terrorist activities. Pappu Kalani, a Congress MLA from Ulhasnagar with ties to Dawood Ibrahim, was subsequently incarcerated in 1992 under the stringent Terrorist and Disruptive Activities (Prevention) Act, commonly known as TADA.</span></p>');
