ense jungle inhabited by wildlife such as hyenas and jackals, underwent significant transformation over the centuries. Dr. John Fryer first referred to the area as Malabar Point in 1672, while John Burnell described the hills of Walkeshwar and Cumballa in 1710. By the end of the 18th century, Malabar Hill remained largely forested, with only a few areas inhabited, including a Parsi cemetery established in the late 17th century.','Malabar Hill, once a dense jungle inhabited by wildlife such as hyenas and jackals, underwent significant transformation over the centuries. Dr. John Fryer first referred to the area as Malabar Point in 1672, while John Burnell described the hills of Walkeshwar and Cumballa in 1710. By the end of the 18th century, Malabar Hill remained largely forested, with only a few areas inhabited, including a Parsi cemetery established in the late 17th century.','https://www.dnaindia.com/mumbai/report-malabar-hill-how-a-jungle-turned-into-a-posh-address-1569445'),(22766,'statistic/images/maharashtra/mumbai/environment/coastal-road-mumbai-by-night-5cc813ed.jpg','Coastal Road (Mumbai), by night','Coastal Road (Mumbai), by night','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Coastal_Road_%28Mumbai%29%2C_by_night.jpg/1280px-Coastal_Road_%28Mumbai%29%2C_by_night.jpg'),(22768,'statistic/images/maharashtra/mumbai/environment/coastal-road-mumbai-by-night-fb600a78.png','Coastal Road (Mumbai), by night','Coastal Road (Mumbai), by night','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Coastal_Road_%28Mumbai%29%2C_by_night.jpg/1280px-Coastal_Road_%28Mumbai%29%2C_by_night.jpg'),(22771,'statistic/images/maharashtra/mumbai/environment/coastal-road-mumbai-by-night-26eaa7e9.png','Coastal Road (Mumbai), by night','Coastal Road (Mumbai), by night','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Coastal_Road_%28Mumbai%29%2C_by_night.jpg/1280px-Coastal_Road_%28Mumbai%29%2C_by_night.jpg'),(22774,'statistic/images/maharashtra/mumbai/environ